    "label": "4B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named activity – Tourism in the Paris Basin:\nTourism is a tertiary activity and one of the main services in the Paris Basin, a core region of northern France centred on Paris.\nFactor 1 – Heritage and culture:\nThe Eiffel Tower was built for the 1889 Exposition Universelle and is a major attraction for sightseeing visitors.\nThe Louvre is the world's most visited art museum.\nThe Palace of Versailles is a major heritage attraction in the region.\nNotre-Dame Cathedral, damaged by fire in April 2019, reopened in December 2024.\nFactor 2 – Paris as a service centre:\nParis is the capital and primate city, with a large concentration of services, wealth and head offices, which brings business visitors as well as tourists.\nFactor 3 – Transport:\nThe road and rail network is radial, centred on Paris, giving easy access from all over France.\nThe first TGV high-speed line, Paris–Lyon, opened in 1981.\nThe Channel Tunnel opened in 1994, and Eurostar links Paris with London.\nCharles de Gaulle Airport, opened in 1974, is France's busiest airport, and Orly is the second Paris airport.\nThe RER regional express rail network moves visitors around the city region.\nFactor 4 – Attractions and events:\nDisneyland Paris at Marne-la-Vallée opened in 1992 and is Europe's most visited theme park, which is one specific tourist activity.\nParis hosted the 2024 Olympic and Paralympic Games, which brought large numbers of visitors.\nChampagne Hillsides, Houses and Cellars became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2015.\nThe Champagne houses in Reims and Épernay offer cellar tours, which is a second specific tourist activity.\nFactor 5 – Central location:\nThe basin has a central position in the EU, so visitors from neighbouring countries can reach it quickly.\nProblems:\nTourism brings congestion and seasonal pressure on sites, and large visitor numbers cause wear on heritage sites.\nSketch map points:\nOn a sketch map the River Seine is drawn flowing through Paris to Rouen and Le Havre at its mouth.\nDisneyland Paris at Marne-la-Vallée is marked east of Paris, Versailles to the west, and the Champagne towns of Reims and Épernay further east.",
