    "label": "6B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named region – India:\nIndia is a subcontinental region in South Asia, bounded by the Himalayas to the north, the Arabian Sea to the west and the Bay of Bengal to the east.\nA sketch map would show the Chota Nagpur Plateau and Jamshedpur in the north-east of the peninsula, Mumbai on the west coast, Pune inland from it on the Deccan and Bengaluru on the southern Deccan.\nFactor 1 – Raw materials:\nHeavy industries locate near the raw materials they use, because these are costly to move over long distances.\nThe Chota Nagpur Plateau holds most of India's coal and iron ore, with coal mined in the Jharia and Damodar valley coalfields in Jharkhand.\nIron and steel is the first example of secondary activity, and the Tata Iron and Steel Company, founded in 1907, built India's first large steel works at Jamshedpur on these resources.\nLimestone and manganese from the same plateau are also used in steel making.\nThis clustering made the Chota Nagpur and Damodar valley region around Jamshedpur a major industrial region.\nCotton grown on the black soils of Maharashtra and Gujarat supplied the cotton textile mills of Mumbai and Ahmedabad.\nCotton textiles is the second example of secondary activity, and Ahmedabad became known as the Manchester of India.\nJute grown in the Ganges delta supplied the jute mills along the Hooghly near Kolkata.\nFactor 2 – Government policy:\nGovernment policy has changed both the scale and the location of Indian industry.\nAfter 1947 industrialisation was state-led under the Five-Year Plans, and the state built steel plants at Bhilai, Rourkela and Durgapur.\nImports were protected from foreign competition by high tariffs, so home industry was sheltered while it developed.\nThe Licence Raj required permits to start or expand a factory, which made industry inefficient and slowed growth.\nA balance-of-payments crisis in 1991 led to reforms that removed most licences, cut tariffs and opened India to foreign investment.\nAfter 1991 manufacturing and export markets grew rapidly.\nSpecial Economic Zones, created by the Act of 2005, offer incentives that steer firms to chosen sites.\nMake in India was launched in 2014 and production-linked incentives from 2020 subsidise electronics, cars and pharmaceuticals, for example the Foxconn assembly of iPhones at Sriperumbudur near Chennai.",
