    "label": "15",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Fair trade contributes to sustainable development by giving small producers a fairer price and a voice, which supports the economic, social and environmental sides of development.\nAspect 1 – Economic sustainability:\nCommodity prices are low and unstable, small farmers receive a tiny share of the final price, and middlemen take a large share of the value of their crops.\nThe Fairtrade Minimum Price is a floor that covers the cost of sustainable production, so farmers can plan and invest with more security.\nWhen the market price is above the minimum, producers receive the market price plus the Fairtrade Premium.\nLong-term contracts and pre-finance give producers credit before the harvest.\nThe Oromia Coffee Farmers Co-operative Union in Ethiopia, founded in 1999 and led by Tadesse Meskela, sells members' coffee directly to buyers and so cuts out middlemen.\nDivine Chocolate, set up in Britain in 1998 and part-owned by the Ghanaian co-operative Kuapa Kokoo, gives farmers a share of profits as well as the Fairtrade price and premium.\nProducers learn processing and marketing skills, which helps them to keep more of the value of their crops.\nAspect 2 – Social sustainability:\nThe Fairtrade Premium is an extra sum paid to the co-operative and spent on projects that the members choose democratically.\nKuapa Kokoo, founded in Ghana in 1993, has spent its premium on wells for clean water, school buildings and mobile health clinics.\nThe Oromia union's premium has paid for schools, clinics and water.\nLabour standards ban forced and child labour, protect the freedom to join unions and require safe conditions.\nChild labour can fall when families earn a fairer income.\nWomen's participation in co-operatives gives women a voice and an income, and Kuapa Kokoo encourages women to take leadership roles in its village societies.\nDemocratic co-operatives empower producers to take decisions together.\nAspect 3 – Environmental sustainability:\nEnvironmental standards restrict pesticides, protect water and ban GM crops.\nFairtrade standards forbid clearing primary forest to plant crops, which protects habitats and biodiversity.\nOrganic farming and reduced clearing of land protect soil and biodiversity.\nOrganic Fairtrade crops earn an extra price on top of the minimum, which rewards farmers for avoiding chemical inputs.\nFairtrade coffee is often grown under shade trees, which shelter birds and other wildlife and protect the soil from heavy rain.\nA more stable income lessens the pressure on farmers to overuse their land in order to survive.\nThe Fairtrade Mark guarantees to consumers that these standards have been met.\nAspect 4 – Limits on its contribution:\nFair trade is only a tiny share of world trade.\nHigher prices put off some consumers, and certification costs exclude the poorest farmers.\nBenefits go mainly to co-operative members rather than hired labourers, and a 2014 study by SOAS of Ethiopia and Uganda found hired workers on some certified farms paid less than on comparable non-certified farms, though Fairtrade disputed parts of it.\nMarket prices sometimes exceed the minimum price, so the floor then gives no extra protection.\nFair trade does not change trade rules such as subsidies and tariff escalation, where roasted coffee faces higher tariffs than coffee beans.\nCadbury Dairy Milk carried the Fairtrade Mark from 2009 but switched in 2017 to Mondelēz's own Cocoa Life programme.\nIts overall impact depends on its scale and on wider changes in trade rules.",
