    "label": "4B(ii)",
    "marks": 27,
    "model": "Named region – The West of Ireland (Galway, Mayo, Roscommon):\nThe West has a dramatic Atlantic coastline and mountain scenery, such as Killary Harbour, Croagh Patrick, Achill Island and Clew Bay, which draws visitors to the coast and mountains.\nConnemara National Park at Letterfrack protects the Connemara landscape, so visitors can enjoy unspoilt scenery.\nThe Atlantic coast and beaches are used for surfing and water sports, which attract activity tourists.\nThe Wild Atlantic Way, a touring route of about 2,500 km launched by Fáilte Ireland in 2014, links the coastal attractions and encourages visitors to travel through the region.\nThe Great Western Greenway, about 42 km from Westport to Achill, opened in 2010 and supports walking and cycling, and angling is also popular.\nThe region has rich heritage, such as the Céide Fields in north Mayo, Stone Age field systems over 5,000 years old preserved under blanket bog, and Dún Aonghasa on Inis Mór.\nKylemore Abbey and Westport House add to the heritage attractions.\nGaeltacht culture and Irish summer colleges attract visitors who want to experience the Irish language.\nGalway city has festivals, nightlife and the Galway Races at Ballybrit, and the Galway International Arts Festival in July draws large crowds.\nThe M6 Dublin–Galway motorway, completed in 2009, rail lines from Galway, Westport and Ballina to Dublin, and Ireland West Airport Knock make the region easy to reach.\nThe North Atlantic Drift keeps winters mild, so frost and snow are rare at the coast, which makes it possible to visit outside the summer.",
