    "label": "5B",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Named region – The Paris Basin:\nThe Paris Basin is a large, low sedimentary lowland in northern France, centred on Paris, and Île-de-France is its core.\nActivity 1 – Why tourists are attracted:\nParis has world-famous heritage, such as the Eiffel Tower, built for the 1889 Exposition Universelle, which draws millions of visitors.\nThe Louvre is the world's most visited art museum, so culture tourists are drawn to the city.\nNotre-Dame Cathedral was damaged by fire in April 2019 and reopened in December 2024, which attracted renewed interest.\nThe Palace of Versailles is a major heritage attraction in the region.\nDisneyland Paris at Marne-la-Vallée opened in 1992 and is Europe's most visited theme park, so it attracts families.\nParis hosted the 2024 Olympic and Paralympic Games, which brought sport and event visitors.\nChampagne Hillsides, Houses and Cellars became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2015, and cellar tours at Reims and Épernay attract wine tourists.\nCharles de Gaulle Airport is France's busiest airport and Eurostar links Paris with London through the Channel Tunnel, so visitors can reach the region easily.\nActivity 2 – Development of transport:\nThe road and rail network is radial, centred on Paris, because Paris is the capital and the main market and centre of government.\nThe Boulevard Périphérique ring road, completed in 1973, carries traffic around the city and reduces congestion in the centre.\nThe first high-speed TGV line, Paris–Lyon, opened in 1981 and cut journey times.\nThe Channel Tunnel opened in 1994, and Eurostar links Paris with London, which connects the region to Britain.\nThe RER regional express rail network serves the city region, and the Grand Paris Express is about 200 km of new automatic metro lines with 68 new stations to relieve congestion.\nCharles de Gaulle Airport at Roissy opened in 1974, and Orly is the second Paris airport, so air links serve business and tourism.\nThe Port of Paris at Gennevilliers is one of Europe's largest river ports, and the Seine and Le Havre let bulk goods reach factories and markets cheaply.",
