    "model": "Location – Leixlip, Co. Kildare:\nIntel is located at Leixlip, Co. Kildare, on the M4 motorway west of Dublin, where it has operated since 1989.\nReasons for choosing the location:\nLeixlip is on the M4 motorway west of Dublin, which gives road access to the capital, its port and its airport.\nThe site was a large, flat greenfield site, which is undeveloped land with space for large fabs and later expansion.\nMaynooth University is nearby in Co. Kildare, and Trinity College Dublin and University College Dublin are in the city, which gives Intel graduates and research links.\nThe Dublin area has a large labour pool, so the company could recruit the workers it needed.\nFabs use huge amounts of water and electricity, and a supply of both was available at the site.\nIreland has a low corporation tax rate of 12.5 per cent, or 15 per cent for very large groups, which is a major attraction for multinationals.\nThe workforce is young, educated and English-speaking, which suits a company headquartered in California.\nIreland's membership of the EU gives access to the single market, so Intel can sell its chips in Europe without tariffs.\nIDA Ireland offers grants and support to companies that invest here, which reduced the cost of setting up.\nIreland has good international links, including ports, airports and broadband, and a cluster of similar firms and suppliers already in the country.",
