    "model": "Developing economy – India:\nIndia came under the rule of the East India Company after the Battle of Plassey in 1757, and under direct British rule from 1858 until independence on 15 August 1947.\nIndia's large handloom textile industry, including Bengal's fine cottons and Dhaka muslin, was destroyed by cheap machine-made cloth from the cotton mills of Lancashire.\nRaw cotton was exported from India to feed the mills of Britain, so India changed from a major manufacturer to a supplier of raw materials.\nCash crops such as cotton, jute, indigo, tea and opium replaced food crops, which made the food supply more vulnerable.\nThe Permanent Settlement of Bengal in 1793 collected heavy land taxes through landlords called zamindars, who passed the burden on to peasants, leaving them in debt and poverty.\nFamines occurred under British rule, and the Bengal famine of 1943 killed about 3 million people while grain was still being exported.\nRevenue, profits and salaries were sent from India to Britain in what Dadabhai Naoroji called the drain of wealth, which took capital out of the economy.\nRailways, such as the first line from Bombay to Thane in 1853, were built mainly to carry raw materials to the ports of Bombay and Calcutta, and they did not link regions of India to each other.\nPorts and colonial cities such as Bombay and Calcutta grew, so development was concentrated on the coast.\nIndia's share of world GDP fell from about a quarter around 1700 to about 4 per cent by 1950, according to estimates by the historian Angus Maddison.\nAt independence literacy was very low, at about 18 per cent in 1951, and life expectancy was around 32 years, because education had reached only a small elite.\nBritish rule left India with a civil service, courts, a parliamentary system and the English language, which has since become an asset for the IT industry.",
