    "marks": 24,
    "model": "Map evidence – Site at W 330 677:\nI would locate the wind farm on the high ground at Mountmusic and Mullaghroe at W 330 677, where a 200 m spot height is surrounded by contour lines.\nReasons for the location:\nThe site is high, exposed upland above about 200 m, where wind speeds are stronger and steadier than in the sheltered lowland of the River Lee valley, so the turbines generate more electricity.\nIreland's prevailing south-westerly winds are strong on exposed upland, which makes the site reliable for power generation.\nThe site is clear of the forestry at Kilnarovanagh around W 340 677, because trees cause turbulence and slow the wind.\nSettlement is sparse, with few houses shown on the upland, so few people are disturbed by noise, shadow flicker or the view of the turbines.\nMacroom, the nearest large town, is about 6 km to the north, so there is little risk of nuisance to a large population.\nLocal roads pass close to the site, for example around Ballina and Mullaghroe, so heavy turbine parts can be delivered and the cost of building access roads is reduced.\nThe land is rough farmland, so land costs are low and grazing can continue between the turbines.\nThe site is about 2 km south of The Gearagh at W 324 697, which is a protected wetland, and it is away from the reservoir and the River Lee, so wildlife and water are not threatened.\nThe site is several kilometres from the N22 national primary road, so the turbines will not distract drivers.",
