    "label": "10C(ii)",
    "marks": 27,
    "model": "Named city – Dublin:\nPopulation growth in Dublin, which is Ireland's primate city, created huge demand for more houses.\nRural-to-urban migration, drawn by jobs in industry and services concentrated in the capital, added to that demand.\nHouse prices in the inner city were very high, so people looked for cheaper housing at the edge of the city.\nFamilies wanted houses with gardens, which are found in low-density suburbs and not in the centre.\nDevelopers preferred cheaper greenfield sites on the edge of the city to costly sites inside it.\nPlanning authorities zoned large areas of greenfield land for housing.\nThe Wright Report of 1967 planned new towns at Tallaght, Clondalkin, Lucan and Blanchardstown, which pushed the city westward.\nRising car ownership and the M50 orbital motorway, built in stages from 1990, made distant sites easy to reach.\nTax incentives during the Celtic Tiger boom of the late 1990s and 2000s encouraged building on the edge of the city.\nInner-city tenements were cleared and decay set in, so many residents moved to the suburbs.\nSprawl has extended into the commuter counties of Meath, Kildare and Wicklow, with commuter towns such as Navan and Naas.",
