    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Reason 1 – Bridging point on a river:\nThe River Sullane flows in from the south-west and round the north of Macroom, and it is crossed by the N22 in the town at about W 337 731.\nA river was a barrier to travel, so a place where it could be crossed, first by ford and later by bridge, became a natural stopping point.\nTravellers and traders had to pass through the crossing, which encouraged inns, shops and houses to grow beside it.\nAs trade through the crossing increased, the settlement expanded into a market town serving the surrounding area.\nThe flat ground beside the river also gave space for the town to spread.\nReason 2 – Route focus:\nSeveral roads meet in the centre of Macroom, where the N22 and many local roads converge near W 339 729, and the R618 joins the N22 just east of the town at about W 354 725.\nRoads naturally focus on one point where the land is level and the river can be crossed, so Macroom became a nodal point.\nTrade and traffic travelling between the surrounding districts passed through the town, which brought customers for its shops and services.\nMarket days and fairs drew farmers from the surrounding countryside into the town along these roads.\nGood access to a wide area gave the town a large hinterland, which allowed it to grow in size and importance.\nReason 3 – Defence:\nMacroom Castle is named on the map beside the river at about W 339 729, right in the town centre.\nA castle on a dry point above the wet ground by the river could guard the river crossing and the routes meeting there.\nPeople settled around the castle for protection, so the town grew up under its shelter.\nA defended site attracted merchants and craftsmen, who felt safe to trade there.\nThe castle also made the town an administrative and market centre for the surrounding area.",
