    "label": "6B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named urban area – Mumbai, India:\nFactor 1 – Site and port function:\nMumbai grew from seven islands on the west coast of India that were joined by land reclamation.\nThe site has a deep, sheltered natural harbour on the Arabian Sea, which suited a trading port.\nThe British East India Company developed Mumbai as a port, and the Gateway of India marks this colonial past.\nJawaharlal Nehru Port in Navi Mumbai is one of India's main container ports, so port functions still drive growth.\nFactor 2 – Economic functions:\nCotton mills grew in the 19th century, drawing on the cotton of Gujarat and Maharashtra, and created many jobs.\nMumbai is India's financial capital, with the headquarters of the Reserve Bank of India and the Bombay Stock Exchange, founded in 1875.\nBollywood, the Hindi film industry, is based in Mumbai and attracts workers and investment.\nFactor 3 – Migration and natural increase:\nPush factors such as rural poverty, landlessness and crop failure drive migrants to the city.\nPull factors such as jobs, higher wages, schools and hospitals attract migrants to Mumbai.\nNatural increase adds to the population because many of the migrants are young adults who start families in the city.\nFactor 4 – Problems caused by rapid growth:\nDharavi, in central Mumbai, is one of Asia's largest slums, where migrants with little money settle.\nOvercrowding, high housing costs, congestion and overcrowded suburban trains are serious problems.\nThe flood of 26 July 2005, when about 944 mm of rain fell at Santacruz in one day, paralysed the city.\nNavi Mumbai, begun in the 1970s, is a planned twin city across the harbour that diverts growth from the overcrowded core.",
