    "label": "7B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named multinational company – Intel:\nFactor 1 – The product life cycle:\nProduct life cycle theory says a product passes through introduction, growth, maturity and decline, and its best location changes at each stage.\nEach Intel chip generation has a short life of a few years, so the company must keep developing new products.\nNew processes are developed at Hillsboro in Oregon, then copied exactly into fabs such as Leixlip under Intel's Copy Exactly policy.\nFab 34 at Leixlip, opened in 2023, makes the Intel 4 and Intel 3 generations, so Ireland keeps its place by winning each new generation.\nOlder fabs are upgraded or closed as products move into maturity and decline.\nIntel missed the start of the smartphone and artificial intelligence chip cycles, which let rivals such as Nvidia take the lead in AI chips.\nApple began replacing Intel chips in its Mac computers with its own M1 chip in November 2020, which cut demand for a mature Intel product.\nFalling profits led Intel to announce about 15,000 global job cuts in August 2024, an example of decline forcing rationalisation.\nFactor 2 – Developments in information technology:\nGlobal communication lets the headquarters at Santa Clara in California run fabs and assembly plants on several continents.\nComputer-aided design and automation are used in the fab, which raises precision and cuts costs.\nMoore's Law, stated by Gordon Moore in 1965, says the number of transistors on a chip doubles about every two years, so advances in IT drive the design and sales of the product.\nReal-time stock control and just-in-time supply reduce the stock held at each plant.\nWafers processed at Leixlip are flown to assembly and test plants in Penang, Kulim, Ho Chi Minh City and Chengdu, which IT links into one production chain.\nNew technology changes demand, as the shift from PCs to smartphones, cloud computing and artificial intelligence creates new markets and destroys older ones.\nData centres need server chips such as Xeon for cloud computing, a market created by developments in IT.\nDependence on Asian supply chains meant the 2021 chip shortage, caused by COVID-19 disruption, halted car and electronics production worldwide.",
