    "label": "9C(ii)",
    "marks": 14,
    "model": "I have chosen wind energy at G 705 440 on Kings Mountain.\nExposed site: the high open slope has no trees or buildings to block the wind, so the turbines get a strong, steady wind.\nAltitude: the land is over 400 m, where wind speeds are higher than at sea level, so more electricity is generated.\nAtlantic winds: the site is close to the west coast and faces the prevailing south-westerly winds, so it has reliable wind most of the year.\nLow population: few houses are built on the mountain, so noise and shadow flicker disturb very few people.\nCheap land: the steep upland is poor for farming, so land is cheap to buy or rent for the wind farm.\nNearby grid: the N15 and N16 roads and the town of Sligo are close, so the electricity can be sent quickly to a large market and the turbine parts can be brought in by road.\nSmall visual effect: the mountainside is away from the main settlements, so fewer people object to the look of the turbines.",
