    "label": "14",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Views of development have changed from growth-centred modernisation to dependency, human development and sustainability, and each view gives a different explanation of why some countries are underdeveloped.\nAspect 1 – Modernisation theory:\nModernisation theory holds that all societies pass along one path from traditional to modern, as Western Europe and North America did, so poor countries are simply behind and need investment, technology and industry to catch up.\nWalt Rostow set out five stages of growth in 1960, running from traditional society through preconditions for take-off, take-off and drive to maturity to the age of high mass consumption.\nAt take-off, investment rises to over 10% of national income and a few leading industries grow fast.\nSouth Korea seems to fit the model, since export-led industrialisation under Park Chung-hee and firms such as Samsung and Hyundai lifted it from poverty after the Korean War of 1950–53 to developed-country status by 2021.\nIreland can be read in the same way, with the First Programme for Economic Expansion in 1958, EEC membership in 1973 and the Celtic Tiger from the mid-1990s to 2007.\nThe model is criticised as Eurocentric and linear, because Zimbabwe and Syria show that countries can go backwards, and the Irish crash of 2008 reversed earlier gains.\nIt also ignores colonialism and unequal trade as causes of poverty and assumes that wealth will trickle down to the poor, which often does not happen.\nAspect 2 – Dependency theory:\nDependency theory argues that poor countries are poor because of how they were drawn into the world economy, not because of their own backwardness.\nRich core countries develop by draining wealth from poor periphery countries, so development and underdevelopment are two sides of one process.\nThe theory began in Latin America, where Raúl Prebisch and Hans Singer argued in 1950 that the terms of trade of primary-product exporters tend to decline, and Andre Gunder Frank wrote The Development of Underdevelopment in 1966.\nCôte d'Ivoire and Ghana grow over half of the world's cocoa, mostly exported as beans, while most of the value is added in rich countries.\nZambia depends on copper for most of its exports, which leaves it exposed to price changes.\nOther mechanisms include tariff escalation on processed goods, profit repatriation by multinationals, debt repayments and brain drain.\nCritics point out that the Asian Tigers and China developed through trade with the core, and that countries which cut their links with the world economy stayed poor.\nAspect 3 – Basic needs and human development:\nThe basic needs approach of the 1970s put food, water, shelter, health and education ahead of growth in GNP, and the ILO World Employment Conference of 1976 placed it at the centre of policy.\nE. F. Schumacher's Small Is Beautiful of 1973 argued for small-scale appropriate technology that local people can afford, use and repair.\nAmartya Sen argued in Development as Freedom in 1999 that development means widening people's choices and capabilities.\nThe first Human Development Report appeared in 1990, and the Human Development Index judges progress by people's lives and not by national output alone.\nBhutan put forward Gross National Happiness in the 1970s as a goal ahead of GNP.\nThe Washington Consensus of the 1980s and 1990s pushed privatisation, free trade and cuts in state spending from the top down, whereas bottom-up development puts participation, co-operatives and NGOs at the centre.\nRwanda rebuilt after the genocide of 1994 and women have held over 60% of the seats in its lower house of parliament since the 2013 election.\nAspect 4 – Sustainability and a changing view of the world:\nSustainable development, set out in the Brundtland Report of 1987, means meeting today's needs without harming the ability of future generations to meet theirs.\nThe 17 Sustainable Development Goals were adopted in 2015 with a target year of 2030, following the eight Millennium Development Goals of 2000–2015.\nEnvironmental determinism, argued by Ellen Churchill Semple and Ellsworth Huntington, once claimed that tropical climates produced poor and backward societies, and it was used to justify colonialism.\nThat view is now rejected because Singapore is tropical yet one of the richest countries, and Botswana became an upper-middle-income country through diamonds and stable government.\nThe North–South divide of the 1980 Brandt Report is outdated because many countries in the South, such as Singapore and South Korea, are high-income, and China has been the second-largest economy since 2010.\nEurocentric thinking, such as the Mercator projection that makes Africa look similar in size to Greenland although it is about 14 times larger, shaped how the world viewed development.\nMedia images of famine in Africa, such as the 1984–85 Ethiopian famine, fixed a view of the continent as helpless, which counter-images such as Kenya's M-Pesa mobile money service launched in 2007 now challenge.",
