    "label": "18",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "The tropical rainforest of the Amazon Basin has a hot, wet climate and infertile soils, and together these control the dense, layered and specially adapted vegetation of the biome.\nAspect 1 – Climate of the tropical rainforest:\nThe tropical rainforest lies mainly between about 10 degrees north and south of the equator, as in the Amazon Basin, the Congo Basin and South-East Asia.\nMean monthly temperatures are about 26 to 28 °C all year, with an annual range of only 2 to 3 °C.\nThe range is small because the sun stays high and day length barely changes.\nAnnual rainfall is over 2,000 mm, and Manaus in the central Amazon receives about 2,300 mm.\nRain falls throughout the year, and relative humidity is often over 80%.\nIntense morning heating makes moist air rise, and it cools and builds cumulonimbus clouds.\nHeavy afternoon convectional thunderstorms follow almost daily.\nThe ITCZ, the low-pressure belt of rising air along the equator, drives this rainfall.\nA large share of the Amazon's rain is water recycled from the forest by evapotranspiration.\nThe daily range of temperature is greater than the annual range, and there are no seasons in the temperate sense.\nAspect 2 – Soils of the tropical rainforest:\nThe soils are latosols, which are deep, often many metres, and red or yellow in colour.\nThey form by laterisation, in which heat and heavy rain leach out silica and bases and leave iron and aluminium oxides.\nThey are clay-rich and acidic, with very little humus.\nLitter decomposes within weeks in the heat and humidity, so little humus builds up.\nShallow roots and mycorrhizal fungi take up nutrients almost at once and pass them to the trees.\nMost nutrients are therefore held in the living vegetation rather than the soil, so the nutrient cycle is rapid and closed.\nThe soils are infertile despite the lush forest above them.\nWhen the forest is cleared, the soil loses fertility within a few years and erodes under heavy rain.\nCleared soil that dries out can bake into a hard laterite crust.\nShifting cultivation has traditionally used the soil for a few years before moving on.\nAspect 3 – Impact on the vegetation:\nConstant heat and heavy rain give year-round growth, so the forest is dense, evergreen and broadleaved.\nThe vegetation forms layers, with emergents such as kapok and the Brazil nut tree at 45 to 60 m, a canopy at 25 to 40 m, an understorey up to 15 m and a shrub layer below.\nOnly about 2% of the sunlight reaching the canopy arrives on the forest floor, so understorey plants have large leaves to capture light.\nShallow roots catch nutrients from the litter layer, and buttress roots support tall trees such as kapok.\nDrip tips and waxy leaves shed the heavy rain.\nTrees shed and replace leaves all year, and a fallen tree opens a light gap into which young trees grow rapidly.\nLianas climb trees to reach the light, and epiphytes such as orchids and bromeliads grow on branches and take moisture from the air and rain.\nPitcher plants in the nutrient-poor soils of South-East Asia trap and digest insects.\nHundreds of tree species can grow in a single hectare of Amazon rainforest, compared with a handful in an Irish wood.\nRainforests cover only about 6% of the earth's land surface but are widely estimated to hold over half of the world's land plant and animal species.",
