    "label": "21",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Boundaries, both physical and political, shape identity by uniting the people inside them, dividing cultural groups and creating minorities.\nAspect 1 – Physical boundaries:\nPhysical boundaries follow natural features such as mountains, rivers, lakes or seas.\nThe Pyrenees form much of the border between France and Spain, as fixed by the Treaty of the Pyrenees in 1659.\nThe Rhine forms part of the border between France and Germany, and the Rio Grande forms part of the border between the USA and Mexico.\nPhysical boundaries often fit cultural divides, so the people on each side develop a distinct national identity.\nThey can also cut through a cultural group, as the Basques live on both sides of the Pyrenees and share an identity across the border.\nThe sea around the island of Ireland makes it a clear physical unit, which supports a shared sense of island identity.\nAbout 60% of Switzerland lies in the Alps, and the mountains isolated valleys, which helped preserve local dialects and Romansh.\nBarriers limit contact between peoples, so isolated groups keep their own customs and traditions.\nThe Himalayas separate India from China and help give each side a distinct culture.\nAspect 2 – Political boundaries and partition:\nA political boundary is a line drawn by governments, not by nature, which defines the territory and citizens of a state.\nThe Government of Ireland Act 1920 created two administrations on the island, and Northern Ireland was established in 1921.\nThe border is about 499 km long and follows old county boundaries rather than physical features.\nIt cuts through communities, farms and the Erne and Foyle catchments.\nPartition created two states, two majorities and two minorities, with most unionists feeling British and most nationalists feeling Irish.\nIdentity is expressed through symbols, as Orange Order parades on 12 July, GAA clubs and painted murals and kerbstones mark out unionist and nationalist areas.\nThe Troubles from the late 1960s to 1998 killed over 3,500 people, which shows how deeply identity can divide.\nUnder the Good Friday Agreement of 1998 people in Northern Ireland may be Irish, British or both, which recognises layered identity.\nThe UK left the EU on 31 January 2020 after Northern Ireland voted about 56% Remain in 2016, and the Windsor Framework of 2023 keeps the land border free of checks.\nAspect 3 – Imposed, hardened and softened boundaries:\nSuperimposed boundaries are imposed by outside powers regardless of the cultures they divide.\nThe Berlin Conference of 1884–85 set the rules for the European division of Africa, and many African borders are straight lines that ignore ethnic territories.\nThese borders split some groups and joined rival ones, which caused later conflict and secession.\nThe Treaty of Lausanne in 1923 drew the borders of modern Turkey with no Kurdish state, so the Kurds are divided between Turkey, Iran, Iraq and Syria.\nTurkey banned Kurdish in public life until the early 1990s, yet the Kurds keep their identity through their language and Newroz on 21 March.\nBoundaries can harden into barriers, as the Berlin Wall stood from 1961 to 1989 and the Cyprus Green Line has divided Nicosia since 1974.\nBoundaries can also soften, as the Schengen Agreement of 1985 removed passport checks between most EU states.\nOpen borders encourage a European identity to grow alongside national identities.\nChanging boundaries create new identities and new minorities, as the break-up of Yugoslavia produced new nation states in the 1990s.",
