    "label": "22",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Mid-latitude depressions, anticyclones and land and sea breezes each form in a different way, and each has a clear impact on weather patterns.\nAspect 1 – Mid-latitude depressions:\nA depression is a low-pressure system about 1,000 to 3,000 km across, with closed isobars and winds blowing anticlockwise inwards in the northern hemisphere.\nIt forms at the polar front, where cold polar maritime air from the north meets warm, moist tropical maritime air from the south-west.\nA wave or kink develops on the front, often where the jet stream above removes air aloft.\nWarm air rises over the cold air, surface pressure falls and air is drawn in and turned anticlockwise by the Coriolis force.\nA warm front forms ahead of the warm air and a cold front forms behind it, with the warm sector between them.\nThe cold front moves faster and catches up, lifting the warm sector to form an occluded front, and the depression then fills and dies after a few days to a week.\nThe warm front brings thickening cloud and a long spell of steady rain, while the cold front brings short heavy showers, gusts and a sharp fall in temperature.\nBecause depressions cross Ireland regularly, the weather is changeable with rain in every month, and the west is wetter than the east.\nThe strongest storms come in autumn and winter, as with ex-hurricane Ophelia on 16 October 2017 and Storm Éowyn on 24 January 2025, and flooding follows when depressions arrive in quick succession.\nAspect 2 – Anticyclones:\nAn anticyclone is an area of high pressure with widely spaced closed isobars, light winds blowing clockwise outwards in the northern hemisphere and no fronts.\nIt is large and slow-moving and can last for days or weeks.\nAir converging high in the atmosphere sinks in a process called subsidence, and as it is compressed it warms, so its relative humidity falls and cloud evaporates.\nDynamic highs form from sinking air in the general circulation, as the Azores High does when it extends a ridge over Ireland in summer.\nThermal highs form when the ground cools intensely in winter, as the Siberian High does over Central Asia.\nIn summer an anticyclone brings clear skies, strong sunshine, high daytime temperatures and light winds, and a prolonged spell causes heatwaves and drought.\nIn winter clear nights bring hard frost and freezing fog in valleys, or anticyclonic gloom when cloud is trapped under a temperature inversion.\nA blocking anticyclone diverts depressions north or south, so one region has a long dry spell while others get prolonged rain.\nIn late February and early March 2018 a block over Scandinavia drew polar continental air from Russia over Ireland in the Beast from the East, and Storm Emma then brought heavy snow on 1 and 2 March.\nAspect 3 – Land and sea breezes:\nLand and sea breezes are local coastal winds caused by the uneven heating of land and sea.\nBy day the land heats faster than the sea under sunshine.\nThe air over the land warms, expands and rises, which creates lower pressure over the land while the cooler sea has relatively higher pressure.\nCool air flows onshore as a sea breeze, and the air that rose returns seaward at height and sinks over the sea to complete a circulation cell.\nAfter sunset the land loses heat faster than the sea, so the air over the land cools and becomes denser, which creates higher pressure over the land.\nAir then flows offshore as a land breeze, which is weaker than the sea breeze because the night-time temperature difference is smaller.\nSea breezes lower coastal temperatures in the afternoon and raise humidity.\nA sea-breeze front can trigger a line of cumulus cloud or showers inland, and sea breezes can carry sea fog onshore.\nLand breezes help clear coastal fog, and sea breezes are strongest on hot, calm summer afternoons, often under an anticyclone.",
