    "label": "23",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "The cool temperate oceanic climate of north-west Europe, with Ireland as the main example, is distinctive, and it is examined here through its location, temperature and precipitation.\nAspect 1 – Location:\nThis climate lies on the west coasts of continents in the mid-latitudes, in the path of the westerly winds.\nIn Europe it includes Ireland, Britain, north-west France, Belgium, the Netherlands, Denmark and coastal Norway.\nOther west coasts at the same latitude in both hemispheres share it, such as British Columbia in Canada and southern Chile.\nPrevailing south-westerly winds blow from the subtropical high towards the subpolar low and bring maritime air off the Atlantic.\nThe region lies near the polar front, where depressions form and are steered across the area by the jet stream.\nThe Icelandic Low and the Azores High dominate the weather of Ireland.\nThe North Atlantic Drift, the extension of the Gulf Stream, warms the coast.\nTropical maritime and polar maritime air masses are the most common.\nEastwards the oceanic influence weakens, and the climate grades into the continental climate of central and eastern Europe.\nAspect 2 – Temperature:\nWinters are mild and summers are cool, so the annual range is small.\nIreland's mean January temperature is about 4 to 7 °C and its mean July temperature is about 14 to 16 °C.\nThe annual range is about 10 °C in Ireland, compared with over 60 °C at Verkhoyansk in the continental interior of Russia.\nThe sea heats and cools slowly, which moderates the seasons.\nThe North Atlantic Drift warms the winter air and seas.\nSouth-westerly winds carry mild maritime air onto the land.\nFrost and snow are infrequent on low ground.\nSummers stay cool because the sun is lower in the sky at mid-latitudes than in the tropics.\nCold spells occur when a block of high pressure draws polar continental air from Russia, as in the Beast from the East in 2018.\nAspect 3 – Precipitation:\nRain falls in all months, with a maximum in autumn and winter.\nThere are about 150 wet days a year in the east and south-east of Ireland and up to about 225 in parts of the west.\nAnnual rainfall is about 750 mm around Dublin, about 1,000 to 1,250 mm across much of the west and over 2,000 mm on many western mountains.\nMost rain is frontal, because depressions cross the region regularly along the polar front.\nRelief rain falls on west-facing mountains, where moist air is forced to rise, cool and condense.\nAmounts fall from west to east because depressions and moist Atlantic air arrive in the west first.\nThe warm, moist air that comes from the Atlantic carries a large amount of water vapour.\nThe weather is changeable as depressions and ridges of high pressure alternate.\nOccasional anticyclones bring dry spells, most often in summer.",
