    "label": "4C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named urban area – Paris, France:\nParis is the capital and primate city of France and lies near the centre of the Paris Basin.\nFactor 1 – Site and situation:\nParis began on the Île de la Cité, an island in the Seine that gave a defensive site and a crossing point of the river.\nThe Seine and its tributaries the Marne and Oise gave Paris water and river transport.\nFactor 2 – Transport:\nParis is the hub of a radial road and rail network, so traffic from all over France focuses on the city.\nCharles de Gaulle and Orly airports and the river port at Gennevilliers add air and water links.\nFactor 3 – Administration and services:\nAs capital of a centralised state, Paris concentrates government, finance, head offices and universities.\nLa Défense, west of the city, is one of Europe's largest purpose-built business districts and draws international firms.\nFactor 4 – Farming hinterland, industry and migration:\nThe fertile limon plateaux around the city supplied food to a growing population.\nThe large market and labour force attracted manufacturing and services.\nRural-to-urban migration brought people to Paris as jobs concentrated there.\nFactor 5 – Planning and size today:\nHaussmann rebuilt central Paris between 1853 and 1870, and the grands ensembles and the five new towns of the 1965 plan housed the growing population.\nÎle-de-France now has about 12 million people, about 18% of the population of France.",
