    "label": "6B(ii)",
    "marks": 36,
    "model": "Named region – The Paris Basin, France:\nThe Paris Basin in northern France is a core European region centred on Paris.\nFactor 1 – Climate:\nThe basin has a transitional climate, between cool temperate oceanic conditions in the west and more continental conditions in the east.\nParis has an average January temperature of about 5°C and an average July temperature of about 20°C, so summers are warm enough to ripen cereals and grapes.\nAnnual rainfall in Paris is about 600 to 650 mm, spread fairly evenly through the year, which gives crops enough moisture without waterlogging the fields.\nThe long growing season gives crops such as wheat, barley and maize more time to develop and produce high yields.\nChampagne lies near the northern limit of commercial vine growing, so its warm summers are essential to the grape crop.\nDry harvest weather lets crops be gathered in good condition, which helps the cereal farms of the Beauce, known as the granary of France.\nWetter oceanic conditions and clay soils in the west, in Normandy, suit grassland and dairy farming, which produces Camembert cheese.\nFactor 2 – Relief and soils:\nThe relief is low and gently rolling, mostly below about 300 m, so large fields can be worked with machinery.\nLimon, a fine wind-blown loam, covers the Beauce, Brie and Picardy plateaux.\nLimon is deep, well-drained and easily worked, which makes it among the most fertile soils in Europe and gives high yields.\nThe Beauce, south-west of Paris around Chartres, is the granary of France because large mechanised farms of over 100 hectares grow wheat, barley and maize on its flat limon land.\nThin chalk soils on the slopes of the escarpment around Reims and Épernay suit vines, and only sparkling wine from this protected area may be called Champagne.\nFertilisers and modern farming turned the dry chalk plain east of Reims, once called poor Champagne, into productive cereal land.\nHeavier, wetter clay soils in the vales and in the west suit grass rather than crops, so they support dairy farming in Normandy and Brie.",
