    "model": "Developed economy – Ireland and footloose industry:\nFootloose industries such as computer chips, pharmaceuticals and software are not tied to a raw material or a market, so they were free to choose Ireland from many possible countries.\nLow corporation tax of 12.5%, rising to 15% for very large multinational groups from 2024, attracts multinational companies to Ireland.\nIDA Ireland, set up in 1949, attracts firms with grants, serviced sites and advance factories.\nMembership of the EU since 1973 gives firms in Ireland tariff-free access to the single market from an English-speaking base.\nA young, educated, English-speaking workforce is supplied by the universities, for example Maynooth University beside the Intel plant at Leixlip.\nLight, high-value products such as chips and medicines are cheap to move by air, so distance from the market matters little.\nGood infrastructure helped Intel, which opened at Leixlip in 1989 on a large greenfield site beside the M4 motorway and close to Dublin Airport.\nClusters such as pharmaceuticals in Cork Harbour and medical technology in Galway draw in more firms because they share skills, suppliers and research.",
