    "label": "7C(ii)",
    "marks": 27,
    "model": "Conflict – Corrib gas, Co. Mayo:\nThe Corrib gas field lies about 80 km off north-west Co. Mayo and was discovered in 1996.\nThe economic interest was strong, because the project promised energy security, a domestic gas supply, revenue for the State and the companies, and construction jobs.\nWhen gas first flowed in December 2015 it supplied over half of Ireland's gas for a time.\nThe original plan piped high-pressure, unprocessed gas onshore near homes at Rossport, which created a safety risk for local residents.\nThe Shell to Sea campaign, from 2005, argued that the gas should be processed at sea rather than onshore.\nThe pipeline route crossed Sruwaddacon Bay, a protected estuary and Special Area of Conservation, so environmental groups feared damage to the habitat.\nThe Rossport Five were jailed in June 2005 for refusing to obey a court order not to obstruct pipeline work, and they were released after 94 days.\nProtests were policed heavily for years and the dispute divided the local community.\nThe conflict arose because the economic benefits to the State and the company were set against the safety and environmental concerns of local people.\nThe onshore route was changed to keep the pipeline further from houses, and a tunnel was bored under Sruwaddacon Bay to carry it.\nAlmost twenty years passed between discovery in 1996 and first gas in 2015, which shows how long a conflict can delay a project.",
