    "label": "5B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Region – India:\nAgriculture employs the largest share of India's workers, about 55% in Census 2011, so its development is vital to the subcontinent.\nFactor 1 – Climate and the monsoon:\nTropical temperatures allow two or three crops a year.\nThe south-west monsoon brings seasonal rain from June, which supports kharif crops such as rice, cotton and maize.\nRabi crops such as wheat are sown in October and November and harvested in March and April, and they depend on irrigation and winter rain from western disturbances.\nThe monsoon is unreliable, bringing drought in some years and floods in others, which limits farming in some areas.\nFactor 2 – Soils and relief:\nFertile alluvium deposited by Himalayan rivers covers the Northern Plains, making them the agricultural heartland.\nThe flat land of the plains eases cultivation and transport.\nBlack soils weathered from the Deccan basalt hold moisture and are ideal for cotton in Maharashtra and Gujarat.\nLaterite soils on the Western Ghats and in Kerala are infertile but support tea, coffee and cashew when fertilised.\nSteep slopes limit cultivation, but terraces on Himalayan slopes grow tea and fruit.\nFactor 3 – Rivers and irrigation:\nPerennial Himalayan rivers, fed by snow and glacier melt as well as monsoon rain, supply water for irrigation.\nCanals such as the Indira Gandhi Canal bring water into the Thar Desert, and tubewells draw groundwater.\nOveruse of groundwater has lowered the water table in Punjab, and irrigation has caused salinisation and waterlogging.\nFactor 4 – Technology and government policy:\nThe Green Revolution from the mid-1960s brought high-yielding varieties of wheat and rice, fertilisers, pesticides, irrigation and machinery.\nFood-grain output rose from about 51 million tonnes in 1950–51 to about 330 million tonnes in 2022–23, and India became self-sufficient in food.\nThe government sets minimum support prices, buys grain stored by the Food Corporation of India, and has paid PM-KISAN support to farm families since 2019.\nOperation Flood from 1970 built co-operative dairying, such as the Amul co-operative at Anand in Gujarat, and made India the world's largest milk producer.\nA huge domestic market of over 1.4 billion people drives demand for farm produce.\nAverage holdings are only about 1.1 hectares and about 86% are small or marginal, which limits productivity.\nNamed examples – Punjab wheat and Assam tea:\nPunjab is a core Green Revolution area where canal irrigation supports commercial irrigated wheat farming.\nAssam in the Brahmaputra valley has plantation farming of tea.",
