    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Region studied – India:\nIndia is a subcontinental region in South Asia, bounded by the Himalayas to the north, the Arabian Sea to the west and the Bay of Bengal to the east.\nFactor 1 – Raw materials:\nThe Chota Nagpur Plateau in Jharkhand holds most of India's coal and iron ore, which are the basis of the steel industry.\nCoal is mined in the Jharia coalfield and the Damodar valley coalfields, so steel makers have fuel close to their plants.\nIron ore, limestone and manganese from the same plateau supply the other inputs needed to make steel.\nBecause these raw materials are heavy and lose weight in processing, siting plants close to them cuts transport costs.\nThe Tata Iron and Steel Company was founded in 1907 at Jamshedpur on this coal and iron ore, making it India's first large steel works.\nThe state steel plants at Bhilai, Rourkela and Durgapur were later built to use the same mineral resources.\nCotton grown on the black soils of the Deccan supplied the cotton textile mills of Mumbai and Ahmedabad.\nJute grown in the Ganges delta supplied the jute mills along the Hooghly near Kolkata, so raw materials shaped the location of several industries.\nFactor 2 – Labour:\nIndia has a huge, young and low-cost labour force, which keeps production costs low for manufacturers.\nLow wages make Indian goods competitive on export markets and attract foreign firms.\nGraduates from the Indian Institutes of Technology, the first of which opened at Kharagpur in 1951, give a large pool of skilled engineers.\nThis skilled labour supports high-technology industries such as aerospace and defence in Bengaluru, where Hindustan Aeronautics Limited and the headquarters of ISRO are based.\nFoxconn assembles Apple iPhones at Sriperumbudur near Chennai, using this large workforce for electronics assembly.\nCheap labour supports labour-intensive garment making, as in the knitwear exports of Tiruppur in Tamil Nadu.\nMaruti Suzuki, a joint venture with Suzuki of Japan, makes cars at Gurugram and Manesar, drawing on the large labour pool near Delhi.\nMost workers are in the informal sector, which keeps costs low but limits wages and working conditions.",
