    "model": "EU policy – Common Agricultural Policy (CAP):\nThe Common Agricultural Policy was launched in 1962 to raise farm productivity, give farmers a fair standard of living, stabilise markets and secure food supplies.\nInfluence 1 – Higher farm incomes and modernisation:\nWhen Ireland joined on 1 January 1973, guaranteed prices above world levels raised Irish farm prices and incomes sharply.\nIntervention buying opened a guaranteed market for Irish beef and dairy produce.\nFarms were modernised with machinery, fertiliser, silage, land drainage and farm buildings, remembered as the golden age of the 1970s.\nIreland was a net recipient of EU money until about 2014, receiving over €40 billion more than it paid in, mostly through the CAP.\nThe MacSharry reform of 1992 replaced price cuts with direct payments, and the Single Farm Payment from 2005 decoupled payments from production.\nThese payments gave farmers stable incomes, and the Basic Payment Scheme replaced the Single Farm Payment from 2015.\nInfluence 2 – Limits and costs for Irish farming:\nMilk quotas, introduced in 1984, capped each farm's output and held back Irish dairying for 30 years.\nWhen quotas ended on 1 April 2015, Irish milk output rose from about 5.5 billion litres in 2014 to about 8.3 billion litres in 2020.\nIntensification damaged water quality through nitrates and raised greenhouse gas emissions as the dairy herd grew.\nThe Nitrates Directive of 1991 limits the nitrogen spread per hectare, so EU rules directly shape Irish farming.\nPayments have historically favoured bigger farmers, and many beef and sheep farms depend heavily on direct payments.\nThe number of farms has fallen and farmers are ageing, with the Census of Agriculture 2020 counting 135,037 farms and a third of holders aged 65 or over.\nInfluence 3 – Rural development and the environment:\nLEADER, from 1991, funds local rural enterprise such as farm diversification, rural tourism and crafts.\nThe Rural Environment Protection Scheme ran from 1994 to 2009 and paid farmers for environmentally friendly farming.\nIreland's CAP Strategic Plan for 2023 to 2027 is worth about €9.8 billion, with about 45% for environmental and climate measures.",
