    "label": "8C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Renewable energy source – Wind energy:\nWind turns turbine blades that drive a generator, with onshore farms such as Galway Wind Park in Connemara and offshore farms such as Arklow Bank off Co. Wicklow.\nEconomic advantages:\nWind supplied about a third of Irish electricity in the early 2020s, so it is a main home-grown source of power.\nOnce the turbines are built the fuel is free, so running costs are low.\nIreland imported about three-quarters of its energy in the early 2020s, at a cost of billions of euro a year, and wind reduces this import bill.\nLess dependence on imports improves energy security and shields Ireland from fossil fuel price shocks.\nWind farms create jobs in construction, maintenance and manufacture, often in rural areas.\nFarmers and landowners earn rental income from turbines on their land, which supports rural communities.\nMeeting EU and national 2030 renewable targets avoids the cost of buying compliance.\nGreen electricity attracts multinational companies that want renewable power.\nEnvironmental advantages:\nWind produces no carbon dioxide when generating electricity, so it reduces the enhanced greenhouse effect and global warming.\nUnlike coal and oil, it releases no sulphur dioxide or nitrogen oxides, so it does not add to acid rain.\nWind is inexhaustible, so it does not deplete finite fossil fuel resources.\nReplacing coal and peat in power generation avoids the landscape damage of mining and peat cutting.\nWind farms need no cooling water, so unlike thermal power stations they cause no thermal pollution of rivers.\nLand around onshore turbines can still be used for grazing, so the footprint on farmland is small.\nOffshore sites such as Arklow Bank use the sea and take up no farmland.\nDenmark gets about half of its electricity from wind, showing that large cuts in emissions are possible.",
