    "label": "12C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named example – Kolkata, India:\nKolkata lies on the low-lying delta land beside the Hooghly river and had an urban population of about 14.1 million at Census 2011.\nIt grew rapidly as a colonial port and capital, with migrants arriving from rural Bengal, Bihar and Odisha, and refugees after Partition in 1947.\nProblem 1 – Slums and informal settlements:\nMigrants with little money and no legal land settle in shanty towns, called bustees in Kolkata, built on marginal land such as river banks and beside railways.\nTenure is insecure, so residents can be evicted and have no legal right to the land they occupy.\nOvercrowding is severe, and pavement dwellers in Kolkata have no shelter at all.\nHousing cannot be built as fast as migration driven by rural poverty and by the pull of jobs and natural increase add people.\nProblem 2 – Water, sanitation and health:\nWithout piped water or sewers, settlements depend on open drains and shared toilets.\nContaminated water and waste spread water-borne diseases such as cholera, typhoid and dysentery.\nIn Kolkata water supply and sewerage are poor, so many residents lack safe water and proper sanitation.\nMonsoon rain floods the low-lying streets and slums, which worsens the risk to health.\nProblem 3 – Traffic and pollution:\nRoad networks cannot cope with the growth in traffic, so congestion becomes permanent and journeys take longer.\nTrains and buses are overcrowded at peak times.\nAir pollution comes from traffic, industry and cooking fuels burned in crowded homes.\nUntreated sewage and industrial waste are discharged into rivers, and rubbish builds up where it is not collected.\nProblem 4 – Unemployment and social problems:\nJobs did not keep pace with the number of migrants, so many people depend on low-paid informal work such as street vending, waste picking and rickshaw pulling, which have no job security.\nIn Kolkata hand-pulled rickshaws still operate, and underemployment and child labour are common.\nWeak local government struggles to provide services, and inequality and crime are visible where rich and poor districts sit side by side.",
