    "label": "14",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "This answer examines how aid programmes can help and hinder development, by looking at government and EU aid, NGO aid and the ways in which aid can harm recipients.\nAspect 1 – Government and EU aid that helps:\nBilateral aid is given directly from one government to another, while multilateral aid is pooled through bodies such as the EU and UN agencies.\nEmergency aid saves lives after famine, floods or conflict by providing food, shelter and medical help.\nLong-term development aid funds schools, clinics, wells and vaccination programmes, which improve health and education.\nInfrastructure such as roads and power supplies helps local economies to grow.\nIrish Aid began its bilateral programme in 1974, with partner countries including Ethiopia, Malawi, Mozambique, Tanzania, Uganda, Zambia and Vietnam.\nIrish aid is untied, so recipients can spend it anywhere to get the best value.\nThe EU institutions and member states together, known as Team Europe, are the world's largest aid donor, giving over two-fifths of all official development aid.\nECHO, the EU's humanitarian aid office, was set up in 1992 to deliver emergency relief.\nUganda in 1998 became the first country to receive HIPC debt relief, and its savings went to schools, health and water.\nAspect 2 – NGO aid that helps:\nNGOs are non-profit organisations that are independent of governments and are funded by donations, governments and the EU.\nThey provide emergency relief and long-term development in water, health, education and farming.\nConcern Worldwide was founded in 1968 in response to famine in Biafra during Nigeria's civil war.\nTrócaire was founded in 1973 by the Irish Catholic bishops, and its Lenten Trócaire box funds its work.\nBóthar gives farm animals to poor families, who pass on the gift of the first female offspring to another family.\nSelf Help Africa was founded in 1984 in response to the Ethiopian famine and supports smallholder farmers.\nNGOs work close to communities, from the bottom up, at small scale and with appropriate methods.\nNGOs are fast in emergencies and reach places that governments cannot.\nMédecins Sans Frontières, founded in France in 1971, won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1999 for its international work.\nAspect 3 – How aid can hinder development:\nTied aid must be spent on goods and services from the donor country, so much of the money returns to donor firms.\nUK aid for the Pergau Dam in Malaysia was linked to a British arms deal, and in 1994 the English High Court ruled that the aid was unlawful because it was not for sound development.\nUS food aid has long been required by law to be bought largely from US farmers and shipped largely on US ships.\nAid has been used to win political allies, trade deals and influence, as in Cold War aid.\nCorruption and elite capture can divert aid money to powerful groups rather than the poor.\nRecipients can come to rely on aid rather than building their own economies, and the Zambian economist Dambisa Moyo argued in Dead Aid in 2009 that aid breeds corruption and dependency in Africa.\nIn the mid-1990s Haiti cut its tariff on rice under pressure from lenders, and cheap subsidised US rice flooded in and ruined many Haitian rice farmers.\nIn 2010 former US President Bill Clinton publicly apologised for the rice policy, saying it had been a mistake.\nAid given as loans must be repaid, so it adds to the debt burden of poor countries.",
