    "label": "16",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Human use of land changes soil characteristics such as humus content, structure, fertility and depth, either by degrading the soil through overgrazing and desertification or by improving it through conservation.\nAspect 1 – Overgrazing in the west of Ireland:\nOvergrazing means keeping more animals on land than its vegetation can support.\nFrom the 1980s EU headage payments paid farmers per animal, so sheep numbers rose sharply on hill commonage in Connemara and the mountains of Mayo, Donegal and Kerry.\nGrazing stripped the heather and grass, so roots died and less litter was returned, which lowered the humus content of the soil.\nFalling humus weakened the crumb structure and reduced the soil's ability to hold water and nutrients.\nTrampling by livestock compacted the soil, which is called poaching on wet Irish ground, and this reduced infiltration and increased runoff.\nBare soil was removed by rain-splash, sheet wash, rills and gullies, so the topsoil became thinner and less fertile.\nOn the hills exposed blanket peat eroded and silt washed into rivers, damaging salmon and trout spawning grounds.\nIn Iceland sheep grazing prevented birch regrowth, and the light volcanic andosols were blown and washed away, leaving erosion escarpments.\nStocking rates must stay within the carrying capacity of the land if the soil's cover, structure and depth are to be kept.\nAspect 2 – Desertification in the Sahel:\nDesertification is land degradation in arid, semi-arid and dry sub-humid areas, resulting from climatic variations and human activities.\nThe Sahel is a semi-arid belt along the southern edge of the Sahara, from Senegal and Mauritania to Sudan, with highly unreliable rainfall of about 200 to 600 mm a year.\nPopulation growth concentrated cattle, goats and sheep around wells, shortened fallow periods, spread cash crops such as groundnuts and cotton and increased fuelwood cutting.\nDroughts in the 1970s and 1980s left the soil bare, so wind and the heavy rain of the short wet season removed the topsoil and humus.\nLoss of topsoil and humus lowers fertility, so crop yields fall.\nThe bare surface forms a crust, so rain runs off instead of soaking in and water retention is reduced.\nWind-blown sand and dust remove the fine particles from the soil.\nPoorly managed irrigation causes salinisation, which leaves salt crusts and can make the ground bare and sterile, as on the canal-irrigated land of Punjab and Haryana in India.\nHuman pressure and drought act together, because overuse leaves the soil bare and drought and storms then remove it.\nAspect 3 – Conservation methods:\nConservation methods protect soil from erosion and rebuild its fertility and structure.\nContour ploughing across a slope makes the furrows trap water instead of channelling it downhill, which reduces runoff and soil loss.\nTerracing cuts steep slopes into level steps, as in the rice terraces of Banaue in the Philippines, which keeps soil in place and holds water.\nShelter belts of trees and hedges cut wind speed and reduce wind erosion, and they were promoted after the Dust Bowl of the 1930s in the Great Plains of the USA.\nCrop rotation, fallow and green manure such as clover restore nutrients and humus, which improves crumb structure and fertility.\nReduced tillage and mulching leave crop residue on the surface to protect the soil.\nIn Burkina Faso farmers revived zaï pits filled with manure or compost, which catch rainwater and concentrate nutrients around the seed in crusted soil.\nIn Niger farmer-managed natural regeneration has regreened about 5 million hectares since the 1980s, as the trees shade the soil, cut wind speed and add leaf litter.\nLiming raises the pH of acid soils and drainage improves gleys, so soil characteristics can be improved as well as protected.",
