    "label": "20",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Conflict arises when a state's boundaries or political structures ignore, dominate or suppress a cultural group, as shown by Northern Ireland, Spain and the Kurds.\nAspect 1 – Northern Ireland:\nThe Government of Ireland Act 1920 led to partition, and Northern Ireland was established in 1921 with six counties and a Protestant, unionist majority.\nThe state was created around a unionist majority with a large nationalist minority.\nCatholics faced discrimination in housing, jobs and local voting, including gerrymandering in Derry.\nThe civil rights movement began in 1968, British troops were deployed in 1969 and internment without trial was introduced in 1971.\nOn Bloody Sunday, 30 January 1972, British paratroopers shot dead 13 civil rights marchers in Derry.\nThe Troubles, from the late 1960s to 1998, killed over 3,500 people.\nIn the 1981 hunger strikes ten republican prisoners, including Bobby Sands, died.\nThe Good Friday Agreement of 10 April 1998 redesigned the political structure with a power-sharing Assembly and Executive, North-South bodies and the consent principle.\nPeace walls separate Catholic and Protestant areas of Belfast, and the first was built in 1969 and some still stand.\nAspect 2 – Basques and Catalans in Spain:\nBasques and Catalans have their own languages, autonomy statutes and strong regional identities within Spain.\nBasque and Catalan were banned from public life and schools under Franco from 1939 to 1975.\nSpain's 1978 constitution created 17 autonomous communities.\nThe Basque Autonomous Community was created by the Statute of Gernika in 1979 and collects its own taxes.\nETA, founded in 1959, killed over 800 people in its campaign for an independent Basque state.\nETA declared a definitive end to violence in 2011 and dissolved in 2018.\nIn 2010 Spain's Constitutional Court struck down parts of Catalonia's 2006 autonomy statute, including its description of Catalonia as a nation, which fuelled the independence movement.\nOn 1 October 2017 an independence referendum was declared illegal by Spain's courts and police tried to stop the voting, and about 90% of those voting backed independence on a turnout of about 43%.\nSpain imposed direct rule using Article 155 of its constitution, and nine Catalan leaders were jailed in 2019 and pardoned in 2021.\nAspect 3 – The Kurds:\nThe Kurds, commonly estimated at 30 million or more, are the largest ethnic group without their own state.\nKurdistan is divided between Turkey, Iran, Iraq and Syria, and the largest number of Kurds live in south-east Turkey.\nThe Treaty of Sèvres in 1920 provided for a possible Kurdish state, but the Treaty of Lausanne in 1923 drew the borders of modern Turkey with no Kurdish state.\nTurkey long denied a separate Kurdish identity and banned Kurdish in public life until 1991 and in broadcasting until the early 2000s.\nThe PKK, founded in 1978, began an armed campaign in 1984, and the conflict cost about 40,000 lives.\nOn 16 March 1988 Saddam Hussein's forces attacked the Kurdish town of Halabja with chemical weapons, killing about 5,000 people.\nIraq's 2005 constitution recognised the Kurdistan Region as autonomous, with its own government and parliament in Erbil.\nAbout 93% voted for independence in a referendum on 25 September 2017, which Iraq's government rejected, and Iraqi forces then retook Kirkuk.\nIn Syria Kurdish-led forces fought the Islamic State group, including the defence of Kobani in 2014 and 2015.",
