    "label": "6B",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Part (i) – Named primary activity:\nCattle rearing in the West of Ireland.\nPart (ii) – Factor 1 – Climate:\nThe West Region of Galway, Mayo and Roscommon has a cool, wet oceanic climate, with about 1,000 to 1,250 mm of rain a year on the lowlands.\nMild, moist conditions, warmed by the North Atlantic Drift, grow grass well, which suits cattle and sheep rearing.\nWaterlogged land and wet weather make tillage difficult, so farmers rely on grass-based livestock instead of crops.\nWet weather makes hay and silage hard to save, which raises the cost of winter feed.\nRainfall of over 2,000 mm on the mountains gives a short effective grazing season on higher ground.\nExposure to Atlantic gales limits the land that can be farmed and the crops that can be grown.\nCool, cloudy summers, with mean July temperatures of only 14 to 16 degrees Celsius, restrict the ripening of crops.\nPart (ii) – Factor 2 – Relief and soils:\nRugged uplands such as the Twelve Bens, Maumturks and Nephin are too steep and rocky for machinery, so cattle are kept on the lower land while the hills are left to sheep.\nBlanket bog covers much of west Mayo and Connemara and supports only poor rough grazing.\nHeavy, poorly drained gley soils on the drumlins and clay lowlands are too wet for tillage, so they are used for cattle.\nLeached, acidic podzols with an iron pan on the uplands are infertile and limit plant growth.\nBetter-drained, more fertile brown earths on the limestone of east Galway and Roscommon support the best grassland and the only tillage.\nFarms on the poor land are small and fragmented, so incomes are low and many farmers work part-time.\nYoung store cattle are sold through marts such as Ballinasloe to farmers in the east for fattening, which reflects the limits of the land.",
