    "label": "6C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Problem 1 – Out-migration and an ageing population:\nThe West of Ireland, the Northern and Western region, is a peripheral region far from Dublin, the core, with a weaker economy and fewer opportunities.\nYoung adults leave for education and work in Dublin and abroad, because few well-paid jobs are available in the west.\nThis leaves an ageing population and falling numbers in rural areas, so fewer babies are born and natural increase is low.\nFalling numbers cause services such as schools, shops, post offices and Garda stations to close, which makes the area less attractive to stay in.\nSmall parishes and clubs struggle to field teams and keep community life going, and vacant houses and derelict farms become common.\nEU funds and state agencies respond with investment, and LEADER, from 1991, funds local rural enterprise to keep jobs in the area.\nPoor public transport and slow broadband in remote areas make living and working there harder, which pushes more people to leave.\nGraduates seldom return because the west has few jobs that match their qualifications.\nProblem 2 – Small farms on poor land:\nMuch of the west has thin, waterlogged soils, high rainfall and rugged relief, which limit what farmers can grow.\nFarms are small and fragmented and mostly rear beef cattle and sheep, so incomes are low.\nMany farms depend heavily on direct payments, and Areas of Natural Constraint payments exist because the land is disadvantaged.\nThere are few other jobs because industry is limited and far from markets, so underemployment is common.\nFarm holders are ageing, with a third of Irish farm holders aged 65 or over, and small western farms are the most uneconomic.\nTourism built on the scenery and culture, and LEADER support for rural tourism and crafts, give farm families extra income.\nWet ground is easily damaged by cattle in winter, so animals must be housed for longer and feed costs rise.\nMany farm families rely on a member working part-time off the farm to earn enough to stay on the land.",
