    "model": "Part (i) – Named MNC and location:\nIntel is the multinational company studied.\nIt is located at Leixlip, Co. Kildare, on the M4 west of Dublin.\nPart (ii) – Raw material and product:\nThe raw material is silicon, bought as silicon wafers.\nThe product is the microprocessor, or computer chip.\nPart (iii) – Advantages of Ireland for Intel:\nIreland has a low rate of corporation tax, which keeps Intel's profits high.\nThe IDA offers grants and support to attract and keep foreign investment.\nA young, educated, English-speaking workforce is available, with graduates from Maynooth University nearby and from Trinity College and University College Dublin.\nMembership of the EU gives Intel access to the single market of hundreds of millions of customers.\nLeixlip has a large, flat greenfield site beside the M4, which gives easy road access to Dublin and Dublin Airport for exporting its small, valuable processed wafers by air.\nChip plants need large amounts of water and electricity, and both are available at Leixlip.\nA cluster of similar firms and suppliers already exists in Ireland, and the political situation is stable and favours business.\nPart (iv) – A difficulty facing Intel:\nIntel faces strong competition from firms such as TSMC, AMD, Samsung and Nvidia.\nApple stopped using Intel chips in its Mac computers and began using its own M1 chip in 2020, so Intel lost a major customer.\nFalling profits led to about 15,000 job cuts worldwide being announced in 2024, which puts the future of plants such as Leixlip under pressure.\nBuilding new chip plants costs enormous sums, so Intel struggles to match the investment of its rivals.",
