    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named colonised country – India:\nIndia is a developing economy that was colonised.\nNamed colonising country – Britain:\nIndia was colonised by Britain, first through the East India Company and then through direct British rule from 1858.\nEffects of colonialism on India:\nIndia's large handloom textile industry, including Bengal's fine cottons, was destroyed by cheap machine-made cloth from the Lancashire mills.\nThis happened because Britain bought raw cotton from India cheaply and sold manufactured cloth back, so India changed from a manufacturer into a supplier of raw materials.\nCash crops such as cotton, jute, indigo and tea replaced food crops, so farmers became dependent on export markets.\nThis weakened food security and contributed to famines, such as the Bengal famine of 1943 that killed about 3 million people.\nRailways were built mainly to carry raw materials to the ports of Bombay and Calcutta, not to link Indian regions with each other.\nPorts and colonial cities such as Bombay and Calcutta grew quickly while the interior was neglected, so development was concentrated on the coast.\nRevenue, profits and salaries were sent from India to Britain in what is called the drain of wealth, so money that could have financed development was lost.\nHeavy land taxes under the Permanent Settlement left the peasantry in debt and poverty.\nIndia inherited English, a civil service, courts and a parliamentary system, and English is now an asset for the IT industry in Bengaluru.\nAt independence in 1947 literacy was only about 18% and life expectancy about 32 years, because colonial education reached only a small elite.",
