    "label": "5B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Region – India:\nIndia is a subcontinental region in South Asia, bounded by the Himalayas, the Arabian Sea and the Bay of Bengal.\nCensus 2011 recorded about 1.21 billion people, with an average density of 382 people per square kilometre.\nDense area 1 – The Ganges plain:\nThe Ganges plain in Uttar Pradesh and Bihar is one of the most densely populated areas of India.\nBihar is the densest state, with about 1,100 people per square kilometre.\nThe plain is flat and built from fertile alluvium deposited by rivers, so it supports intensive farming.\nThe Ganges and its tributaries, together with monsoon rain, provide a reliable water supply for irrigation.\nHuman factors include a very long history of settlement and large numbers of farmers.\nDense area 2 – Coastal plains and cities:\nCoastal plains and deltas, such as Kerala and West Bengal, have flat land, fertile soil and heavy rainfall.\nAround large cities such as Mumbai, Delhi and Kolkata, population is dense because of jobs in industry and services.\nRural to urban migration moves people from Bihar and Uttar Pradesh to the cities.\nSparse area 1 – The Thar Desert:\nThe Thar Desert in Rajasthan is sparsely populated because of aridity.\nIt is the driest region, because the Arabian Sea branch of the monsoon passes parallel to the Aravalli Range and has no barrier to force it upwards.\nWater is scarce and soils are poor, so farming and settlement are very limited.\nSparse area 2 – The Himalayas:\nThe Himalayas are sparsely populated because of cold temperatures, steep slopes and thin soils.\nArunachal Pradesh in the north-east is the least dense state, with about 17 people per square kilometre.\nThe mountains are also prone to earthquakes and landslides and are a barrier to transport, so access is poor.\nThe forested hills of the north-east are also sparsely populated.",
