    "label": "6C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Example of a region defined by language – the Gaeltacht:\nA cultural region is an area where people share a trait such as language that sets them apart from neighbouring areas.\nThe Gaeltacht is the officially recognised area where Irish is, or was, the community language.\nIt is not one continuous region, because it is made up of pockets in seven counties, including Donegal, Mayo, Galway, Kerry, Cork and Waterford.\nConamara and the Aran Islands in Co. Galway form the largest Gaeltacht area.\nIrish survived in remote, mountainous areas with poor farmland and poor communications, which had the least contact with English-speaking trade, towns and administration.\nIrish is the first official language under Article 8 of the 1937 Constitution.\nÚdarás na Gaeltachta, set up in 1980, promotes employment and the language in the Gaeltacht.\nRaidió na Gaeltachta and TG4 broadcast in Irish.\nThe two Meath Gaeltachtaí were created in the 1930s by resettling Irish-speaking families from Connemara.\nThreats include out-migration of young people, in-migration of English speakers, holiday homes and English-language media.\nSecond example – Belgium:\nBelgium is divided by a language border running east to west, fixed by law in 1962 and 1963.\nFlanders in the north is Dutch-speaking, and Wallonia in the south is French-speaking.\nA small German-speaking community lives in the east around Eupen, and the Brussels-Capital Region is officially bilingual in French and Dutch.\nBelgium became a federal state in 1993, with three regions and three language communities, each with its own parliament.\nSchools, media and political parties are divided by language, and the university at Leuven split in 1968, when its French-speaking section moved to Louvain-la-Neuve.\nAfter the 2010 election Belgium went 541 days without a new government because the Flemish and French-speaking parties could not agree.\nThe language divide matches an economic divide, because Flanders became the wealthier region.",
