    "model": "Reason 1 – Good transport links:\nArklow is easy for tourists to reach by road, rail and sea.\nThe map shows the M11 motorway with Junction 20 at T 249 761 and the R772 regional road crossing the Avoca at T 247 735.\nThe aerial photograph shows a bridge over the river in the right middleground and a busy main road in the right foreground carrying traffic into the town.\nThe M11 links Arklow to Dublin, the main gateway for overseas visitors through its airport and port.\nA railway line passes through the town at about T 242 729, so visitors without cars can arrive by train.\nArklow Harbour at T 250 732 is open to the sea, so visiting yachts and boats can berth there.\nEasy access reduces journey time and cost, which makes day trips and short breaks possible.\nReason 2 – Coast, beach and harbour amenities:\nArklow has a coastal location with beaches, a harbour and a marina that attract visitors.\nThe map shows the beach along Ferrybank at T 256 741, a sailing club at T 252 734, a pier at T 256 731 and a car park at T 256 742.\nThe aerial photograph shows a marina with moored boats in the left background and a sea wall and beach along the left edge.\nBeaches and the sea attract families for swimming, walking and relaxation.\nThe harbour and sailing club provide sailing and boating, which draw activity holidaymakers.\nThe athletics track and playing field in the left foreground give visitors space for sport and recreation.\nReason 3 – Services and attractions:\nArklow is a large town with the services, attractions and walks that tourists need.\nThe map shows a tourist information point at T 242 735, a post office at T 245 735 and the castle at T 244 734 within a large built-up area around T 243 731.\nThe aerial photograph shows a large shopping and leisure centre in the centre middleground, apartments beside the marina in the left middleground and shops along the main street in the right foreground.\nTourist information and a post office provide useful services for visitors.\nShops, restaurants and accommodation in the town meet the needs of overnight visitors and create local jobs.\nGlenart Walk at T 213 744 and the wooded Vale of Avoca give scenery and walking trails close to the town.\nHeritage sites such as the castle and Shelton Abbey at T 221 756 add cultural interest.",
