    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Issue 1 – Housing supply and affordability:\nDemand for homes in cities rises with population growth, smaller households and migration, and supply has not kept pace.\nDublin is an example, where high rents and house prices put homes beyond the reach of many households.\nHomelessness is a visible result, with over 15,000 people in emergency accommodation in 2025.\nHousing for All, published in 2021, aims for about 33,000 new homes a year on average to 2030.\nRent Pressure Zones, introduced from 2016, limit rent increases in the areas where they apply.\nHigher-density apartments near public transport use land more efficiently.\nPublic and cost-rental housing provide homes at below-market rents.\nIn developing-world cities the same issue appears as slum growth with poor water and sanitation.\nIssue 2 – Transport and sustainable mobility:\nCar dependence causes congestion and vehicle emissions in growing cities.\nDublin plans MetroLink and BusConnects, and the Greater Dublin Area Transport Strategy 2022–2042 sets out the long-term plan.\nLondon's Ultra Low Emission Zone began in 2019 and was extended London-wide in August 2023 to reduce emissions.\nCopenhagen is a cycling city, where cycling is the most common way of commuting to work or study.\nParis is promoting the 15-minute city, where everyday needs can be met within 15 minutes on foot or by bike.\nVauban in Freiburg, Germany, is a low-car eco-district built from the late 1990s on a former military barracks.\nElectric vehicles reduce the emissions of the cars that remain on the roads.",
