    "label": "10C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Pattern 1 – Heavy emigration in the 1950s:\nEmigration in the 1950s was the highest since the 1880s, with net emigration of about 400,000 between 1951 and 1961.\nIt was caused by economic stagnation and protectionism, unemployment, and the decline of small farms and farm labouring as farming mechanised.\nMost emigrants went to Britain, drawn by post-war demand for labour in building, factories, nursing and transport, in cities such as London, Birmingham and Manchester.\nThe Common Travel Area, low fares and chain migration made the move to Britain easy.\nPattern 2 – Net immigration in the 1970s:\nThe 1970s were the first decade of net immigration since the Famine, as returning emigrants and their children came back.\nThe First Programme for Economic Expansion of 1958 and the IDA's attraction of foreign factories from 1969 created jobs and cut emigration.\nIreland joined the EEC on 1 January 1973, and the State population rose from about 3.0 million in 1971 to about 3.4 million in 1981.\nPattern 3 – Emigration again in the 1980s:\nRecession, high unemployment and high taxes caused net emigration of over 200,000 between 1981 and 1991.\nMany emigrants were graduates, so the loss became known as the brain drain.\nDestinations were Britain and the USA, where many Irish emigrants were undocumented until the Donnelly and Morrison visas.\nPattern 4 – Immigration during the Celtic Tiger:\nFrom 1996 there was net immigration as returning Irish, EU migrants, work-permit holders and asylum seekers arrived for jobs in the booming economy.\nWhen ten new states, including Poland and Lithuania, joined the EU on 1 May 2004, Ireland opened its labour market immediately.\nBy Census 2006 about 420,000 non-Irish nationals lived in Ireland, about 10% of the population, and Polish people were the largest group after UK nationals.\nPattern 5 – Emigration after the 2008 crash:\nThe property crash, the banking crisis and the EU–IMF bailout of 2010 caused unemployment, and emigration peaked at about 89,000 in the year to April 2013.\nMany leavers were young graduates, and the main destinations were Australia, the UK, Canada and the USA.\nPattern 6 – Renewed immigration since the mid-2010s:\nEconomic recovery, jobs in multinationals and services, and returning Irish emigrants brought net immigration back.\nOver 100,000 people from Ukraine had come to Ireland by 2024 under EU temporary protection after Russia's invasion in February 2022.\nCensus 2022 recorded about 630,000 non-Irish nationals, about 12% of the population, with Polish, UK and Indian nationals the largest groups.",
