    "label": "16",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Human activity can damage soil through overcropping, overgrazing and poor land management, but it can also protect and improve soil through conservation and careful management.\nAspect 1 – Negative impact of overcropping:\nOvercropping is growing crops on the same land too intensively, without fallow, rotation or enough organic matter returned.\nNutrients are removed faster than they are replaced, and the humus content falls.\nThe crumb structure breaks down, so the soil becomes dusty or compacted and holds less water.\nMonoculture takes the same nutrients each year and encourages pests, so yields fall.\nIn the 1930s the natural grassland of the Great Plains of the USA, including Oklahoma and Kansas, was ploughed up for wheat.\nSevere drought then left bare, dry soil that strong winds blew away in huge dust storms, and many farming families were forced to leave in the Dust Bowl.\nThe US Soil Conservation Service was set up in 1935, and shelter belts of trees, contour ploughing and crop rotation were promoted.\nAspect 2 – Negative impact of overgrazing:\nOvergrazing is keeping more animals on land than its vegetation can support, so the vegetation dies and the soil is exposed to rain-splash and wind.\nTrampling compacts the soil, and on wet Irish ground this poaching reduces infiltration and increases runoff.\nFrom the 1980s EU headage payments paid farmers per animal, so sheep numbers rose sharply on hill commonage in the west of Ireland, such as in Connemara.\nOvergrazing there stripped heather and grass, eroded blanket peat and sent silt into rivers, harming salmon and trout spawning grounds.\nIn the Sahel, the semi-arid belt south of the Sahara, livestock were concentrated around wells, fallow periods shortened and fuelwood was cut, while droughts in the 1970s and 1980s left bare soil to be eroded.\nThis desertification brought loss of topsoil and humus, falling yields, food shortages and migration to cities and abroad.\nLake Chad has shrunk by about 90% since the 1960s through drought and the use of its water for irrigation.\nAspect 3 – Positive impact of soil conservation:\nContour ploughing across a slope makes furrows trap water instead of channelling it downhill.\nTerracing cuts steep slopes into level steps, as in the rice terraces of Banaue on Luzon in the Philippines.\nShelter belts and windbreaks of trees and hedges cut wind speed and reduce wind erosion.\nCrop rotation, fallow, cover crops and green manure such as clover restore nutrients and humus.\nIn Niger, farmer-managed natural regeneration, where farmers protect and prune tree shoots that regrow from old stumps, has regreened about 5 million hectares since the 1980s.\nIn Yatenga in Burkina Faso, farmers such as Yacouba Sawadogo revived zaï pits, which catch rainwater and concentrate manure around the seed.\nTrees shade the soil, cut wind speed and add leaf litter, and species such as Faidherbia albida also add nitrogen.\nAspect 4 – Positive impact of improving soils:\nLiming raises the pH of acid soils, and field drainage and arterial drainage improve gleys.\nThe Arterial Drainage Act of 1945 began state drainage programmes to deepen and straighten Irish rivers to drain wet farmland.\nOn the bare limestone of the Aran Islands, islanders built soil by layering sand and seaweed, and later manure, on the rock inside stone-walled fields.\nIn the Burren, the BurrenLIFE project of 2005 to 2010 and the later Burren Programme pay farmers by results, scoring each field for habitat quality.\nIceland's Soil Conservation Service, founded in 1907, fences off eroding land to keep sheep out and sows lyme grass to stabilise drifting sand.\nFarmer-led reclamation projects called Farmers Heal the Land began in Iceland in 1990, and sheep numbers have fallen since their peak in the late 1970s.\nIceland shows both sides, because Norse settlers from about AD 874 cut and burned birch woodland and kept sheep that prevented regrowth, yet conservation has reclaimed land for over a century.",
