    "label": "17",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Brown earth is Ireland's main lowland soil, and its composition and characteristics are explained by the mild, wet climate, the glacial till and the deciduous woodland under which it formed.\nAspect 1 – Location and formation of brown earth:\nBrown earth is the most widespread soil of the Irish lowlands, especially in the south, east and Midlands.\nThe fertile grassland and tillage soils of the Golden Vale in Tipperary and Limerick and of the south-east are brown earths.\nIt formed under Ireland's mild, wet, cool temperate oceanic climate, with rainfall of roughly 750 to 1,000 mm in the east.\nThe deciduous woodland of oak, ash, elm and hazel that covered most of Ireland after the Ice Age supplied the litter.\nThe parent material is usually glacial till laid down during the last glaciation, which ended about 10,000 years ago, so the soil is young.\nMild leaching carries some iron and clay down from the A horizon to the B horizon.\nEarthworms mix the mull humus into the soil, so no bleached layer forms.\nAspect 2 – Profile of brown earth:\nThe O horizon is a thin layer of mull humus.\nThe A horizon is dark brown and loamy, with a crumb structure, and is roughly 20 to 30 cm deep.\nThe B horizon is lighter brown, with some iron and clay washed in from above.\nThe boundary between the A and B horizons is gradual, because earthworms mix the soil.\nThe C horizon is weathered till or rock.\nThere is no bleached E horizon, because leaching is only mild, unlike in a podzol.\nThe brown colour comes from humus together with iron compounds.\nAspect 3 – Composition of brown earth:\nA good loam such as brown earth has about 45% mineral matter, 5% organic matter, 25% water and 25% air by volume.\nMineral matter is particles of weathered rock, which form the soil's skeleton and decide its texture.\nThe mineral matter releases nutrients such as calcium, magnesium and potassium as it weathers.\nOrganic matter is living organisms plus dead remains, which decomposers break down into humus.\nMull humus is mild, crumbly and well decomposed, and it binds particles into crumbs, holds water and nutrients and releases nutrients slowly.\nEarthworms mix and aerate the soil, and their casts improve its structure.\nWater and air share the pore spaces, so the soil drains well and is aerated yet holds enough moisture for plants.\nAspect 4 – Characteristics and uses of brown earth:\nThe texture is loamy, which combines good drainage with good water and nutrient retention.\nThe structure is crumb, the best structure for farming, because it opens pores for both air and water.\nThe soil is slightly acidic, at roughly pH 5.5 to 6.5.\nIt is fertile because the clay-humus complex holds nutrients such as calcium, magnesium and potassium so that they are not washed away.\nIt is used for grassland and tillage in the Golden Vale and the south-east.\nFarmers spread lime where the pH falls below the Teagasc targets of about 6.3 for grassland and 6.5 for tillage.\nGrey-brown podzolics, slightly more leached relatives with clay washed into the B horizon, occur on the limestone-rich till of the east Midlands in Meath and Kildare.",
