    "label": "18",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "The tropical rainforest, found in the Amazon Basin, the Congo Basin and South-East Asia, has a hot, wet equatorial climate that shapes its soils and its vegetation.\nAspect 1 – Climate of the tropical rainforest:\nMean monthly temperatures are about 26 to 28 °C all year.\nThe annual range is only about 2 to 3 °C, because the sun stays high and day length barely changes.\nAnnual rainfall is over 2,000 mm, and Manaus in the central Amazon receives about 2,300 mm a year.\nRain falls in every month, with no dry season.\nIntense morning heating makes moist air rise, cool and build up cumulonimbus clouds.\nHeavy afternoon thunderstorms follow, almost daily.\nThe low-pressure belt of rising air along the equator, the ITCZ, drives this convectional rainfall.\nRelative humidity is often over 80%.\nA large share of the Amazon's rain is water recycled from the forest by evapotranspiration.\nAspect 2 – Impact of the climate on soils:\nThe heat and heavy rain cause intense chemical weathering to great depth, so latosols are many metres deep.\nHeavy rainfall leaches silica and bases out of the soil in a process called laterisation.\nInsoluble iron and aluminium oxides are left behind, which gives the soil its deep red or yellow colour.\nThe soil is acidic, clay-rich and low in nutrients.\nLitter decomposes within weeks in the heat and humidity, so little humus builds up.\nShallow roots and mycorrhizal fungi take up nutrients almost at once and pass them to the trees.\nMost nutrients are held in the living vegetation, so the nutrient cycle is rapid and closed.\nWhen the forest is cleared the soil loses fertility within a few years and erodes under heavy rain.\nWhere cleared soil dries out, the iron-rich layer can harden into a brick-like laterite crust.\nAspect 3 – Impact of the climate on vegetation:\nThe constant warmth and moisture support a dense, evergreen, broadleaved forest, with leaves shed and replaced all year.\nHundreds of tree species grow in a single hectare of Amazon rainforest, compared with a handful in an Irish wood.\nEmergents such as kapok and the Brazil nut tree rise above the canopy at about 45 to 60 m.\nThe canopy at about 25 to 40 m is a dense, continuous layer that intercepts most sunlight and rain.\nOnly about 2% of the sunlight reaching the canopy arrives at the dark, humid forest floor.\nLarge leaves in the understorey capture the little light that reaches it.\nDrip tips and waxy leaves shed the heavy rain.\nBecause the nutrients are held near the surface, trees have shallow roots, and buttress roots support tall trees such as kapok.\nLianas climb trees to reach the light, and epiphytes such as orchids and bromeliads grow on branches and take moisture from the air and rain.",
