    "label": "22",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Climate characteristics such as temperature, rainfall and seasonal reliability influence economic development by deciding what can be farmed, how much water is available, where tourists go and how vulnerable people are to drought.\nAspect 1 – Agriculture:\nClimate decides what crops can be grown and how productive farming is, so it strongly influences agricultural development.\nIn Ireland mild temperatures keep soil above about 6 °C for most of the year, giving a long grass-growing season that supports dairy, beef and sheep farming.\nRainfall falls from over 1,000 mm across much of the west to about 750 mm in the drier east, so tillage is concentrated in the south and east, in counties such as Wexford, Carlow and Kildare.\nToo much rain waterlogs the soil, shortens the grazing season and raises feed costs, and the very wet summer of 2012 helped cause the fodder crisis of spring 2013.\nToo little rain also damages output, because the long anticyclonic spell of summer 2018 almost stopped grass growth and forced fodder imports.\nIn India the south-west monsoon reaches Kerala around 1 June and brings about three-quarters of the annual rainfall, so a late or weak monsoon cuts harvests, farm incomes and GDP.\nIn Egypt, which is almost rainless, farming depends on irrigation from the Nile, and the Aswan High Dam, completed in 1970, makes year-round irrigation and more than one crop a year possible.\nRich economies overcome climatic limits with irrigation and technology, as in California's Central Valley, but poor rain-fed economies remain the most exposed.\nAspect 2 – Water supply and drought:\nRainfall decides how much water is available for homes, farms and industry, and drought is a major brake on development.\nIreland has plenty of rain overall, but most of it falls in the west while most people live in the drier east, where Dublin receives about 750 mm a year.\nDemand peaks in hot dry weather when supply is lowest, so Irish Water imposed a hosepipe ban in the Greater Dublin Area in July 2018 that was later extended nationwide.\nCape Town in South Africa, which has a Mediterranean climate, had three dry winters from about 2015 to 2017 that left its reservoirs very low and led to a Day Zero warning in early 2018.\nFrom February 2018 residents were limited to 50 litres per person per day, and Day Zero was avoided after use was roughly halved and the winter rains of 2018 returned.\nDrought causes crop failure, livestock losses, higher food prices and lost rural income, and it can cut hydroelectricity and disrupt river transport.\nIn the Horn of Africa in 2011 two failed rainy seasons killed crops and livestock, and the UN declared famine in parts of southern Somalia in July 2011.\nDeveloped economies such as Australia absorbed the Millennium Drought through insurance, government aid, water trading and desalination plants, while poor economies with few such resources faced famine.\nAspect 3 – Tourism:\nClimate is a tourism resource, so it influences where tourism develops and when visitors arrive.\nHot, dry, sunny summers made the Mediterranean one of the world's most visited regions, and Spain received about 85 million international tourists in 2023.\nTourists arrive in the driest season, so water demand from hotels, pools and golf courses peaks when supply is lowest, and many jobs are seasonal.\nThe Canary Islands, at about 28° N, have mild dry winters and the cooling Canary Current, which gives them a year-round season that attracts northern Europeans.\nAlpine ski tourism depends on reliable snow cover, and climate change is raising the snow line so that lower resorts struggle or close, while others invest in snow-making.\nIreland's cool, cloudy and wet summers limit beach tourism, so the industry markets scenery and outdoor activities, for example along the Wild Atlantic Way, which was launched in 2014 and runs for about 2,500 km.\nHurricane Irma in September 2017 destroyed or damaged almost every building on Barbuda, showing how climatic hazards can wreck island tourism.\nHeatwaves and wildfires also threaten tourism, as in July 2023 when wildfires on the Greek island of Rhodes forced thousands of tourists to be evacuated.\nAspect 4 – Desertification in the Sahel:\nWhere rainfall is low and unreliable, drought and human pressure can cause desertification, which undermines economic development.\nThe Sahel runs from Senegal and Mauritania through Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger and Chad to Sudan, with rainfall of roughly 200 to 600 mm falling in one short summer wet season.\nRainfall reliability matters as much as the total, because the lower the average rainfall, the more variable it is from year to year.\nDroughts in the 1970s and 1980s caused failed harvests and livestock deaths, and pastoralists lost their livelihoods.\nThe region became dependent on food imports, and many people migrated to cities and abroad.\nLake Chad has shrunk by about 90 per cent since the 1960s, which has hit the fishing and farming around it.\nConflict between herders and farmers has grown over shrinking land and water.\nResponses include the Great Green Wall launched by the African Union in 2007 and the regreening of about 5 million hectares in Niger through farmer-managed natural regeneration.",
