    "label": "3B(iii)",
    "marks": 18,
    "model": "Feature named – Stalactite:\nA stalactite is an icicle-shaped deposit of calcite that hangs from a cave roof.\nFormation:\nRainwater containing carbon dioxide percolates down joints and bedding planes in the limestone and dissolves it by carbonation, forming caves.\nWater dripping from the cave roof carries dissolved calcium bicarbonate.\nAs the drop hangs there, it loses carbon dioxide and evaporates, so it deposits a small ring of calcite.\nRepeated drops build up the deposit into a stalactite that grows downward from the roof.\nDrops that fall to the floor deposit calcite to build a thicker, blunter stalagmite upward from the floor beneath, and where the two meet they form a pillar.\nDoolin Cave in Co. Clare holds the Great Stalactite, about 7.3 m long, one of the longest free-hanging stalactites in the world.\nA labelled cross-section shows the cave roof, a stalactite, a stalagmite, a pillar and an underground river along a bedding plane.\nThe diagram also shows water percolating down a joint above the cave.",
    "model_source": "sonnet-long",
    "answerStatus": "written",
