    "label": "3C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Diagram – Plate collision cross-section:\nA cross-section diagram would show two continental plates moving towards each other on convection currents, with arrows showing the direction of movement.\nIt would label the ocean sediments squeezed between the plates and folded up into peaks and troughs to form the fold mountains.\nDescription and explanation – Formation:\nFold mountains form where two plates converge at a destructive boundary.\nAn ocean once separated the plates, and sediments from it lay between them.\nWhen two continental plates collide, both are too light to subduct.\nThe crust between them, including the sediments, is crushed, folded and pushed up into high fold mountains.\nEarthquakes are common at these boundaries, but there is little or no volcanic activity.\nThe crust is thickened and folded rather than destroyed.\nNamed example – The Himalayas:\nThe Indo-Australian plate is colliding with the Eurasian plate, forming the Himalayas and the Tibetan Plateau.\nThe collision began about 50 million years ago and continues today.\nMount Everest, at 8,849 m, is the highest mountain on earth.\nMarine fossils in limestone near its summit show the rocks were once on the floor of the Tethys Sea.\nSecond type – Oceanic and continental plates, the Andes:\nWhere an oceanic plate meets a continental plate, the denser oceanic plate is forced down into the mantle in subduction.\nSediments scraped off the subducting plate are crumpled against the continent into fold mountains.\nThe Nazca plate subducts beneath the South American plate, forming the Peru-Chile Trench offshore and the Andes, with many volcanoes, on land.\nThe Alps formed where the African plate is colliding with the Eurasian plate.",
