    "label": "5C(ii)",
    "marks": 27,
    "model": "Region named – County Kerry:\nCo. Kerry in south-west Ireland is a mass-tourism region, and tourism is a tertiary activity.\nKerry has outstanding scenery, centred on Killarney National Park, which was created in 1932 as Ireland's first national park and includes the Lakes of Killarney and Torc Waterfall.\nCarrauntoohil in the Macgillycuddy's Reeks, at 1,038 m, is Ireland's highest mountain and attracts walkers and climbers.\nThe Ring of Kerry, a circular route of about 180 km around the Iveragh Peninsula, is a popular scenic touring route.\nThe Dingle Peninsula, with the Slea Head drive and the Dingle Gaeltacht, adds scenery and Irish culture.\nSkellig Michael, an early Christian monastery on a rocky island, has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1996, and it was a film location for Star Wars in 2015 and 2017.\nHeritage attractions such as Muckross House and Ross Castle add to the appeal.\nTourism in Killarney dates from the 18th and 19th centuries, as the railway reached Killarney in 1853 and Queen Victoria visited in 1861, so Kerry has a long-established reputation.\nKerry Airport at Farranfore and the airports at Shannon and Cork give good access for overseas visitors.\nThe Wild Atlantic Way, launched by Fáilte Ireland in 2014, passes through Kerry and markets the region.\nA well-developed supply of hotels, guesthouses, restaurants and activity providers has grown up to serve the visitors.",
