    "label": "7C(ii)",
    "marks": 24,
    "model": "Global warming:\nBurning fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide, the main greenhouse gas.\nCarbon dioxide traps heat in the atmosphere, so extra carbon dioxide enhances the greenhouse effect and causes global warming.\nAtmospheric carbon dioxide has risen from about 280 ppm before the Industrial Revolution to over 420 ppm by the mid-2020s.\nThe warming melts glaciers and Arctic sea ice, which has shrunk by about 13 per cent per decade since 1979, and it raises sea level and increases the intensity of heatwaves.\nAcid rain:\nBurning coal and heavy oil releases sulphur dioxide, and vehicles and power stations release nitrogen oxides.\nThese gases dissolve in water vapour to form sulphuric and nitric acids, which fall as acid rain with a pH below 5.6.\nWinds carried emissions from Britain and Germany to Norway and Sweden, so thousands of Scandinavian lakes were acidified and lost their fish.\nAcid rain also damaged forests such as the Black Forest in the 1980s and dissolves limestone and marble buildings.\nSmog:\nBurning coal and turf in homes releases smoke and fine particles, which a temperature inversion in calm, cold anticyclonic weather traps near the ground as smog.\nSmog causes bronchitis, asthma and heart and lung disease, and London's Great Smog of December 1952 caused about 4,000 deaths at the time.\nDublin banned the sale of smoky coal in 1990, and black smoke levels fell by about 70 per cent.",
