    "label": "3B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Cause – Fall in base level and rejuvenation:\nBase level is the lowest level to which a river can erode, and sea level is the ultimate base level.\nA fall in base level relative to the land gives the river renewed energy for vertical erosion, which is called rejuvenation.\nA eustatic fall in sea level happens when water is locked up in ice sheets, and isostatic or tectonic uplift also raises the land relative to the sea.\nRejuvenation begins at the mouth and spreads upstream by headward erosion, so the river has a new steeper lower section and an old gentler upper section.\nChange 1 – Knickpoints and waterfalls:\nWhere the new and old graded profiles meet, the long profile has a break of slope called a knickpoint.\nA waterfall or rapids often forms at the knickpoint, and it retreats upstream until the whole river is graded to the new base level.\nChange 2 – Incised meanders:\nRenewed downcutting cuts existing meanders down into the land, forming incised meanders.\nEntrenched meanders have steep, symmetrical sides because downcutting is rapid, as at the Goosenecks of the San Juan River in Utah, USA.\nIngrown meanders have an asymmetrical cross-section with one gentle and one steep side, because downcutting is slower and some lateral erosion continues, as on the River Wye.\nChange 3 – River terraces on the Thames:\nWhen the river cuts down through its old floodplain, remnants of that floodplain are left as flat steps called river terraces above the new floodplain.\nPaired terraces at the same height on both sides form where downcutting is rapid, while unpaired terraces at different heights form when the river also migrates sideways.\nThe River Thames in and around London has well-known river terraces.\nChange 4 – Gorge of the Colorado River:\nDeep incision into uplifted land cuts a gorge, as the Colorado River cut the Grand Canyon in Arizona, USA, while the Colorado Plateau was uplifted.\nThe number of rejuvenations equals the number of knickpoints on a long profile or the number of terrace levels above the present floodplain.\nIn Ireland, rivers of the north-west were rejuvenated when the land rose isostatically after the ice melted about 10,000 years ago.",
