    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Rock 1 – Granite at the Wicklow Mountains:\nGranite is a plutonic igneous rock, and the Leinster batholith in the Wicklow Mountains is the largest granite mass in Ireland and Britain.\nThe magma formed deep in the crust during Caledonian mountain building as the Iapetus Ocean closed, and it cooled about 400 million years ago.\nThe magma was intruded into the surrounding rock and cooled slowly because it was insulated deep underground.\nSlow cooling gave time for large crystals to grow, so granite is made of large interlocking crystals of quartz, feldspar and mica.\nThe magma formed a huge dome-shaped mass called a batholith.\nLater, the rock above was eroded away, which exposed the granite at the surface as the Wicklow upland.\nThe heat of the intrusion baked the surrounding rock and produced a metamorphic aureole of mica schist around the granite.\nThe Mourne granite in Co. Down is a younger intrusion, about 56 million years old.\nRock 2 – Basalt on the Antrim Plateau:\nBasalt is a volcanic igneous rock, and it covers Co. Antrim to form the Antrim plateau.\nAbout 60 million years ago the Atlantic began to open, and cracks developed in the crust.\nRunny lava poured out through these cracks and flowed across the surface in successive sheets.\nThe lava cooled quickly at the surface, so crystals had little time to grow, and basalt is dark and fine-grained.\nRepeated lava flows built up the plateau layer on layer.\nBasalt is the commonest volcanic rock and forms at constructive boundaries and hotspots, so it was produced as the Atlantic opened.\nAs thick lava flows cooled and contracted, the basalt split into columns, as at the Giant's Causeway in Co. Antrim.\nChalk lies beneath the basalt along the Antrim coast, which shows that the lava flowed over older sedimentary rock.",
