    "label": "4B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named region – Cork Harbour:\nSecondary activity in the Cork Harbour region is mainly pharmaceuticals and chemicals, with Pfizer, Janssen and Novartis at Ringaskiddy and Eli Lilly at Kinsale.\nFactor 1 – Deep-water port and industrial sites:\nThe Port of Cork has deep-water berths at Ringaskiddy, so large ships can bring in raw materials and carry out finished goods.\nWhitegate, Cork Harbour, is Ireland's only oil refinery, opened in 1959, and it depends on crude oil imported by sea.\nPharmaceuticals are light, high-value goods that can also be exported quickly by air from Cork Airport.\nLarge flat sites at Ringaskiddy and Little Island are zoned for industry, so firms have room to build and expand.\nThese sites have water supplies and facilities for waste and effluent, which chemical plants need.\nRingaskiddy lies on the harbour shore, away from the city centre, which suits hazardous chemical industry.\nRoad links connect the harbour to Cork city and the national motorway network, so goods reach markets quickly.\nFactor 2 – Skilled labour and government support:\nUniversity College Cork and Munster Technological University supply graduates in science and engineering.\nMunster Technological University was formed in 2021 from Cork IT and IT Tralee, which widened the supply of trained workers.\nIDA Ireland offers grants, serviced sites and advance factories to attract foreign firms to the region.\nCorporation tax of 12.5% from 2003 made Ireland attractive to multinational firms.\nAn English-speaking base in the EU gives firms access to the single market.\nFirms in the same industry gather together to share skilled workers, suppliers and research, so the cluster reinforces itself.\nPharmaceuticals and chemicals are Ireland's largest goods exports, so the Cork Harbour plants are important to the national economy.\nOlder heavy industry such as Irish Steel at Haulbowline closed in 2001, and the pharmaceutical plants replaced the lost jobs.",
