    "model": "Social challenge – Migration and integration:\nConflict in Syria and instability elsewhere led to more than a million migrants and refugees reaching the EU in 2015.\nFrontline Mediterranean states such as Greece and Italy received large numbers of arrivals and found it hard to cope.\nMember states disagreed over how to share responsibility for asylum seekers, and some, such as Hungary, resisted quotas.\nSchengen border-free travel came under strain when some states reintroduced border checks.\nIntegrating newcomers into housing, schools, jobs and language is difficult and can cause social tension and segregation.\nSupport for anti-immigration parties has grown in several member states.\nMigrants also fill labour shortages and help to support ageing societies.\nA second social challenge is an ageing population, because low birth rates and longer life expectancy raise pension and healthcare costs for a shrinking workforce.\nEconomic challenge – Debt, unemployment and trade:\nAfter the 2008 financial crash, countries such as Greece, Ireland and Portugal had high public debt and needed bailouts.\nThe bailouts demanded austerity, with cuts in public spending that reduced demand and raised unemployment.\nEuro members cannot devalue their currency, so weaker economies found it hard to regain competitiveness.\nYouth unemployment stayed high in southern Europe, in countries such as Spain, Greece and Italy.\nPeripheral members such as Greece trade much less within the EU than core members, so development remains uneven.\nBrexit moved the UK from internal to external partner after it left on 31 January 2020, and trade became more costly through customs declarations and checks.\nRussian gas fell from about 45% of EU gas imports in 2021 to about 19% in 2024 after the invasion of Ukraine, and the EU had to find costly new energy supplies.\nThe EU-USA agreement of July 2025 capped the US tariff on most EU goods at 15%, which affects exporters such as Irish pharmaceutical firms.",
