    "label": "6B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named region – the Paris Basin, France:\nThe Paris Basin is a large, low sedimentary lowland in northern France, centred on Paris.\nIt is the main arable region of France, with cereals in the Beauce and vines in Champagne.\nFactor 1 – Relief:\nThe basin is a saucer of sedimentary rock and the land is mostly below about 300 m, so the relief is low and gently rolling.\nGentle slopes allow large fields to be worked with machinery, and many cereal farms in the Beauce exceed 100 hectares.\nThe flat plateau of the Beauce, south-west of Paris around Chartres, is the granary of France and grows wheat, barley and maize.\nThe level plateaux of Brie and Picardy also suit large mechanised farms, which grow sugar beet and oilseed rape.\nOn flat plateaux the deep, fine limon soil stays in place instead of washing downhill, so it remains fertile and easy to work.\nOn the eastern side, resistant rock layers form escarpments, and vines grow on their slopes around Reims and Épernay.\nThe sloping ground and thin chalk soils drain freely, which suits vines, and only sparkling wine from this protected area may be called Champagne.\nThe dry chalk plain east of Reims is flat and was once called Champagne pouilleuse, but fertiliser and mechanised farming turned it into productive cereal land.\nThe gentle gradients of the Seine and its tributaries make them navigable, so barges can carry grain and other produce towards Rouen and Paris.\nBetween the escarpments lie low, flat clay vales, and the wetter low-lying land in the west suits grass, so Normandy has dairy farming and produces Camembert cheese.\nFactor 2 – Markets:\nÎle-de-France has about 12 million people, about 18% of France's population, so there is a very large market for food close to the farms.\nMarket gardening of vegetables and flowers is practised on the edge of Paris because fresh produce reaches the market quickly.\nRungis International Market, south of Paris, opened in 1969 and is one of the world's largest wholesale fresh-food markets, linking farms to the city's shops and restaurants.\nThe road and rail network is radial, centred on Paris, so produce travels quickly from the farms to the city.\nRouen is France's main grain-exporting port, so it links the basin's cereal surplus to overseas markets.\nLe Havre, at the mouth of the Seine, is France's largest container port and gives farm produce a further outlet to export markets.\nBrie, east of Paris, is a dairy area that supplies its cheese to the city's large market.\nChampagne is sold under a protected AOC name as a branded product, which adds value to the grapes grown on the slopes.\nFarm co-operatives and agribusiness firms buy, process and market the produce, so farmers have a ready outlet for their crops.",
