    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named region – India:\nIndia is a continental region in south Asia in which language and religion have strongly shaped development.\nProcess 1 – Language:\nIndia has 22 languages recognised in the Eighth Schedule, and Census 2011 found 121 languages each spoken by 10,000 or more people.\nIndo-Aryan languages such as Hindi, Bengali and Gujarati are spoken in the north, and Dravidian languages such as Tamil and Telugu in the south.\nThe States Reorganisation Act of 1956 redrew state boundaries along language lines, so most states are language regions.\nAndhra State, created in 1953 for Telugu speakers, was the first state formed for a language.\nHindi, the mother tongue of about 44% of Indians, is the official language of the Union, and the Hindi belt covers states such as Uttar Pradesh and Bihar.\nResistance to Hindi being imposed, especially in Tamil Nadu, led to the anti-Hindi agitation of 1965 and created tension between north and south.\nThe Official Languages Act of 1963 kept English in official use, so it acts as a link language between states.\nEnglish is an economic advantage that helped India develop IT and business-process outsourcing industries.\nProcess 2 – Religion:\nCensus 2011 recorded Hindus at about 80% of the population, Muslims at about 14%, Christians at about 2.3% and Sikhs at about 1.7%.\nReligions are regionally concentrated, with Sikhs in Punjab, Christians in Nagaland, Mizoram and Meghalaya, and Muslims in Jammu and Kashmir.\nPartition in 1947 divided British India into India and Muslim-majority Pakistan, displaced more than 10 million people and killed hundreds of thousands.\nIndia is constitutionally a secular state, but communal tension has persisted, as in the riots after the demolition of the Babri Masjid at Ayodhya in 1992.\nThe caste system linked to Hinduism still affects rural life, and the Constitution abolished untouchability in 1950 and reserved places for Scheduled Castes.\nReligion also shapes the economy, because pilgrimage tourism at Varanasi and the Kumbh Mela, and spending at Diwali, support local incomes.\nSacred cattle support dairying, and vegetarian diets increase demand for pulses and milk.",
