    "label": "11B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named example – São Paulo, Brazil:\nSão Paulo is a developing world city of about 11.5 million people, and about 21 million in its metropolitan region, according to Census 2022.\nTraffic congestion:\nRapid growth in the number of people and vehicles overloads a road network that cannot cope, so congestion is permanent.\nCar ownership has risen as the growing middle class can afford cars, while the road and metro network has lagged behind this growth.\nThe growth of the outer favelas has lengthened commutes, because many workers live on the edge of the city and make long journeys to jobs in the centre and industrial areas, which adds to peak-hour traffic.\nTrains and buses are overcrowded at peak times, and the metro, whose first line opened in 1974, has not kept pace with growth.\nJourneys take longer, which wastes time and reduces productivity.\nLorries carrying goods for the city's large industrial and commercial economy add to the traffic on the main roads.\nNarrow streets in the old centre were not built for the volume of modern traffic, so they jam easily.\nThe city introduced the rodízio in 1997, which bans cars from central streets at rush hours according to the last digit of the number plate.\nUrban sprawl:\nRural-to-urban migration, notably of nordestinos from the drought-prone North-East, and natural increase have fed the city's growth.\nMigrants with little money and no legal land settle in favelas at the urban edge on marginal land such as steep slopes and floodplains.\nThe built-up area has spread far beyond the original city, so the metropolitan region has about 21 million people.\nIndustry has spread into the ABC region, where car plants such as Volkswagen's at São Bernardo do Campo were built.\nBetter-off families move out to gated condominiums such as Alphaville on the edge of the metropolitan region, which spreads the city further.\nSprawling informal settlements lack piped water and sewers, which spreads disease.\nNew housing on the edge of the city takes over farmland and green space as the city spreads outwards.\nBuilding on floodplains and steep slopes at the edge removes vegetation, so heavy summer rain brings floods and landslides to the outer districts.\nFurther example – Kolkata, India:\nIn Kolkata, planned townships such as Salt Lake City and New Town have been built beyond the crowded core to take growth.",
