    "label": "12B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named city – Dublin:\nDublin is a large European capital city with clearly separated zones of land use.\nLand-use zone 1 – Central business district:\nDublin's CBD lies on both sides of the River Liffey and has the highest land values in the city because accessibility is greatest.\nTall buildings with several floors of offices or shops make the most of the expensive sites.\nDepartment stores and chain stores line the pedestrianised shopping streets of Grafton Street and Henry Street.\nBanks, offices and company head offices cluster together in the centre.\nO'Connell Street is the main street, and government and cultural buildings are located centrally where they are most accessible.\nFew people live in the CBD, so it is crowded during the working day and quiet at night.\nLand-use zone 2 – Zone in transition:\nThe inner city around the north inner city and the Docklands is a zone in transition of old housing, warehouses and small industry.\nDecay and renewal exist side by side here, with derelict sites close to new apartments and offices.\nOld port and industrial land has been regenerated, for example at Grand Canal Dock where Google has its European headquarters.\nIndustry such as Jacob's biscuit factory moved out to the edge of the city because inner-city sites lacked space and land was cheaper on the edge.\nLand-use zone 3 – Suburbs:\nThe suburbs are lower-density areas of mainly semi-detached housing, built later and further from the centre.\nLand values fall with distance from the centre, so there is space for gardens and large developments.\nThe western new towns of Tallaght, Clondalkin, Lucan and Blanchardstown were planned after the Wright Report of 1967.\nSuburban shopping centres such as Dundrum Town Centre and Blanchardstown Centre, and business parks beside the M50, serve the outer city.",
