    "label": "18",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Human activities alter biomes by removing, replacing or polluting natural vegetation, and the felling of tropical rainforests, intensive agriculture and industrial development each do so in a different way.\nAspect 1 – Felling of tropical rainforests in the Amazon:\nSatellite records show that clearance in the Brazilian Amazon peaked at about 27,800 km² in 2004, and close to a fifth of the original forest has been cleared.\nCattle ranching is the largest single cause and accounts for most of the cleared land.\nSoya farming spread north from Mato Grosso, and much of the soya is exported as animal feed to Europe and China.\nRoad building such as the Trans-Amazonian Highway, begun in the early 1970s, opened the forest to settlers, and BR-364 into Rondônia produced a fishbone pattern of clearings.\nMining at Carajás in Pará, one of the world's largest iron-ore mines, and dams such as Belo Monte on the Xingu River cleared and flooded forest.\nClearance destroys habitat and species, many of them endemic, and fragments the remaining forest so that its edges become drier and hotter.\nThe nutrient cycle is broken because cleared latosols lose their fertility within a few years, and soil erosion and a hard laterite crust follow.\nLess evapotranspiration means less rainfall downwind and more frequent fires, and burning and decay release stored carbon as carbon dioxide.\nIndigenous peoples such as the Yanomami lose land and suffer disease and violence, and illegal gold mining led to a public health emergency in Yanomami territory in 2023.\nAspect 2 – Intensive agricultural practices:\nIntensive farming uses high inputs of capital, machinery, fertiliser, pesticides and water to get maximum output per hectare, and it creates monocultures and larger fields.\nAfter EU milk quotas ended on 1 April 2015, Irish dairy herds expanded.\nHedgerows were removed, wetlands and callows were drained and species-rich meadows were reseeded with ryegrass.\nMechanised silage cutting destroys the nests of ground-nesting birds such as the corncrake, which has declined to a small population in north-west Donegal, west Mayo and the Shannon Callows.\nHeavy use of fertiliser and slurry raises nitrate levels in rivers and causes eutrophication of lakes.\nWild bees and farmland birds have declined, and agriculture produces over a third of Ireland's greenhouse gas emissions.\nIn Europe the Common Agricultural Policy, begun in 1962, used guaranteed prices that encouraged farmers in East Anglia to remove hedgerows and drain wetlands.\nAt the Aral Sea, on the border of Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan, Soviet planners diverted the Amu Darya and Syr Darya from the 1960s to irrigate cotton and rice, and the sea shrank to a fraction of its former size.\nSalinity rose and the fishing industry collapsed at ports such as Moynaq, and dust storms now carry salt and pesticide residues from the exposed seabed onto farmland and people.\nAspect 3 – Industrial development:\nSulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from coal- and oil-burning power stations and industry form acids that are carried downwind across national borders as acid rain.\nAcid rain acidified thousands of lakes in southern Norway and Sweden from the 1960s to the 1980s, killing fish and releasing aluminium into the water.\nIn the Black Triangle where Germany, Poland and the Czech Republic meet, lignite-burning power stations killed large areas of spruce forest in the Ore Mountains in the 1970s and 1980s, a process known as Waldsterben.\nResponses included the 1979 Convention on Long-Range Transboundary Air Pollution and flue-gas desulphurisation, and European sulphur dioxide emissions have since fallen by well over half.\nIn Ireland Bord na Móna, set up in 1946, harvested milled peat on a large scale from the Midland raised bogs for power stations.\nPeatlands cover about a fifth of Ireland, and harvesting ended in 2021 after the Shannonbridge and Lanesborough power stations closed in December 2020.\nCutaway bogs are being rehabilitated, as at Lough Boora Discovery Park in County Offaly, which has become wetlands, woodland and amenity land.\nThe Athabasca oil sands in Alberta, Canada, are mined by open-pit methods that strip away boreal forest and muskeg and leave large tailings ponds of polluted water.\nRepeated oil spills in the Niger Delta in Nigeria have polluted creeks, mangrove forest and farmland, and a 2011 UNEP study of Ogoniland found that a clean-up could take 25 to 30 years.",
