    "label": "19",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Conflict arises when state boundaries and governments cut across, deny or divide cultural groups, as the cases of the Kurds, Spain and Northern Ireland show.\nAspect 1 – The Kurds in Turkey, Iraq and Syria:\nThe Kurds number 30 million or more and are the largest ethnic group without their own state.\nMost live in a mountainous region, Kurdistan, which is divided between Turkey, Iran, Iraq and Syria.\nThe Treaty of Sèvres in 1920 provided for a possible Kurdish state, but the Treaty of Lausanne in 1923 replaced it and drew the borders of modern Turkey with none.\nTurkey long denied a separate Kurdish identity and banned Kurdish in public life until 1991 and in broadcasting until the early 2000s.\nThe PKK, founded in 1978, began an armed campaign in 1984, and the conflict cost about 40,000 lives.\nOn 16 March 1988 Saddam Hussein's forces attacked the Kurdish town of Halabja with chemical weapons, killing about 5,000 people.\nIraq's 2005 constitution recognised the Kurdistan Region as autonomous, with its own government and parliament in Erbil.\nAbout 93% voted for independence in a referendum on 25 September 2017, but Iraq's government rejected it and Iraqi forces retook Kirkuk.\nThe PKK announced its dissolution in May 2025.\nAspect 2 – Basques and Catalans in Spain:\nBasques and Catalans have their own languages, autonomy statutes and strong regional identities within Spain.\nBasque and Catalan were suppressed under Franco from 1939 to 1975.\nSpain's 1978 constitution created 17 autonomous communities, and the Basque Autonomous Community received the Statute of Gernika in 1979.\nETA, founded in 1959, killed over 800 people in its campaign for an independent Basque state.\nETA declared a definitive end to violence in 2011 and dissolved in 2018.\nIn 2010 Spain's Constitutional Court struck down parts of Catalonia's 2006 autonomy statute, including its description of Catalonia as a nation, which fuelled the independence movement.\nOn 1 October 2017 Catalonia held an independence referendum that Spain's courts had declared illegal, and police tried to stop the voting.\nAbout 90% of those voting backed independence, on a turnout of about 43%.\nSpain imposed direct rule using Article 155 of its constitution, and the regional president Carles Puigdemont fled to Belgium.\nNine Catalan leaders were jailed in 2019 and pardoned in 2021.\nAspect 3 – Northern Ireland:\nThe Government of Ireland Act 1920 created two administrations on the island, and Northern Ireland was established in 1921.\nNorthern Ireland consists of six of Ulster's nine counties, while Cavan, Donegal and Monaghan went to the Irish Free State in 1922.\nThe border is about 499 km long and follows old county boundaries rather than physical features, so it cuts through communities and farms.\nThe state was created around a unionist majority with a large nationalist minority.\nDiscrimination against nationalists fuelled the civil rights movement from 1968.\nThe Troubles, from the late 1960s to 1998, killed over 3,500 people.\nThe Good Friday Agreement was signed on 10 April 1998, and referendums on 22 May 1998 returned about 71% Yes in Northern Ireland and about 94% Yes in the Republic.\nIt set up a power-sharing Assembly and Executive, a North/South Ministerial Council and the consent principle, but it did not move the border.\nBrexit challenged the Agreement, as Northern Ireland voted about 56% Remain in the 2016 referendum, and the Windsor Framework of 2023 keeps the land border free of checks.",
