    "label": "4B(ii)",
    "marks": 27,
    "model": "Named region – Cork Harbour:\nThe Cork Harbour region on the south coast of Ireland has developed as a centre for pharmaceutical manufacturing.\nAdvantage 1 – Skilled labour:\nUniversity College Cork and Munster Technological University supply the scientists and engineers that pharmaceutical plants need.\nA large labour force lives in and around Cork city, so firms can recruit technicians and operators locally.\nAdvantage 2 – Transport:\nThe Port of Cork has deep-water berths at Ringaskiddy, which suit the import of raw materials and the export of finished pharmaceutical goods.\nPharmaceuticals are light and valuable, so they can be flown out quickly through nearby Cork Airport.\nAdvantage 3 – Sites and services:\nLarge flat sites around the harbour are zoned for industry, which gives plants room to build and expand.\nA water supply and waste treatment facilities are available on these sites, and chemical manufacture needs both in large quantities.\nAdvantage 4 – Clustering:\nPfizer, Janssen and Novartis at Ringaskiddy and Eli Lilly at Kinsale form a cluster that shares skilled workers, suppliers and research links.\nOnce a cluster exists, new firms are attracted to join it, which reinforces the advantage of the region.\nAdvantage 5 – Government support:\nIDA Ireland offers grants and serviced sites to attract pharmaceutical firms to the region.\nLow corporation tax, at 12.5% from 2003, makes manufacturing in the region very profitable for multinational firms.\nMembership of the EU gives plants in the region tariff-free access to the single market.",
