    "marks": 27,
    "model": "Location – Leixlip, Co. Kildare:\nIntel is located at Leixlip in Co. Kildare, to the west of Dublin.\nReason 1 – Skilled labour:\nMaynooth University is nearby, and Trinity College Dublin and University College Dublin are in the city, so Intel can recruit the graduates that chip making needs.\nThe Greater Dublin Area has a large labour pool for the plant to draw on.\nReason 2 – Transport:\nLeixlip lies on the M4 motorway, which gives fast road access to Dublin.\nDublin Airport is close by, and microchips are small, light and valuable, so they can be flown quickly to customers and assembly plants.\nReason 3 – Site and services:\nIntel built on a large flat greenfield site, which gave room for several fabs and for later expansion.\nFabs use huge amounts of water and electricity, and Leixlip had a supply of both.\nReason 4 – Government support:\nIDA Ireland offered grants and support to attract the investment.\nLow corporation tax, at 12.5% from 2003, increases the profits that Intel can keep.\nReason 5 – Market access:\nIreland's membership of the EU gives Intel access to the single market.\nIreland is English-speaking, which suits a company with its headquarters in California.",
