    "label": "8C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Financial services – Ireland:\nIreland is a developed economy in which financial services have grown into a major high-value tertiary activity.\nDevelopment – Financial services and the IFSC:\nFinancial services are a highly footloose activity that includes banking, insurance, fund administration, asset management and aircraft leasing.\nThe International Financial Services Centre was set up in 1987 by the Haughey government on the derelict Custom House Docks on the north bank of the Liffey.\nApproved firms paid a low corporation tax rate of 10%, later replaced by the 12.5% national rate, which attracted firms from abroad.\nAn educated, English-speaking workforce suited the work of fund administration and banking.\nMembership of the EU gave firms based in Ireland access to the wider European market.\nIreland's time zone between the USA and Asia enabled firms to deal with both markets in one working day.\nThe government also wanted to regenerate a declining dock area, so it offered incentives to firms to locate there.\nThe IFSC became one of the world's largest centres for investment funds, and Irish-domiciled funds held over €3 trillion in assets in the 2020s.\nAircraft leasing is a second specialism, and Ireland manages about half of the world's leased commercial aircraft.\nThe IFSC provides high-value jobs and helped regenerate the north docklands, which are served by the Luas Red Line from 2004 and have new housing nearby.\nThe 2008 banking crisis damaged confidence in Irish finance, and the Central Bank of Ireland now regulates the sector closely.\nAfter the 2016 Brexit vote, Bank of America and Barclays moved their main EU operations to Dublin, which strengthened the centre further.",
