    "label": "9B(ii)",
    "marks": 27,
    "model": "Chosen site – G 259 043 near Bellass, west of Foxford:\nI would locate the factory at G 259 043, on the edge of the built-up area at Bellass, just west of Foxford.\nReason 1 – Flat land:\nThe land around G 259 043 is low and level, with spot heights of only 18 and 22 metres and no closely packed contour lines.\nFlat ground is cheap to build on because it needs little levelling, and it gives room for a large single-storey factory.\nThere is open land around the site for car parking, lorry turning and future expansion.\nReason 2 – Road transport:\nThe site lies on the R318 regional road, which leads east into Foxford.\nAt Foxford the R318 meets the N26 national secondary road at G 268 042, which carries goods to larger towns and markets.\nGood road access keeps down the cost of delivering raw materials and moving the finished products.\nReason 3 – Railway:\nA railway line runs about 400 metres west of the site at G 255 043, which offers a link for heavy freight if it is needed.\nReason 4 – Labour and services:\nFoxford at G 270 040 has schools, a post office and a visitor centre, so there is a settled population to supply workers.\nHouses at Bellass and in Foxford are close enough for workers to walk, cycle or drive to the factory in minutes.\nReason 5 – Water supply:\nThe River Moy flows beside Foxford, less than a kilometre east of the site, which provides water for the factory.\nLough Cullin lies to the south-west, which is a further source of water.\nReason 6 – Planning:\nThe site is beside an existing built-up area, so the factory would not intrude on open countryside or scenic uplands.",
