    "label": "11B(ii)",
    "marks": 15,
    "model": "Example – Mumbai, India:\nMumbai has grown to a megacity of about 18.4 million people in Census 2011.\nPush factors from rural areas:\nMigrants from poorer rural states, such as Bihar and Uttar Pradesh, leave because small subdivided farms cannot support a family.\nDrought, debt and landlessness leave rural families poor, so they look for work elsewhere.\nFew jobs and services in the villages push people towards the city.\nPull factors of Mumbai:\nMumbai is India's financial capital, with the Reserve Bank of India and the Bombay Stock Exchange, so it offers many jobs.\nJobs in construction, factories and the informal sector pay more than farm work, so migrants expect a higher income.\nIts deep sheltered natural harbour made Mumbai a major port, which has drawn trade and industry for generations.\nNatural increase:\nMigrants are mostly young adults, so the birth rate in the city is high.\nBetter health care has lowered the death rate, so the population grows even faster.",
