    "label": "11C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Reason 1 – Bridging point on the River Moy:\nThe map shows the River Moy flowing past the western side of the town with a crossing in the town centre at about G 270 040.\nA river is an obstacle to movement, so a place where it can be crossed attracts travellers and trade.\nA bridge gave a safe, permanent crossing that was easier to use than a ford during winter floods.\nThe settlement grew where traffic had to stop and cross, and traders and services located there to serve those travellers.\nThe river meanders west and south of the town, so crossing points were few, which made Foxford's crossing more valuable.\nReason 2 – Route focus where several roads meet:\nThe N26 enters the town from the north at about G 262 060 and leaves to the east at about G 290 048, while the R318 approaches from the west at about G 255 042 and the N58 runs south from the town at about G 272 985.\nRoads converge on the crossing point from several directions, so the town became a natural meeting place for trade.\nThe town became a centre where people from the surrounding area came to buy, sell and use services.\nGood routes made it easy to bring goods in and send produce out, which encouraged shops, markets and businesses to locate there.\nA railway line runs north to south at about G 253 040 a short distance west of the town, which shows how important the transport links to the town were.\nReason 3 – Centre of a fertile farming area:\nThe town lies in a low, flat area at about 20 m to 40 m above sea level, surrounded by farmsteads, field boundaries and small settlements such as Bellass at G 262 046, Irishtown at G 272 039 and Belgarrow at G 283 051.\nLow-lying land beside the River Moy has fertile soils suited to grassland farming, so many farmers lived in the area and needed a market town.\nThe town supplied goods and services to these farmers and took in their produce.\nThe River Moy at the town provided water, fish and a source of power for early industry.",
