    "model": "Urban area – Dublin:\nDublin has been a settlement for over a thousand years, and its functions have changed several times.\nEarly function – Viking port and Norman centre:\nDublin began as a Viking longphort in 841 on the Liffey near its mouth, so its first function was as a sheltered port and trading base.\nAfter the Normans took the town it became the centre of English administration, with Dublin Castle, so government and defence became important functions.\nEighteenth century – Capital and residential city:\nIn the 18th century Dublin was the capital of Ireland under British rule, so it had political and administrative functions, and the Irish Parliament met at College Green.\nThe Wide Streets Commissioners, set up in 1757, laid out wide streets, and wealthy residents built Georgian squares such as Merrion Square, which gave the city a residential function.\nNineteenth century – Industry and transport:\nGuinness, founded at St James's Gate in 1759, grew into a major brewing industry and employer, which gave Dublin an industrial function.\nThe Dublin–Kingstown railway opened in 1834, and later lines made the city a transport hub that linked its port to the rest of Ireland.\nTwentieth century – State capital and suburban city:\nAfter independence in 1922 Dublin became the capital of the new Irish state, so national government became its main administrative function.\nThe Wright plan of 1967 led to new suburban towns at Tallaght, Clondalkin, Lucan and Blanchardstown, so Dublin gained a large residential function as its population spread outwards.\nModern function – Services and global business:\nManufacturing declined in the inner city and was replaced by office-based services, which now employ most workers.\nMultinationals such as Google, with its European headquarters at Barrow Street, and Meta, with its international headquarters in Dublin, have made the city a technology hub.\nDublin Port is Ireland's busiest port and Dublin Airport handled over 30 million passengers in 2023, so transport remains a key function.\nTrinity College, University College Dublin, DCU and TU Dublin make Dublin the main educational centre in Ireland.",
