    "model": "Pattern 1 – Dispersed settlement:\nIndividual farmhouses are scattered across the land, each on its own farm, and this is the dominant rural pattern in Ireland.\nIrish farms are family-owned, so each family lives on its own holding.\nAfter the Land Acts, the Land Commission consolidated holdings and moved houses out of clusters onto individual farms.\nPattern 2 – Nucleated settlement:\nBuildings are grouped together in one place around a shared focus such as a bridging point, a crossroads or a church.\nFoxford in Co. Mayo is nucleated because it grew up at a bridging point on the River Moy where several routes converge.\nServices such as a church, school and shop gather in the cluster, which attracts more people to live nearby.\nPattern 3 – Linear settlement:\nHouses are strung out along a line such as a road, a coast, a river valley or a ridge.\nA road gives each plot access to services such as electricity, water mains and broadband, so people build along it.\nLinear settlement avoids marshy or steep land, so houses follow the dry, level ground beside the route.",
