  "legacy": false,
  "totalMarks": 80,
  "attemptRule": "1C: one of four listed forms of interaction (mining, extraction of building materials, oil/gas exploitation, geothermal energy)",
  "images": [
   "exam-images/geography/2022/higher-q101-1.webp"
  ],
  "parts": [
   {
    "label": "1A",
    "marks": 20,
    "model": "Outline: draw a rectangle 12 cm wide by 9 cm high, with all four sides drawn, and an arrow marking north at the top. Each 1 cm is 1 km, so the grid goes from easting 14 on the left to 26 on the right and northing 18 at the bottom to 27 at the top.\nRiver Suir: draw a blue line entering at the bottom left corner near S 142 180, running north up the west edge to about northing 21, bending east to reach Clonmel at about S 205 220, then flowing east through the town and leaving the right edge at about S 260 235. Label it River Suir.\nLand over 400 m: shade a small area in the south east around Long Hill at about S 230 199, with a second patch around Lachtnafrankee at about S 233 188. Label it Land over 400 m.\nWaterford Cycling Trails: draw a dashed red line starting at the bottom left at S 148 180, running north east through Caherbaun to end beside Clonmel at S 200 218. Label it Waterford Cycling Trails.\nConiferous plantation: in the north west block, west of easting 17 and north of northing 24, draw a small area shaded green with tree symbols. Label it Coniferous plantation.",
    "model_source": "sonnet-figure",
    "answerStatus": "written",
    "type": [
     "diagram"
    ],
    "diagramRelation": "draw-a-diagram",
    "offSpec": false,
    "notesRefs": [
     "geo-mapwork-s1"
    ]
   },
   {
    "label": "1B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Influence 1 – Folding by compression:\nTectonic activity has shaped the Irish landscape through folding, volcanic extrusion and igneous intrusion, all caused by plate movement.\nCompression where plates converged squeezed layers of rock into folds, forming anticlines and synclines.\nCaledonian folding produced the north-east to south-west trend of the mountains of Donegal, Mayo and Wicklow.\nArmorican folding produced the east to west ridges and valleys of Munster, with the Armorican front marking the northern limit of strong folding.\nResistant Old Red Sandstone survives on the anticlines as ridges such as Macgillycuddy's Reeks, the Galtee Mountains and the Knockmealdown Mountains.\nWeaker Carboniferous limestone lies in the synclines as valleys such as those of the Blackwater and the Lee, and the Blackwater turns sharply south at Cappoquin where it cuts across a ridge.\nThe coastal ends of the valleys in Cork and Kerry were drowned to form rias, including Bantry Bay, Dingle Bay and the Kenmare River.\nInfluence 2 – Volcanic activity (Antrim plateau):\nAbout 60 million years ago, linked to the opening of the North Atlantic, basic lava poured out of fissures in the crust in Co. Antrim.\nRepeated flows built up the Antrim plateau, a high plateau of basalt.\nAlong the Antrim coast the dark basalt lies on top of white chalk, which it protected from erosion.\nThe Giant's Causeway formed when a thick lava flow cooled and contracted, creating about 40,000 mainly six-sided basalt columns that sea erosion has exposed.\nSlemish, Co. Antrim, is a volcanic plug of resistant magma left standing as a steep-sided hill after the softer cone was eroded.\nInfluence 3 – Plutonic intrusion:\nThe Leinster batholith is the largest granite mass in Ireland, formed when magma cooled slowly underground about 400 million years ago during the Caledonian period.\nErosion removed the overlying slate and exposed the granite as the rounded Wicklow Mountains, with Lugnaquilla at 925 m, surrounded by a band of mica schist that was altered by the heat.\nThe Mourne Mountains in Co. Down are granite intrusions from the same period as the Antrim basalts, and Slieve Donard is their highest peak.\nGranite is resistant, so these intrusions stand out as uplands once the weaker rock around them has been eroded.",
    "model_source": "sonnet-thirty-fix",
    "answerStatus": "written",
    "type": [
     "long-answer"
    ],
    "diagramRelation": "none",
    "offSpec": false,
    "notesRefs": [
     "geo-volcanoes-earthquakes-s3",
     "geo-volcanoes-earthquakes-s5"
    ]
   },
   {
    "label": "1C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named example – Tara Mines, Navan, Co. Meath:\nTara Mines is an underground zinc and lead mine at Navan, Co. Meath, and it is Europe's largest zinc mine.\nThe mine has been in production since 1977 and is owned by the Swedish company Boliden.\nThe ore lies in Carboniferous limestone, where minerals were concentrated by hot fluids moving through the rock.\nInteraction 1 – Extraction of rock from the crust:\nHumans interact with the rock cycle by removing metal ores from the crust far faster than natural weathering and erosion could.\nPeople therefore act as an exogenic agent, with extraction as the human equivalent of erosion.\nExploration begins with geological surveys and test drilling before a mine is developed.\nUnderground mining reaches the deep ore through shafts and tunnels, as at Tara.\nThe ore is crushed and ground, and the zinc and lead are separated from the waste rock.\nExtraction exposes fresh rock to weathering and can cause subsidence above underground workings.\nInteraction 2 – Waste and deposition:\nWaste rock is stored in spoil heaps and fine waste called tailings is stored in tailings ponds, which is the human equivalent of deposition.\nThese deposits take up land and must be lined and capped to stop seepage into groundwater.\nAt Avoca, Co. Wicklow, centuries of copper mining left acid mine drainage that discolours the Avoca River and pollutes it with heavy metals.\nAt Aberfan in south Wales in 1966 a colliery spoil tip collapsed in a flow slide after heavy rain, killing 144 people.\nImpacts and management:\nMining brings jobs, exports and local spending, but it also brings dust, noise and heavy traffic for people living nearby.\nEnvironmental licensing and monitoring control waste handling and the quality of water leaving the site, and after Lisheen mine in Co. Tipperary closed in 2015 the site was rehabilitated.",
    "model_source": "sonnet-thirty",
    "answerStatus": "written",
    "type": [
     "long-answer"
    ],
    "diagramRelation": "none",
    "offSpec": false,
    "notesRefs": [
     "geo-rocks-weathering-s2"
    ]
   }
  ]
