    "label": "8B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named developing economy – India:\nIndia is a developing economy of South Asia that opened up to world trade and investment after 1991.\nIts Human Development Index is 0.644, ranked 134th of 193 countries, in the medium category.\nImpact 1 – Growth of services and IT outsourcing:\nAfter the 1991 crisis, reforms removed most licences, cut tariffs and welcomed foreign investment, which opened India to globalisation.\nIndia became a founding member of the World Trade Organisation in 1995, giving it access to world markets.\nServices now make up over half of Indian GDP.\nBengaluru is the base of the IT and outsourcing industry, with firms such as TCS and Infosys serving overseas clients.\nEnglish, inherited from British rule, is an asset because it lets Indian firms serve customers in English-speaking markets.\nMultinationals invest in India, encouraged by the Make in India programme of 2014.\nIndia is now among the five largest economies in the world by GDP.\nIndia is the world's largest recipient of remittances, which bring foreign money to families.\nImpact 2 – Inequality and informal work:\nGrowth from globalisation has been uneven, because about nine-tenths of Indian workers are in the informal sector without contracts, pensions or legal protection.\nAgriculture still employs about 55 per cent of workers and has low productivity, so most farmers gained little from the IT boom.\nInequality exists between states, as literacy was about 94 per cent in Kerala but about 62 per cent in Bihar in Census 2011.\nInequality also exists between rich and poor, so globalisation has widened the gap between urban professionals and rural workers.\nInfrastructure gaps and pollution remain serious problems, because growth has been faster than investment in roads, power and clean air.\nMany Indian firms and workers depend on overseas demand, so a downturn in the world economy hits Indian jobs.",
