    "model": "Accessibility – N24 national primary road:\nThe N24 national primary road runs across the upper middle of the map from S 141 246 to S 258 237, giving visitors direct road access to Clonmel from Waterford and Limerick.\nThe R665 regional road at S 194 217 and the R680 at S 257 228 link the town to the surrounding countryside, so tourists can reach attractions outside the town.\nRoads from several directions meet near the bridge over the River Suir at S 204 221, so Clonmel is an easy gateway for tourists exploring the area.\nThe Dismantled Railway near S 213 264 shows that there is no rail service on the map, so visitors depend on cars and buses, which limits tourism for those without cars.\nThe N24 passes through the north of the built-up area rather than the narrow streets of the old centre, so through traffic is kept away from the centre and tourists face less congestion.\nServices – Clonmel town centre:\nPost office symbols in the town centre near S 205 226 and S 198 224 show that visitors can find everyday services such as post and banking.\nThe town centre around S 205 225 has many streets, shops and schools such as those at S 195 225 and S 205 220, so visitors can buy food, souvenirs and supplies.\nThe hospital at S 188 225 and the fire station at S 195 227 give the safety services needed by visitors, especially those walking on the nearby hills.\nA large settlement such as Clonmel can support accommodation, restaurants and pubs, so visitors can stay overnight and spend more money locally.\nServices are concentrated in the town, so visitors staying in the countryside have fewer facilities available.\nAttractions – Lachtnafrankee and the Comeragh uplands:\nLachtnafrankee rises to 520 metres at S 236 183, and its uplands offer scenery for hill walking.\nThe Punchbowl at S 238 186 and a waterfall at S 236 189 are natural features that attract walkers and photographers.\nCarey's Castle at S 190 191 and the East Munster Way at S 205 206 are marked walking routes that draw hikers into the uplands.\nThe Waterford Cycling Trails near S 165 191 and the Kelly Legacy Cycle Route at S 254 224 attract cyclists.\nHeritage and leisure attractions include Greenan Castle at S 187 216, Clonmel Racecourse at S 217 238 and the Marlfield Wood walking trail near S 150 211.",
