    "label": "17",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "The tropical rainforest biome of the Amazon Basin has a hot, wet equatorial climate, and that climate controls the formation of its deep, infertile soils and its dense, layered vegetation.\nAspect 1 – Climate of the Amazon rainforest:\nThe Amazon Basin is the largest tropical rainforest, covering about 5.5 million km² with roughly 60% of it in Brazil.\nMean monthly temperatures are about 26 to 28 °C all year, with an annual range of only about 2 to 3 °C.\nThe range is small because the sun stays high in the sky and day length barely changes near the equator.\nThe sun is overhead at the equator twice a year, at the equinoxes in March and September.\nAnnual rainfall is over 2,000 mm and falls in every month, and Manaus receives about 2,300 mm.\nIntense morning heating warms the ground and the moist air above it, which rises.\nThe rising air cools and condenses into cumulonimbus clouds, which bring heavy afternoon thunderstorms almost daily.\nThe ITCZ, a low-pressure belt of rising air along the equator, drives this convectional rainfall.\nRelative humidity is often over 80%, and a large share of the Amazon's rain is recycled by evapotranspiration from the forest.\nAspect 2 – Impact of the climate on soils:\nThe heat and heavy rain produce a deep, red, infertile soil called a latosol through the process of laterisation.\nHigh temperatures and heavy rain cause intense chemical weathering to great depth, so latosols are often many metres deep.\nHeavy rain leaches silica and bases out of the soil.\nInsoluble iron and aluminium oxides are left behind, which gives the soil its deep red or yellow colour.\nLitter decomposes within weeks in the heat and humidity, so very little humus builds up.\nThe soil is therefore acidic, clay-rich and low in nutrients.\nMost nutrients are held in the living vegetation rather than in the soil, so the nutrient cycle is rapid and closed.\nShallow roots and mycorrhizal fungi take up nutrients from the litter almost at once and pass them to the trees.\nWhen the forest is cleared the soil loses its fertility within a few years and erodes under heavy rain.\nExposed iron-rich soil can bake into a hard brick-like laterite crust.\nAspect 3 – Impact of the climate on vegetation:\nConstant warmth and moisture produce a dense, evergreen, broadleaved forest with very high biodiversity arranged in layers.\nGrowth continues all year with no seasons, so the trees are evergreen and shed and replace their leaves all year.\nHundreds of tree species can grow in a single hectare of Amazon rainforest.\nEmergent trees such as the kapok and the Brazil nut tree rise above the canopy to about 45 to 60 m.\nThe canopy at about 25 to 40 m is a dense continuous layer that intercepts most of the sunlight and rain.\nOnly about 2% of the sunlight reaching the canopy arrives at the forest floor, so the shrub layer is sparse and understorey plants have large leaves.\nDrip tips and waxy leaves shed the heavy rain quickly.\nThe nutrient-poor soils mean roots are shallow, so tall trees such as the kapok have buttress roots for support.\nLianas climb the trees to reach the light, and epiphytes such as orchids and bromeliads grow on branches and take moisture from the air and rain.\nBromeliads hold pools of water in their leaf rosettes, which tree frogs use.",
