    "label": "18",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Desertification degrades soil through human pressure and drought, while conservation protects and restores soil, as the Sahel, the Dust Bowl and Irish and Icelandic examples show.\nAspect 1 – Causes of desertification in the Sahel:\nDesertification is land degradation in arid, semi-arid and dry sub-humid areas resulting from climatic variations and human activities.\nThe Sahel is the semi-arid belt south of the Sahara, running from Senegal and Mauritania through Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger and Chad to Sudan, with rainfall of roughly 200 to 600 mm.\nThe rain falls in one short summer wet season when the ITCZ moves north, and it is highly unreliable from year to year.\nDroughts in the 1970s and 1980s left bare soil that was eroded by wind and by the heavy rain of the wet season.\nPopulation growth led to more cattle, goats and sheep concentrated around wells and boreholes, which caused overgrazing.\nShorter fallow periods and cash cropping led to overcropping that exhausted the soils.\nCash crops such as groundnuts in Senegal and cotton took the best land and pushed food crops and herders onto marginal land.\nCutting trees and shrubs for fuelwood removes the cover that protects the soil.\nPoorly managed irrigation causes salinisation of the soil.\nHuman pressure leaves the soil bare and drought and storms then remove it, so the two causes act together.\nAspect 2 – Impact of desertification on soil:\nDesertification strips the soil of its fertility and structure, with serious effects on the people who depend on it.\nTopsoil and humus are lost, so fertility falls.\nSurface crusting makes rain run off instead of soaking in, so water retention is reduced.\nWind blows away fine particles and leaves sand and dust.\nSalt crusts form where irrigation is poorly managed, and in extreme cases the ground becomes bare and sterile.\nLake Chad has shrunk by about 90% since the 1960s through drought and the use of water for irrigation.\nFalling yields cause food shortages, famine and loss of livestock, and people migrate to cities and abroad.\nIn the Dust Bowl of the 1930s the natural grassland of the Great Plains in Oklahoma and Kansas was ploughed up for wheat.\nSevere drought then left bare dry soil that strong winds blew away in huge dust storms.\nMany farming families were forced to leave the Great Plains.\nAspect 3 – Impact of conservation on soil:\nConservation methods protect soil from erosion, rebuild its fertility and restore vegetation cover.\nIn Yatenga in Burkina Faso farmers such as Yacouba Sawadogo revived the traditional zaï technique.\nZaï are small pits dug in hard crusted soil and filled with manure or compost, which catch rainwater and concentrate nutrients around the seed.\nStone lines built along the contours slow runoff and trap soil.\nIn Niger farmer-managed natural regeneration has regreened about 5 million hectares since the 1980s.\nThe regrown trees shade the soil, cut wind speed and add leaf litter, and Faidherbia albida also adds nitrogen.\nThe Great Green Wall, launched by the African Union in 2007, aims to restore 100 million hectares of degraded land by 2030, but progress has been slow.\nAfter the Dust Bowl the US Soil Conservation Service was set up in 1935 and promoted shelter belts, contour ploughing and crop rotation.\nOn hill commonage in the west of Ireland, headage payments raised sheep numbers from the 1980s and overgrazing exposed and eroded blanket peat, sending silt into rivers.\nPayments were changed from per head to per area, and the REPS farm payments of 1994 paid farmers to farm in environmentally friendly ways.\nIn Iceland the Soil Conservation Service, founded in 1907, fences off eroding land to keep sheep out and sows lyme grass to stabilise drifting sand.",
