    "label": "20",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Political and physical boundaries shape cultural groups by uniting some people, splitting others and creating minorities, as partition in Ireland, the divided Kurds and the Pyrenees and colonial borders show.\nAspect 1 – Political boundary in Ireland:\nPartition drew a political border across the island of Ireland that created two states and two minorities.\nThe Government of Ireland Act 1920 created two administrations on the island.\nNorthern Ireland was established in May 1921 with six of Ulster's nine counties, and the Anglo-Irish Treaty of December 1921 created the Irish Free State in the other twenty-six counties.\nCavan, Donegal and Monaghan went to the Irish Free State in 1922.\nThe Boundary Commission of 1925 was not published and the border was left unchanged.\nThe border is about 499 km long and follows old county boundaries rather than physical features, so it cuts through communities and farms.\nPartition created two majorities and two minorities, including a large nationalist minority in Northern Ireland.\nDiscrimination against nationalists fuelled the civil rights movement from 1968, and the Troubles killed over 3,500 people.\nThe Good Friday Agreement of 10 April 1998 set up power-sharing and the consent principle, and people in Northern Ireland may be Irish, British or both.\nIn the Brexit referendum on 23 June 2016 Northern Ireland voted about 56% Remain, and the Windsor Framework of 2023 keeps the land border free of checks.\nAspect 2 – Boundaries dividing the Kurds:\nThe Kurds number 30 million or more and are the largest ethnic group without a state of their own.\nTheir mountainous homeland, Kurdistan, is divided between Turkey, Iran, Iraq and Syria.\nThe Treaty of Sèvres of 1920 provided for a possible Kurdish state.\nThe Treaty of Lausanne of 1923 replaced it and drew the borders of modern Turkey with no Kurdish state.\nTurkey long denied a separate Kurdish identity and banned Kurdish in public life until 1991 and in broadcasting until the early 2000s.\nThe PKK, founded in 1978, began an armed campaign in 1984 that cost about 40,000 lives.\nOn 16 March 1988 Saddam Hussein's forces attacked the Kurdish town of Halabja with chemical weapons and killed about 5,000 people.\nIraq's 2005 constitution recognised the Kurdistan Region as autonomous, with its own government in Erbil.\nOn 25 September 2017 about 93% voted for independence in a referendum that Iraq's government rejected, and Iraqi forces then retook Kirkuk.\nThe Kurds keep their identity through the Kurdish language and Newroz, the Kurdish New Year on 21 March.\nAspect 3 – Physical and colonial boundaries:\nPhysical boundaries often fit cultural divides but can also cut through a cultural group, and colonial boundaries ignored cultures altogether.\nThe Treaty of the Pyrenees of 1659 used the Pyrenees mountains as much of the France–Spain border.\nThe Basques live on both sides of the Pyrenees, so the physical boundary splits them between two states.\nBasque and Catalan were suppressed under Franco from 1939 to 1975.\nETA, founded in 1959, killed over 800 people in its campaign for an independent Basque state, declared an end to violence in 2011 and dissolved in 2018.\nSpain responded with autonomy under its 1978 constitution, and the Statute of Gernika of 1979 gave the Basque Autonomous Community its own taxes.\nThe Berlin Conference of 1884 to 1885 set the rules for the European division of Africa.\nMany African borders are straight lines that ignore ethnic territories, splitting some groups and joining rival ones.\nThese superimposed colonial borders are a cause of later conflict and secession.\nThe Somali people were split between Somalia, Ethiopia, Kenya and Djibouti by colonial borders.\nThe Maasai are divided between Kenya and Tanzania by the colonial border.",
