    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Cultural identity in Switzerland is defined by many diverse factors, including its mountain and political boundaries, languages, religions and migration, and together they make it a Willensnation, a nation formed by the will of its people.\nAspect 1 – Physical and political boundaries:\nPhysical geography and political history shaped Swiss identity and created a federal state in which diversity is managed locally.\nAbout 60% of Switzerland is in the Alps, and the mountains isolated valleys, which helped preserve local dialects and Romansh.\nThe Federal Charter of 1 August 1291 was an alliance of Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden, and 1 August is the Swiss national day.\nThe Congress of Vienna in 1815 recognised Switzerland's permanent neutrality and fixed its borders.\nThe Sonderbund War of November 1847 was a short civil war in which a league of Catholic conservative cantons was defeated.\nThe federal constitution of 1848 created the modern federal state, now of 26 cantons with wide powers over schools, police, taxes and churches.\nThe canton of Jura was created on 1 January 1979 after referendums in which the French-speaking, mainly Catholic north voted to leave the German-speaking, mainly Protestant canton of Bern.\nWillensnation means a nation formed by the will of its people rather than by a common language or ethnicity.\nAspect 2 – Language:\nSwitzerland has four national languages, so identity is based on shared institutions rather than one language.\nGerman is spoken by about 62% of the population, French by about 23%, Italian by about 8% and Romansh by about 0.5%.\nTicino is the only Italian-speaking canton, and Graubünden is trilingual in German, Romansh and Italian.\nThe territoriality principle means each canton has its own official language or languages, used in its schools and administration.\nRomansh became a national language by referendum in 1938.\nThe Röstigraben is the cultural divide between German- and French-speaking Switzerland, and it is often visible in referendum results.\nThe public broadcaster SRG SSR runs separate services in German, French, Italian and Romansh.\nNo language group wants to join its neighbouring country, because French, Italian and German speakers each identify with Switzerland.\nAspect 3 – Religion:\nThe Reformation created a religious geography that cuts across the language regions.\nUlrich Zwingli led the Reformation in Zurich from 1519, and John Calvin led it in Geneva from the 1530s and 1540s.\nZurich, Bern, Basel, Geneva, Vaud and Neuchâtel were historically Protestant.\nThe central cantons of Lucerne, Uri, Schwyz, Zug and Unterwalden, and Fribourg, Valais, Ticino and Jura, were historically Catholic.\nBoth German- and French-speaking Switzerland contain Protestant and Catholic cantons, so religion cuts across language and reduces conflict between language groups.\nIn 2022 about a third of people were Roman Catholic, about a fifth Reformed, about a third had no religion and about 6% were Muslim.\nOn 29 November 2009 a popular initiative banning the building of new minarets passed with about 57.5%.\nIn March 2021 a ban on face coverings in public passed with about 51%.\nAspect 4 – Migration:\nMigration has added new cultural groups and made immigration a central issue of Swiss identity.\nForeign nationals make up about a quarter of the permanent resident population, about 27% in 2023.\nThe largest foreign groups are Italians, Germans, Portuguese and French.\nAfter 1945 many Italian guest workers came on seasonal permits that restricted family reunification, a restriction abolished in 2002.\nLarge communities from Kosovo and the rest of former Yugoslavia and from Turkey have settled, and most Swiss Muslims have roots there.\nAlmost 400,000 cross-border commuters from France, Italy and Germany work in Switzerland, many in Geneva, Basel and Ticino.\nThe Schwarzenbach Initiative of 1970, which sought to cap the foreign population, was rejected by about 54%.\nThe initiative against mass immigration of 9 February 2014 passed with about 50.3%.",
