    "label": "5B",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Named region – India:\nIndia is a sub-continental region in South Asia, bounded by the Himalayas to the north, the Arabian Sea to the west and the Bay of Bengal to the east.\nActivity 1 – Factors influencing agriculture in India:\nTropical temperatures allow two or three crops a year to be grown.\nThe south-west monsoon, from June to September, supplies seasonal rain, so kharif crops such as rice are sown in June and July.\nThe monsoon is unreliable, bringing drought in some years and floods in others, which damages crops and lowers yields.\nFertile alluvium covers the Northern Plains and black soil covers the Deccan, which suit wheat and rice on the plains and cotton in Maharashtra and Gujarat.\nThe flat land of the plains eases cultivation and transport, and perennial Himalayan rivers supply water for irrigation.\nCanals in Punjab and tubewells supply irrigation water, so rabi crops such as wheat can be grown in the dry season.\nThe Green Revolution brought high-yielding varieties, fertilisers and irrigation, which raised food-grain output from about 51 million tonnes in 1950–51 to about 330 million tonnes in 2022–23.\nHoldings are very small and fragmented, with an average of about 1.1 hectares, so farmers lack capital and many remain in debt.\nActivity 2 – Reasons tourists are attracted to India:\nCulture and history attract tourists, for example the Taj Mahal at Agra, a marble tomb built by the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan that is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.\nIndia has varied landscapes, from the beaches of Goa and the backwaters of Kerala to the hill stations of Shimla and Darjeeling in the Himalayan foothills.\nWildlife attracts visitors, because Ranthambore is famous for tigers and Kaziranga for the one-horned rhinoceros.\nReligion and spiritual tourism attract pilgrims and visitors to Varanasi, the Golden Temple at Amritsar and the Kumbh Mela at Prayagraj, and yoga and Ayurveda draw others.\nThe warm, dry winter from October to March matches the European winter, so tourists escape the cold.\nLow costs make accommodation, food and travel cheap for visitors.\nThe Incredible India campaign, begun in 2002, and e-visas, introduced in 2014, promote India abroad and make entry easier.",
