    "label": "5C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Core region named – Paris Basin, France:\nExplanation – why it is a core region:\nParis is the capital of France, so government, finance and company head offices are concentrated there.\nThe region holds the largest concentration of population, wealth, industry and services in France.\nFertile limon soils on the Beauce and Brie plateaux make it the main arable region of France and supply food to the city.\nThe national transport network is focused on the region, with roads and railways radiating from Paris.\nThe Seine and its tributaries converge on Paris and their gentle gradients make them navigable, and the basin has a central position in the EU.\nThe large market draws industry and services, which attract migrants, so the region's growth reinforces itself.\nPeripheral region named – the Mezzogiorno, southern Italy:\nExplanation – why it is a peripheral region:\nThe Mezzogiorno is far from the EU's core markets and the industrial North of Italy, which raises the cost of moving goods.\nIts relief is mostly mountainous or hilly, with the Apennines forming its spine, which limits farming, transport and settlement.\nHot, dry summers cause drought and water shortage, which hold back farming on land that is not irrigated.\nIndustry is weak because of poor infrastructure, a small local market with low incomes and a lack of capital.\nOrganised crime deters investment through extortion payments, and emigration drains the South of its young people.\nThe Mezzogiorno produces only about 22% of Italy's GDP, its GDP per head is a little over half that of the Centre-North and its unemployment is two to three times the North's rate.",
