    "label": "8B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named example – Corrib gas dispute, Co. Mayo:\nThe Corrib gas dispute at Rossport in Co. Mayo was a conflict between the gas companies and the State on one side and local residents and campaigners on the other.\nThe economic case:\nThe Corrib gas field lies about 80 km off north-west Mayo and was discovered in 1996.\nSupporters argued that it would give energy security and a domestic gas supply to a country that imports most of its energy.\nIt would also bring revenue for the State and the companies, and construction jobs.\nLocal and environmental objections:\nThe original plan piped high-pressure, unprocessed gas onshore near homes at Rossport, which created a safety risk for residents.\nThe Shell to Sea campaign, from 2005, argued that the gas should be processed at sea rather than onshore.\nThe pipeline route crossed Sruwaddacon Bay, a protected estuary and Special Area of Conservation, so habitats were threatened.\nProtests and outcome:\nThe Rossport Five were jailed in June 2005 for refusing to obey a court order not to obstruct pipeline work, and they were released after 94 days.\nProtests were heavily policed for years, and the dispute divided the local community.\nThe onshore route was changed to keep the pipeline further from houses, and a tunnel was bored under Sruwaddacon Bay.\nGas first flowed in December 2015 and was processed at the Bellanaboy terminal, and Shell sold its share in the project in 2018.",
