    "model": "Named example – cattle rearing in the West:\nThe West Region of Galway, Mayo and Roscommon has cattle rearing as its main farming enterprise.\nSuckler herds rear calves, and young store cattle are sold through local marts to farmers in the east to be fattened.\nSheep graze the hill commonage of Connemara and the Mayo mountains.\nFactor 1 – Climate and soils:\nHigh Atlantic rainfall, over 1,000 mm on lowlands and over 2,000 mm on some mountains, keeps the ground waterlogged.\nBlanket bog and heavy gley soils cover much of Connemara and west Mayo, so most land is restricted to grazing.\nLeached acidic podzols on the uplands are poor for crops.\nMild winters, warmed by the North Atlantic Drift, mean grass grows well, but the effective grazing season is short because of wet conditions.\nTillage is limited to the better-drained brown earths on the limestone of east Galway and Roscommon.\nExposure to Atlantic gales limits the land that can be farmed.\nFactor 2 – Farm structure and economics:\nFarms are small and often fragmented, a legacy of the rundale system and of population pressure before the Famine.\nPart-time farming is common, with off-farm jobs supplementing low farm incomes.\nFarm income depends heavily on EU subsidies.\nCAP headage payments from the 1980s raised sheep numbers on the hill commonage, and overgrazing eroded the peat.\nThe Rural Environment Protection Scheme, from 1994, was a response to this damage.\nDistance from markets and ports adds to the cost of moving livestock.\nMany farmers are ageing with few young successors, so land is abandoned.\nSketch map of the region:\nA sketch map would show Galway, Mayo and Roscommon, Galway city, the Twelve Bens and Mweelrea uplands in the west, and the limestone lowland in the east.\nIt would also show the Shannon on the eastern boundary and Ballinasloe, site of the October Fair.",
