    "label": "7B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Multinational – Dell:\nDell is an American computer multinational with its headquarters in Round Rock, Texas.\nNamed locations – Limerick and Łódź:\nDell opened a plant at Raheen in Limerick in 1990.\nIn January 2009 Dell announced that manufacturing would move to Łódź in Poland, with about 1,900 jobs lost in Limerick.\nDell kept sales and service functions in Ireland.\nMobility – transport and communications:\nContainerisation and air freight cut transport costs, so computers can be made far from their markets.\nThe internet and video calls let Dell manage plants at a distance from its headquarters.\nMobility – production and trade:\nStandardised assembly can be done by low-skilled workers, so work can go wherever labour is cheap.\nRemoving trade barriers through the EU single market means goods made in Łódź can be sold across Europe.\nPoland joined the EU on 1 May 2004, so its plants lay inside the enlarged EU with lower costs.\nDell can compare countries and move capital quickly.\nMobility – company strategy:\nBranch plants open when a firm wants access to a trade bloc, lower costs or skilled labour, as Dell did in Limerick to serve the EU market.\nPlants close through rationalisation, relocation, outsourcing, automation or mergers, and the Limerick closure was relocation to a lower-cost country.\nBranch plants are vulnerable because decisions are made at a distant headquarters.\nConsequences for Ireland:\nInvestment brings rapid job creation, but jobs can leave just as quickly, as Limerick showed.\nCountries compete for investment by offering tax and grants.\nIreland responded by attracting firms earlier in new product cycles and by funding education and research, such as Science Foundation Ireland, set up in 2000.\nDiagram of the mobility of Dell:\nA diagram would show Dell's headquarters in Texas with arrows to Limerick in 1990 and from Limerick to Łódź in 2009.\nIt would also show the factors behind the move, cost, EU access and low-wage labour, and the sales and service jobs that stayed in Ireland.",
