    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Named region – The Mezzogiorno of southern Italy:\nThe Mezzogiorno is southern Italy and its islands, made up of eight regions including Campania, Puglia, Sicily and Sardinia.\n\nPart (i) – Two tourist attractions:\nPompeii, near Naples, is a Roman town preserved under volcanic ash, and it is a UNESCO World Heritage Site together with Herculaneum since 1997.\nThe Amalfi Coast in Campania is a scenic coastline that has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1997.\n\nPart (ii) – Positive impacts of tourism:\nTourism creates jobs in hotels, restaurants, transport and guiding in a region where industry is weak and many people have emigrated.\nTourists spend money in the region, which brings income and foreign earnings to the local economy.\nTourism uses resources the South already has, such as its sun, coast, volcanoes and heritage, so it can develop where industry is weak.\nAgriturismo, or farm-stay tourism, brings visitors into the countryside and gives farmers an extra source of income.\nUNESCO status for sites such as Pompeii, the sassi of Matera and the trulli of Alberobello draws visitors and attention to the need to conserve them.\nThe Costa Smeralda in north-east Sardinia was developed as a luxury resort from the 1960s by the Aga Khan, which brought investment and employment to the area.\nTourists buy local food, wine and crafts, which supports farmers and small producers.\n\nPart (iii) – Negative impacts of tourism:\nMost visitors come in the summer months, so tourist jobs are short-term and workers are unemployed in winter.\nAbusivismo, which is illegal building including on coasts, damages the landscape.\nHot, dry summers mean that water demand from hotels and pools peaks when water supply is lowest.\nLarge numbers of visitors cause crowding and wear at popular sites such as Pompeii and along the Amalfi Coast.\nMany tourism jobs are low-paid, so the benefits to local people are limited.\nMore visitors in summer put pressure on sewage and waste disposal along the coast, which can pollute beaches and the sea.\nRising prices in busy tourist towns make living there costly for local people.",
