    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named urban area – Mumbai, India:\nMumbai, on the west coast of India, has grown to about 18.4 million people in Greater Mumbai according to Census 2011.\nFactor 1 – Site and situation:\nMumbai was originally seven islands joined together by land reclamation, and it has a deep, sheltered natural harbour that favoured its growth as a port.\nIt became a British East India Company port, and cotton mills built there from the 19th century created jobs.\nFactor 2 – Economic role:\nMumbai is India's financial capital, housing the headquarters of the Reserve Bank of India and the Bombay Stock Exchange, founded in 1875.\nBanking, finance and the Bollywood film industry provide many formal and informal jobs that attract people to the city.\nInvestment concentrates in a major city like Mumbai, which reinforces its pull on migrants.\nFactor 3 – Migration:\nMigrants come from across India, including Bihar and Uttar Pradesh, drawn by jobs, higher wages and the chance of a better life.\nPush factors such as rural poverty, small farms, drought and crop failure drive people from the countryside towards the city.\nFactor 4 – Natural increase:\nThe urban population is young, with high birth rates and falling death rates, so natural increase adds to the growth.\nFactor 5 – Housing and planned growth:\nMigrants with little money settle in informal settlements such as Dharavi, one of Asia's largest slums, so the population keeps rising despite the housing shortage.\nNavi Mumbai, a planned twin city across the harbour begun in the 1970s by CIDCO, extends the urban area to take population and industry from the crowded core.",
