    "label": "6B",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Part (i) – Primary activity named:\nThe primary economic activity I have chosen in India is wheat farming.\nPart (ii) – Advantages for wheat farming:\nPunjab, in the Northern Plains of north-west India, is the heart of India's wheat-growing region.\nThe plains are flat, which makes ploughing, irrigation and the use of machinery easy.\nAlluvial soils deposited by Himalayan rivers are deep and fertile, so they give high wheat yields.\nPerennial Himalayan rivers feed canals and tubewells, which supply irrigation water in the dry winter growing season.\nWheat is a rabi crop that is sown in October and November, and the cool dry winter suits its growth and ripening.\nLight winter rain from western disturbances in the north-west adds to the water that wheat needs.\nThe Green Revolution brought high-yielding wheat varieties, fertilisers, pesticides and machinery to Punjab, which raised yields greatly.\nThe government sets minimum support prices and buys grain through the Food Corporation of India, so farmers are guaranteed a market and an income.\nIndia has a huge domestic market of over 1.4 billion people, so there is constant demand for wheat.\nMigrant workers from Bihar and Uttar Pradesh supply cheap labour at harvest time, which lowers the cost of production.\nPart (iii) – One problem faced by wheat farming:\nThe main problem is the falling water table in Punjab.\nTubewells draw groundwater to irrigate wheat and rice, and the water is pumped out faster than rainfall can replace it.\nFarmers must drill deeper wells and use more energy to pump the water, which raises their costs and increases debt.\nIf the aquifers run dry, yields and farm incomes will fall, and Punjab's role in feeding India will be at risk.\nOverused groundwater also becomes harder to reach for small farmers who cannot afford deeper wells.",
    "model_source": "sonnet-long-fix",
    "answerStatus": "written",
