    "model": "Region 1 – Paris Basin, France:\nThe Paris Basin is a low lowland in northern France centred on Paris and the River Seine, and its climate strongly shapes farming.\nIts climate is transitional, between cool oceanic conditions in the west and more continental conditions in the east.\nParis has an average January temperature of about 5°C and an average July temperature of about 20°C.\nAnnual rainfall of about 600 to 650 mm is spread fairly evenly through the year, which gives crops reliable moisture.\nWarm summers ripen cereals and grapes, and the long growing season gives crops time to develop.\nDry weather at harvest lets crops be gathered in good condition.\nThis climate supports wheat, barley and maize in the Beauce, south-west of Paris, which is known as the granary of France.\nChampagne lies near the northern limit of commercial vine growing, so its warm summers are essential for ripening the grapes grown on the chalk slopes.\nThe cooler, wetter oceanic west favours grassland, so Normandy has dairy farming that produces Camembert cheese.\nRegion 2 – Mezzogiorno, southern Italy:\nThe Mezzogiorno is the peripheral south of Italy, with a Mediterranean climate that strongly affects its primary activities.\nSummers are hot and dry because the subtropical high-pressure belt moves north over the region.\nLowland July temperatures average around 25°C or more.\nWinters are mild and wet, with most rain falling between October and March.\nAnnual rainfall is under 600 mm on parts of the Puglia lowlands and Sicily, so summer drought causes water shortage.\nFarmers respond with irrigation and drought-tolerant crops.\nPuglia is Italy's leading olive-oil region, and durum wheat for pasta is grown on the Tavoliere di Puglia around Foggia.\nHot, sunny summers and mild winters suit lemons on the Amalfi coast and blood oranges on the plain of Catania near Etna.\nThe sirocco, a hot, dry, dusty wind from the Sahara, adds to summer dryness, and intense autumn and winter downpours cause flash floods and soil erosion on bare slopes.",
