    "label": "5C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named urban area – Galway city:\nGalway is a city on the west coast of Ireland, at the mouth of the River Corrib, and it is the regional capital of the West.\nFactor 1 – Site and situation as a port:\nGalway grew because of its harbour site at the mouth of the River Corrib on Galway Bay.\nThe Norman de Burgo family founded the town in the 13th century.\nIt was governed by 14 merchant families, the Tribes of Galway, and a royal charter was granted in 1484.\nGalway traded wine with Spain and France through its port.\nThe Spanish Arch, built in 1584, survives as part of the old town walls.\nWhen Galway surrendered to Cromwellian forces in 1652, a long decline followed, which shows how much its growth depended on trade.\nFactor 2 – Industry and multinationals:\nIndustry, especially foreign investment, has driven the modern growth of Galway.\nDigital Equipment Corporation opened a plant in Galway in 1971, which brought hundreds of jobs until its manufacturing closed in 1993.\nGalway is now one of Europe's leading medical-technology clusters.\nBoston Scientific opened in Galway in 1994 and Medtronic is based at Parkmore.\nLow corporation tax, IDA grants and an educated workforce attract such multinationals to the city.\nClusters of similar firms attract more firms to the same place.\nThese jobs bring in-migrants seeking work, which increases the population of the city.\nFactor 3 – Education, culture and government policy:\nQueen's College Galway was founded in 1845 and is now the University of Galway, which brings students, staff and research to the city.\nGalway was European Capital of Culture in 2020, and the Galway Arts Festival and the Galway Races at Ballybrit attract visitors.\nThe National Spatial Strategy of 2002 named Galway a gateway, and the National Planning Framework of 2018 aims for at least 50% growth by 2040.",
