    "label": "8B(i)",
    "marks": 20,
    "model": "Reason 1 – Access to Bantry Harbour:\nA fish processing plant might locate here because it needs a harbour where the catch can be landed.\nMap evidence is the harbour and pier at Bantry at grid reference W 994 485, on the sheltered waters of Bantry Bay.\nFish are perishable, so boats must land the catch beside the plant so that it can be processed quickly.\nFish processing is a bulk-reducing industry, because heads and bones are removed, so it is cheaper to locate near the landing point than to move the whole catch inland.\nA pier and quay allow boats to unload directly at the plant, which cuts handling and transport costs.\nThe sheltered bay on the Atlantic coast gives access to fishing grounds and safe berthing for boats.\nReason 2 – Labour force and services in Bantry:\nA fish processing plant might locate here because Bantry town provides workers and services.\nMap evidence is the large built-up area of Bantry town centre at grid reference W 997 486, with schools, a hospital at W 998 480 and an Enterprise Centre at W 985 478.\nThe town's population is a ready source of labour for the factory.\nWorkers can live in the town and travel short distances to work, which keeps the labour reliable.\nServices such as schools and a hospital make the area attractive to workers and their families.\nThe Enterprise Centre shows that serviced premises are available, which lowers the start-up cost for a new plant.",
