  "year": 2024,
  "level": "higher",
  "paper": "Part Two",
  "questionNumber": 12,
  "legacy": false,
  "totalMarks": 80,
  "attemptRule": "",
  "images": [
   "exam-images/geography/2024/higher-q112-1.webp"
  ],
  "parts": [
   {
    "label": "12A",
    "marks": 20,
    "model": "Frame: Draw a landscape rectangle with four ruled sides, about 2 cm tall for every 3 cm wide, and put a north arrow in the top right corner.\nBantry Harbour: Shade the large bay in the upper left and centre of the frame, with its shore running left to right across the middle of the map. Label it Bantry Harbour inside the shaded area.\nRoad 1: Draw The Quay as a line along the south shore of the harbour, running east-west in the left centre. Label it The Quay.\nRoad 2: Draw Tower Street as a line leaving the east end of The Quay and running east and south-east towards the centre of the map. Label it Tower Street. The two roads must be joined.\nSchool: Mark a small square on the north shore of the harbour, in the upper centre just left of the middle. Label it School.\nCourt house: Mark a small square in the centre right, just south-east of the open space of Egerton Square and beside Marino Street. Label it Court House.",
    "model_source": "sonnet-figure",
    "answerStatus": "written",
    "type": [
     "diagram"
    ],
    "diagramRelation": "draw-a-diagram",
    "offSpec": false,
    "notesRefs": [
     "geo-mapwork-s1"
    ]
   },
   {
    "label": "12B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Recent trends in migration:\nAbout 1.3 million asylum applications were made in the EU in 2015, and a similar number in 2016, mainly from people fleeing war in Syria, Afghanistan and Iraq.\nOver 1 million people crossed the Mediterranean to Europe in 2015, most reaching Greek islands such as Lesbos from Turkey.\nThe Central Mediterranean route from Libya to Italy, with Lampedusa as a main landing point, continued to carry irregular arrivals.\nThe Ukrainian refugee movement from 2022 added to these pressures, and over 4 million people held temporary protection in the EU in 2024.\nPolicy impact 1 – Sharing the burden between states:\nThe Dublin system makes the state of first entry responsible for an asylum claim, which put most pressure on frontier states such as Italy and Greece and broke down in 2015.\nIn September 2015 the EU agreed to relocate 160,000 asylum seekers from Italy and Greece.\nHungary, Poland and the Czech Republic refused to take part, and in 2020 the EU Court of Justice ruled that they had broken EU law.\nThe Pact on Migration and Asylum, adopted in May 2024, brought mandatory solidarity, under which each state either takes in relocated asylum seekers or pays into a fund.\nPolicy impact 2 – Stronger border control:\nHungary, under Viktor Orbán, built a fence on its border with Serbia in 2015.\nAfter 2015 some states reintroduced temporary checks at internal borders within the Schengen area.\nThe EU-Turkey statement of March 2016 required irregular migrants reaching the Greek islands to be returned to Turkey, with €6 billion for refugees there, and arrivals in Greece fell sharply.\nDeals with Libya and other North African states aimed to control departures before migrants reached EU borders, which is known as externalisation.\nFrontex, the EU border and coast guard agency based in Warsaw, was strengthened.\nPolicy impact 3 – Humanitarian responses and legal routes:\nGermany under Chancellor Angela Merkel took in the largest number of asylum seekers in the EU in 2015 to 2016.\nThe Temporary Protection Directive was activated for the first time on 4 March 2022, giving Ukrainians the immediate right to live and work without an asylum claim.\nPolicy is pulled in two directions, because labour shortages and ageing populations create a need for workers and legal routes such as the EU Blue Card, while public opposition and anti-immigration parties push for tighter control.",
    "model_source": "sonnet-thirty",
    "answerStatus": "written",
    "type": [
     "long-answer"
    ],
    "diagramRelation": "none",
    "offSpec": false,
    "notesRefs": [
     "geo-population-migration-s4"
    ]
   },
   {
    "label": "12C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named example – Niger and the Sahel:\nOverpopulation means a population too large for its resources, technology and economy to support an acceptable standard of living, and Niger in the Sahel is a clear example.\nNiger has about 27 million people in a large, mostly desert country, with most of them living in the south where rainfall allows farming.\nCause 1 – Resource degradation:\nDesertification caused by overgrazing, overcultivation, fuelwood cutting and drought has reduced the land that can support people.\nSahel rainfall is only about 200 to 600 millimetres a year and is very unreliable, so crops often fail.\nLake Chad has shrunk by about 90% since the 1960s because of irrigation demand and drought.\nCause 2 – Society and culture:\nEarly marriage of girls is common, female literacy is low and large families carry prestige.\nNiger's total fertility rate is about 6 children per woman, with population growth of over 3% a year and a median age of about 15.\nCause 3 – Poverty and technology:\nIn a low-income country children are economic assets and there are no pensions, so families have many children, especially where infant mortality is high.\nMedical technology such as vaccines and clean water cut death rates much faster than birth rates fell, which produced rapid growth in stage 2 of the demographic transition.\nSubsistence farming without modern technology keeps carrying capacity low, and poverty limits the ability to develop resources or import food.\nEffect 1 – Food shortages and environmental damage:\nThe Sahel suffered famines in the late 1960s and 1970s and in the early to mid 1980s, with malnutrition widespread.\nPressure on the land causes soil erosion, deforestation, desertification and water shortages.\nEffect 2 – Poverty and pressure on services:\nUnemployment and underemployment are high because jobs cannot keep up with population growth, so incomes stay low.\nThere is heavy pressure on schools, hospitals, housing, water and sanitation.\nA high young dependency ratio means many children per worker, so savings go on feeding people rather than investment, which creates a poverty trap.\nEffect 3 – Migration and conflict:\nPeople leave rural areas for shanty towns in cities or emigrate to find work.\nPressure on scarce land and water can cause conflict.\nThe African Union's Great Green Wall, launched in 2007, aims to restore a belt of land across the Sahel.",
    "model_source": "sonnet-thirty",
    "answerStatus": "written",
    "type": [
     "long-answer"
    ],
    "diagramRelation": "none",
    "offSpec": false,
    "notesRefs": [
     "geo-population-migration-s2"
    ]
   }
  ]
