    "label": "13",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Sustainable development, which meets the needs of the present without compromising the needs of future generations, should be the priority for human and economic development, because growth that destroys the resources it depends on cannot last, as the use of resources in Ireland, economic growth in Costa Rica and fair trade for farmers in Ghana all show.\nAspect 1 – Protecting resources in Ireland:\nRenewable resources such as fish must be used no faster than they regenerate, which is the sustainable yield.\nOverfishing shows the danger, as the Grand Banks cod moratorium of 2 July 1992 cost about 30,000 jobs off Newfoundland.\nThe North Sea herring fishery was closed from 1977 to the early 1980s and recovered under quotas, which proves that sustainable management works.\nThe Common Fisheries Policy was reformed in 2013 to introduce maximum sustainable yield and a landing obligation that bans discards.\nThe Sea-Fisheries Protection Authority, set up in 2007, enforces these rules and protects the income of ports such as Killybegs, Co. Donegal, Ireland's largest fishing port.\nOn land, Bord na Móna was founded in 1946 to harvest peat for power, but the peat stations at Lanesborough and Shannonbridge closed in December 2020 and harvesting ended in January 2021.\nUnder its Brown to Green strategy, cutaway bogs are being rehabilitated and rewetted, and peatland is valued as a store of carbon.\nBellacorick, Co. Mayo, has wind farms on cutaway bog, which shows that power and jobs can continue from renewable energy.\nThe plastic bag levy of 2002 cut plastic bag use by over 90%, which shows that policy can change behaviour quickly.\nAspect 2 – Economic development in Costa Rica:\nIn the 1970s and 1980s Costa Rica cleared forests for cattle pasture, and by the late 1980s forest covered only about a fifth of the country.\nIts Payment for Environmental Services programme, started in 1997 and run by FONAFIFO, pays landowners to protect and replant forest.\nThe programme is paid for largely by a tax on fuel, so those who use resources help to fund conservation.\nForest cover has since risen to over half of the country.\nAbout a quarter of the land is in national parks and other protected areas.\nEcotourism is one of the largest sources of foreign income, so protecting nature creates jobs and earnings.\nSince 2015 almost all of the country's electricity has come from renewable sources, mainly hydroelectric, geothermal and wind.\nCosta Rica abolished its army in 1948 and spends instead on education and health, which builds human development.\nThe limits show that sustainability is a continuing task, because transport still runs mainly on imported oil and tourism puts pressure on protected areas.\nAspect 3 – Human development through fair trade:\nSmall farmers in developing countries receive a tiny share of the final price, because commodity prices are low and unstable and middlemen take a large share.\nFair trade is a trading partnership that seeks greater equity, and the Fairtrade Minimum Price is a floor that covers the cost of sustainable production.\nThe Fairtrade Premium is an extra sum paid to the co-operative, which members spend on projects they choose democratically.\nKuapa Kokoo, a Fairtrade cocoa farmers' co-operative in Ghana founded in 1993, shows this in practice.\nGhana is the world's second-largest cocoa producer, so many families depend on the crop.\nPremium spending has included wells for clean water, school buildings and mobile health clinics.\nKuapa Kokoo part-owns Divine Chocolate, set up in Britain in 1998, so farmers share in the profits of the brand and not just the sale of beans.\nThe co-operative encourages women to take leadership roles, which gives women a voice and an income.\nLabour standards ban forced and child labour, and environmental standards restrict pesticides and protect water and forests, so fair trade links human and environmental sustainability.\nIts limits are that it is only a tiny share of world trade and does not change trade rules such as tariff escalation.",
