    "label": "14",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Decisions and actions taken in one area, such as demand for beef, soya and fossil fuels in rich regions or the overuse of land and water, have effects in other areas, as deforestation, global warming and desertification show.\nAspect 1 – Deforestation in the Amazon:\nThe Amazon is the world's largest tropical rainforest, with about 60% of it in Brazil.\nClose to a fifth of the original forest has been cleared, and cattle ranching is the largest cause.\nSoya, much of it exported as animal feed to Europe and China, has spread north from Mato Grosso, so demand in other regions drives clearance.\nThe Trans-Amazonian Highway was begun in the early 1970s, and roads, mining and dams add to the clearance.\nThe carbon released by clearing and burning forest adds to global warming for the whole planet.\nReduced rainfall recycling by the forest affects farming beyond the forest.\nBiodiversity is lost and indigenous peoples are threatened.\nIn Indonesia and Malaysia, clearance of peat swamp forest for palm oil caused regional haze in 1997 and 2015 that affected Singapore and Malaysia.\nOther countries have responded, through the Amazon Soy Moratorium of 2006 agreed by traders and the Amazon Fund of 2008, with Norway as the largest donor.\nAspect 2 – Global warming:\nGreenhouse gases from burning fossil fuels in industrialised and emerging economies, from deforestation and from agriculture cause global warming.\nCarbon dioxide has risen from about 280 ppm before the Industrial Revolution to over 420 ppm by the mid-2020s.\nChina has been the largest CO2 emitter since the mid-2000s, but the USA emits far more per person, and rich countries bear historical responsibility for much of the build-up.\nCountries that emitted least suffer most, such as Bangladesh in the Ganges–Brahmaputra delta, which faces floods, cyclones and sea-level rise.\nPakistan's floods of 2022 covered about a third of the country, killed over 1,700 people and affected about 33 million, although Pakistan produces under 1% of global greenhouse gas emissions.\nSmall island states such as Tuvalu, Kiribati and the Maldives are threatened by sea-level rise, as global sea level rose about 20 cm between 1901 and 2018.\nDrought in the Sahel, rising food prices when harvests fail, and climate-driven migration spread the effects across regions.\nThe Paris Agreement of 2015 aims to keep warming well below 2 degrees Celsius, which needs co-operation between countries.\nThe Loss and Damage Fund agreed at COP27 in 2022 helps countries hit by climate damage that they did little to cause.\nAspect 3 – Desertification in the Sahel:\nDesertification is land degradation in drylands, not the desert advancing, and drylands cover about 41% of the earth's land and are home to about 2 billion people.\nThe Sahel is the semi-arid belt along the southern edge of the Sahara, where unreliable rainfall and drought, notably in the 1970s and 1980s, are physical causes.\nHuman causes include population growth, overgrazing, overcultivation and fuelwood collection.\nCash crops such as groundnuts in Senegal and cotton take the best land for export, so demand abroad pushes people onto poorer land.\nGlobal warming, linked to emissions in other regions, adds to drought in the Sahel.\nThe effects spread beyond the region, with famine, conflict over land and water, and migration to cities and to Europe.\nLake Chad has shrunk by about 90% since the 1960s, according to a widely quoted UN figure.\nThe African Union's Great Green Wall, begun in 2007, is a planned band about 8,000 km long from Senegal to Djibouti, aiming to restore 100 million hectares by 2030.\nFarmer-managed natural regeneration in Niger has regreened about 5 million hectares since the 1980s, which shows that local action can reverse degradation.",
