    "label": "15",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Views of development and underdevelopment have changed over time, and the simple division of the world into a developed North and a developing South should be challenged.\nAspect 1 – Changing definitions of development:\nDevelopment was first seen as economic growth, measured by GNP per head.\nIn the 1950s and 1960s Rostow's modernisation model claimed that every society passes from traditional society through take-off to high mass consumption.\nSouth Korea seemed to fit this model, as export-led industrialisation under Park Chung-hee from the 1960s turned a country devastated by the Korean War into one reclassified as developed by UNCTAD in 2021.\nModernisation was challenged as Eurocentric, because it assumed that the Western path was the only path and that poor countries were simply behind.\nDependency theorists such as Andre Gunder Frank argued in 1966 that poor countries were made poor by their unequal links with the rich core, which drained wealth through cheap raw material exports, debt and profit repatriation.\nCôte d'Ivoire and Ghana grow over half of the world's cocoa but export mostly beans rather than chocolate, which leaves them dependent on low and unstable primary-product prices.\nIn the 1970s the basic needs approach put food, water, shelter, health and education ahead of GNP growth.\nFrom 1990 the UNDP's Human Development Report and the HDI, built on the ideas of Amartya Sen, judged progress by people's health, education and choices rather than national output.\nSince the Brundtland Report of 1987 sustainable development has added the idea that growth must not harm future generations, and the 17 Sustainable Development Goals were adopted in 2015 with a target year of 2030.\nAspect 2 – The North–South divide challenged:\nThe Brandt Report of 1980 divided the world along a line at roughly 30°N into a rich, industrialised North and a poor South.\nThe gap remains large, as the World Inequality Report 2022 found that the richest 10% of people own about 76% of global wealth and the poorest half about 2%.\nThe model is now outdated because many countries of the South are high-income or industrial powers, such as Singapore, South Korea, Taiwan and Hong Kong.\nChina has been the world's second-largest economy since 2010, and India is among the five largest.\nThe BRIC term was coined in 2001 by Jim O'Neill of Goldman Sachs and grew into BRICS when South Africa joined in 2011, with more members joining from 2024.\nThe G20, set up in 1999, includes China, India, Brazil, South Africa, Indonesia, Mexico and Saudi Arabia, which gives the South a stronger voice in world affairs.\nHans Rosling argued in Factfulness that most of the world's people now live in middle-income countries, so a two-part division hides the real picture.\nPoverty and deprivation also exist within the North, so wealth does not follow the line neatly.\nIn 2016 the World Bank announced that it would stop dividing countries into developing and developed in its main data tables and would keep its four income groups instead.\nAspect 3 – Eurocentric views and images of Africa:\nEurocentric thinking sees the world from a European point of view and judges other societies by European standards, as if Europe's path were the norm.\nThe Mercator projection of 1569 places Europe at the top centre and makes Africa, which is about 14 times the area of Greenland, look similar in size to it, while the Peters projection of 1973 shows true areas.\nEnvironmental determinism claimed that temperate climates produced advanced peoples and tropical climates produced backward ones, but Singapore, a tropical country, is one of the richest in the world.\nThe 1984–85 Ethiopian famine, brought to the world by Michael Buerk's BBC report and by Live Aid in 1985, fixed an image of Africa as helpless and dependent and hid the political causes of civil war and government policy.\nSuch images lead to stereotypes, such as the belief that Africa is a country, and can make donors see themselves as saviours.\nDóchas, the Irish association of development NGOs, drew up a Code of Conduct on Images and Messages that requires dignified images and the full context.\nChimamanda Ngozi Adichie's 2009 talk The Danger of a Single Story argued that one story about a place distorts it.\nRwanda has rebuilt since the 1994 genocide, and since the 2013 election women have held over 60% of the seats in its lower house, the highest share in the world.\nKenya's M-Pesa, launched in 2007 by Safaricom, lets people without bank accounts send, receive and save money by text message, which shows African innovation.",
