    "label": "16",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Soil characteristics such as texture, structure, water content, pH and organic content control the processes that form a soil, and so they decide which type of soil develops.\nAspect 1 – Texture and structure:\nTexture is the proportion of sand, silt and clay in a soil, and it controls how quickly water drains through it.\nSandy soils are free-draining, so water moves quickly downwards and carries nutrients away by leaching.\nWhere leaching is intense on acidic, sandy granite parent material, as in the Wicklow Mountains, podzolisation produces a podzol.\nClay soils hold water and drain slowly, so they become waterlogged and favour gleying.\nGleys have developed on the heavy, impermeable boulder clay of the drumlin belt in Cavan, Monaghan and Leitrim.\nA loam, with roughly 40% sand, 40% silt and 20% clay, combines good drainage with good water and nutrient retention, so it supports fertile brown earths such as those of the Golden Vale.\nStructure is the way particles are grouped into peds, and the crumb structure of humus-rich topsoil opens pores for air and water, which helps a soil to develop.\nA platy or compacted structure hinders drainage and root growth, so water lies on the surface and the soil may gley.\nCompaction and poaching by livestock destroy peds, which reduces infiltration and increases runoff and the erosion of topsoil.\nAspect 2 – Water content and air:\nWater enters the soil as precipitation, and where precipitation exceeds evapotranspiration, as in Ireland, gravitational water moves down and leaches the soil.\nMild leaching produces brown earths, while intense leaching produces podzols.\nIn podzolisation, organic acids carry iron and aluminium down from the A/E horizon, leaving a bleached ash-grey layer above a dark reddish-brown B horizon.\nA thin iron pan may form in the B horizon, which blocks drainage and roots so that water lies above it and peat may form.\nWhere evaporation exceeds precipitation, capillary action raises water to the surface and leaves salts and lime there, as in hot deserts where salinisation occurs.\nIn the dry continental climate of the Ukrainian steppe, calcification moves calcium a short distance down and builds a thick black A horizon over lime nodules in the B horizon, forming a chernozem.\nAir fills the pores not occupied by water, and in well-aerated soils aerobic organisms decompose litter quickly into humus.\nIn poorly aerated, anaerobic soils decomposition is slow, so organic matter builds up as peat.\nAnaerobic conditions also reduce iron from its red ferric form to its grey or blue-grey ferrous form, so a gley is grey with orange mottles where air enters along roots.\nAspect 3 – pH and organic content:\npH measures acidity or alkalinity and decides which organisms can live in the soil.\nSlightly acidic to neutral soils on limestone-rich till support earthworms, which mix litter into mull humus and build brown earths with a crumb structure.\nAcid soils on granite or sandstone, or under conifers and heather, have few earthworms, so fibrous, poorly decomposed mor humus forms.\nMor humus releases organic acids into percolating water, and these drive podzolisation.\nHeavy rain leaches out bases and makes soils more acidic, which slows humification.\nOrganic content darkens the soil, binds particles into crumbs, holds water and nutrients and releases nutrients slowly.\nDense, deep grass roots supply large amounts of organic matter, so chernozems develop a topsoil of about 1 m of black, humus-rich soil.\nIn the tropical rainforest litter decays so fast that latosols have very little humus, and nearly all the nutrients are held in the living vegetation.\nThe clay-humus complex holds nutrient ions such as calcium, magnesium and potassium, so base-rich soils support the vegetation that supplies the litter that feeds the soil.",
