    "label": "12B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Urban area studied – Dublin:\nDublin is the capital city of Ireland, built on the River Liffey at the head of Dublin Bay.\nFunction 1 – Port and trade:\nDublin Port at the mouth of the Liffey is Ireland's largest port and handles most of the country's sea trade.\nFunction 2 – Administration:\nDublin is the seat of national government and the centre of the state's administration as the capital city.\nChange over time – Early Dublin to the 19th century:\nDublin began as a Viking longphort in 841 beside the Liffey near its mouth, so its first functions were defence and a trading base for ships.\nThe Normans took over and extended the Viking town and added a stone castle and walls, so Dublin gained a strong military and administrative function.\nIn the 18th century the Wide Streets Commissioners, set up in 1757, widened the streets and Georgian squares such as Merrion Square were built, giving Dublin a major residential function for the wealthy.\nIn the 19th century the railway, with the Dublin to Kingstown line opening in 1834, and industries such as Guinness's brewery at St James's Gate expanded the transport and industrial functions.\nChange over time – The 20th and 21st centuries:\nInner-city tenements were cleared and residents moved to suburbs and new towns such as Tallaght, Clondalkin, Lucan and Blanchardstown, planned after the Wright Report of 1967, so the residential function spread outwards.\nIndustry moved out of the inner city to industrial estates and business parks near the M50 because of lack of space to expand, cheaper land and road access for lorries, for example Jacob's moved from Bishop Street to Tallaght in the 1970s.\nMany Georgian houses around Merrion Square and Fitzwilliam Square changed from family homes to offices, so the centre became more commercial.\nSuburban shopping centres such as Blanchardstown Centre in 1996 and Dundrum Town Centre in 2005 were built beside major roads where car access is easy and land is cheaper, so retail trade moved away from the city centre.\nVacated docklands sites were redeveloped, with the International Financial Services Centre opening in 1987 and Google's European headquarters at Grand Canal Dock, giving the Docklands a financial and high-technology service function.",
