    "label": "12C",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Part (i) – Area with no settlement:\nThe upland area around Mullaghmesha, centred on W 091 514, has no settlement.\nPart (ii) – Why there is no settlement:\nMullaghmesha rises to a spot height of 494 m at W 091 514, which is far too high for farming or building.\nContour lines are packed closely together on the slopes to the south and south-east of the summit, where the 480 m and 450 m lines are labelled, showing slopes that are too steep for houses.\nThe land is mostly above 250 m, so it is exposed, cold and wet, which makes farming impossible and discourages settlers.\nNo roads or tracks reach the summit area, and the nearest road runs east to west across lower land to the south at about W 082 488, so access is very difficult.\nLough Agower at about 400 m, Coonanore Lough and the streams flowing off the slopes show wet, poorly drained ground that cannot support buildings.\nThe red dashed walking route called the Glanaclougha Loop crosses the area, which shows that the land is used for hill walking and not for living.\nHouses and farm buildings, shown as small black squares, appear only on the lower land around the edge of the upland and none appear on the high ground.\nPart (iii) – Area with high density of settlement:\nBantry town has a relatively high density of settlement at W 001 485.\nPart (iv) – Why there is a high density of settlement:\nBantry is a port on a sheltered inlet of Bantry Bay, with Bantry Harbour, piers and a quay marked along the shoreline, which attracted settlers for fishing and trade.\nThe land around the town is low and gently sloping, mostly below about 60 m with widely spaced contours, so it is easy to build on.\nMany roads converge on the town, including the N71 from the north along the coast and several yellow roads from the east, so Bantry is a route centre that attracts houses.\nThe grey built-up area is large and the buildings are tightly packed, with street names such as Town Lots, which shows a compact urban centre.\nServices are marked in the town, including a church, a post office, a fire station, a hospital, schools and a college, which draw people to live nearby.\nTourist facilities such as the tourist information symbol, Bantry House and the nearby Star-shaped Fort create jobs in tourism and attract more development.\nAn industrial estate is marked near Lahadane on the edge of the town, which provides employment so people settle nearby.",
