    "label": "12B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Variation 1 – Nucleated settlement at Oughterard:\nThe largest and densest concentration of settlement is the nucleated town of Oughterard at about M 122 428, which contrasts with the scattered houses elsewhere on the map.\nThe post office and tourist information symbols at M 122 429 show that services gather in the town.\nPeople from the surrounding area travel to these services, which attracts further houses to the town.\nOughterard grew here as a route focus where the N59 and several other roads meet, at a crossing of the Owenriff River beside Lough Corrib.\nThe quay at M 134 440 on the shore of Lough Corrib shows the lake's use for transport and fishing.\nVariation 2 – Linear settlement along the N59:\nHouses are strung out along the N59 and minor roads, forming linear settlement, for example around Glengowla at M 086 422.\nA similar ribbon of houses follows the N59 east of Oughterard through Gortrevagh at M 142 416 and Moyvoon at M 145 413.\nRoad frontage gives cheap sites with access to electricity and water mains, and farmers sold roadside sites.\nRoads and houses follow the valley floor, because linear settlement avoids marshy or steep land.\nVariation 3 – Dispersed settlement on lowland farmland:\nScattered individual houses lie on the low ground around Cregg at M 121 420, Billymore at M 127 418 and Maghera More at M 124 403.\nIrish farms are family owned, so each family lives on its own holding rather than in a village.\nThe fertile, well-drained lowland near Oughterard lets houses be built almost anywhere.\nVariation 4 – Empty uplands, forest and lakes:\nThere are almost no houses in the south-west around Knockwaumnamoe at M 055 403 and Shannadullaghaun at M 045 392, where contours are close together and land rises above 200 metres.\nSteep, exposed ground with poor soil is unsuitable for farming and building.\nThe forestry plantations at Newvillage at M 095 452 and Derradda at M 073 399 have no houses, because plantations are large areas of planted trees.\nThe water of Lough Corrib in the north-east around M 130 450 and Lettercraffroe Lough at M 057 376 takes up land that could hold houses.\nThe east near Oughterard is served by the N59 and a network of minor roads, whereas the west has few roads, so accessibility also explains why settlement thins out westwards.",
    "model_source": "sonnet-thirty",
    "answerStatus": "written",
    "type": [
     "long-answer"
    ],
    "diagramRelation": "refers-to-figure",
    "offSpec": false,
