    "label": "15",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Sustainable development is a model for the future because it balances economic growth with the protection of resources for future generations, as the concept, the example of Costa Rica and fair trade show.\nAspect 1 – The concept of sustainable development:\nSustainable development is development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs, as defined by the Brundtland Report of 1987.\nIt rests on three pillars, environmental, economic and social, which must be balanced against each other.\nThe Limits to Growth, a report for the Club of Rome in 1972, warned that unchecked population and economic growth would exhaust resources within about a century.\nThe Earth Summit in Rio de Janeiro in 1992 produced Agenda 21 and the UN Framework Convention on Climate Change.\nThe 17 Sustainable Development Goals adopted in September 2015 set a target year of 2030 and followed the eight Millennium Development Goals of 2000 to 2015.\nIt is a model for the future because resources are finite and growth that destroys the resources it depends on cannot last.\nThe poorest people suffer first when resources are degraded, so sustainability also promotes fairness.\nThe model is hard to apply because it is vague and open to greenwashing, where firms and governments claim sustainability without real change.\nPoorer countries argue that rich countries grew without limits and now ask them to slow down.\nAspect 2 – Resource use in Costa Rica and Ireland:\nSustainable resource use means using renewable resources no faster than they regenerate, which is the sustainable yield.\nIn the 1970s and 1980s Costa Rica cleared forest for cattle pasture, and by the late 1980s forest covered only about a fifth of the country.\nIts Payment for Environmental Services programme, started in 1997 and run by FONAFIFO, pays landowners to protect and replant forest.\nThe programme is paid for largely by a tax on fuel.\nForest cover has since risen to over half of the country, and about a quarter of the land is in national parks and other protected areas.\nEcotourism is now one of Costa Rica's largest sources of foreign income, which links the environmental and economic pillars.\nSince 2015 almost all of Costa Rica's electricity has come from renewable sources, mainly hydroelectric, geothermal and wind.\nIn Ireland Bord na Móna, founded in 1946, ended peat harvesting in January 2021 under its Brown to Green strategy and is rehabilitating cutaway bogs such as Lough Boora Discovery Park in Co. Offaly.\nThe Irish Climate Act 2021 requires a 51% cut in emissions by 2030 and net zero by 2050.\nAspect 3 – Fair trade and Kuapa Kokoo in Ghana:\nFair trade is a trading partnership based on dialogue, transparency and respect that seeks greater equity in international trade.\nIt addresses low and unstable commodity prices, which leave small farmers with a tiny share of the final price.\nThe Fairtrade Minimum Price is a floor that covers the cost of sustainable production, and when the market price is higher producers receive the market price.\nThe Fairtrade Premium is an extra sum paid to the co-operative and spent on projects chosen democratically by its members.\nKuapa Kokoo, founded in 1993, is a Fairtrade cocoa farmers' co-operative in Ghana, the world's second-largest cocoa producer.\nIt part-owns Divine Chocolate, set up in Britain in 1998, so farmers share in the profits of the brand and not just the beans they sell.\nPremium spending has paid for wells for clean water, school buildings and mobile health clinics.\nKuapa Kokoo encourages women to take leadership roles in its village societies.\nFair trade is only a tiny share of world trade and does not change trade rules such as subsidies and tariff escalation, so its impact depends on its scale.",
