    "label": "16",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Soil processes shape the depth, colour, structure, acidity and fertility of soil, as podzolisation, calcification and soil erosion show.\nAspect 1 – Podzolisation:\nPodzolisation is an intense form of leaching that forms the podzol, a strongly acidic and infertile soil.\nIt occurs in cool, wet climates where precipitation is well above evapotranspiration, on acid parent material such as granite or sandstone.\nConiferous forest or heather supplies acid mor humus, which releases organic acids into percolating water.\nThese acids combine with iron and aluminium and carry them, along with bases and humus, down the profile.\nThis leaves a bleached, ash-grey, sandy A/E horizon of quartz grains.\nIron, aluminium and humus are deposited in the B horizon, which turns dark reddish-brown.\nA thin, hard iron pan may form in the B horizon, which blocks drainage and root growth and can let peat form on top.\nThe result is a soil with sharply defined horizons and a pH of about 4 to 4.5, found on the granite of the Wicklow Mountains and under Sitka spruce plantations.\nBecause of these characteristics podzols are used only for rough grazing and forestry, and they can be improved by deep ploughing to break the iron pan and by heavy liming.\nAspect 2 – Calcification:\nCalcification is the process in dry continental climates by which calcium carbonate is moved a short distance and deposited in the B horizon, forming the fertile chernozem.\nIt occurs where evapotranspiration roughly equals or exceeds precipitation, with low rainfall of about 250 to 500 mm a year.\nChernozems lie under the natural grassland of the steppes of Ukraine and southern Russia and the Prairies of North America.\nRain penetrates only part of the way down the soil, so leaching is weak and bases are not lost.\nCalcium carbonate is dissolved and deposited in the B horizon as white nodules or a lime-rich layer.\nIn dry periods capillary action draws water and calcium back up towards the surface.\nDeep grass roots take up calcium and return it to the surface in litter, and the dying roots supply large amounts of organic matter.\nThe result is a neutral to slightly alkaline soil with a black, humus-rich A horizon commonly about 1 m deep and a crumb structure.\nThese characteristics make chernozems some of the world's main wheat and maize lands.\nAspect 3 – Soil erosion:\nSoil erosion is the removal of soil, especially topsoil, faster than it forms, and it changes soil characteristics by thinning the profile.\nNatural erosion is slow and balanced by soil formation, but accelerated erosion follows the removal of vegetation.\nErosion by water takes the forms of rain-splash, sheet wash, and rills and gullies.\nErosion by wind, called deflation, removes dry, loose, bare soil.\nErosion removes the humus-rich A horizon and its nutrients, which lowers fertility and exposes infertile subsoil.\nIt also worsens soil structure and reduces its water retention.\nIn the 1930s the Great Plains of the USA, where grassland had been ploughed for wheat, were hit by drought, and winds blew away the bare soil in the Dust Bowl and forced many farming families to leave.\nThe eroded material is redeposited elsewhere as alluvium or loess.\nContour ploughing, terracing and shelter belts of trees reduce erosion by trapping water and cutting wind speed, and the US Soil Conservation Service was set up in 1935.",
