    "model": "Part (i) – Location of the factory:\nI would locate the factory at grid reference M 103 427, on the north side of the N59 about 2 km west of Oughterard.\n\nPart (ii) – Reasons for choosing the location:\nThe site is on flat, low-lying land, shown by widely spaced contours and spot heights of about 33 to 37 metres, which gives room to build and expand.\nIt lies beside the N59 national primary road, which carries goods and workers east to Oughterard and Galway.\nOughterard is about 2 km to the east and supplies a labour force, along with the nearby settlements of Clare and Canrawer.\nOughterard has schools, a post office and shops, so workers and the firm have services close by.\nThe Owenriff River runs within about 500 m of the site, giving a supply of water for industrial processes.\nThe water tower beside the N59 east of the site shows that piped water is available in the area.\nThe surrounding land is gently sloping farmland, so construction costs are lower than on the steep, boggy hills to the south-west.\nFew houses stand on the site itself, so the factory would disturb fewer residents than one built inside the town.\nLocating on the edge of the town keeps lorries out of the narrow streets of Oughterard centre.\nGalway city, with its port and much larger market, is reached along the N59 to the east.\n\nPart (iii) – Disadvantage of the location:\nThe site is within about 500 m of the Owenriff River, which flows into Lough Corrib, so industrial waste or spills could pollute the river and lake.\nLough Corrib is a major scenic and angling lake, so pollution would damage fishing and tourism around Oughterard.\nSpilled chemicals or waste water would poison fish and other wildlife in the river and the lake.\nOughterard depends on anglers and tourists, so pollution would cost local people jobs and income.",
    "model_source": "sonnet-long-fix",
    "answerStatus": "written",
    "type": [
     "short-answer",
     "long-answer"
    ],
    "diagramRelation": "refers-to-figure",
