    "label": "12C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "(i) Urban area – Dublin:\nThe urban area studied is Dublin.\n(ii) Land-use 1 – Industry:\nIndustry is one land-use in Dublin, including the port, inner-city factories and suburban industrial estates.\nLand-use 2 – Retail and commercial:\nRetail and commercial activity is a second land-use, concentrated in the central business district around Grafton Street and Henry Street.\n(iii) Change in industry:\nOld inner-city industry such as brewing, biscuit-making and distilling closed or moved out of the centre.\nIndustry moved because the inner city had no space for expansion, while the edge offered cheaper land and better road access for lorries.\nJacob's biscuit factory moved from Bishop Street to Tallaght in the 1970s, and its old building now houses the National Archives.\nThe vacated sites became brownfield land that was redeveloped for other uses such as housing and offices.\nIndustry relocated to business parks near the M50 such as Grange Castle and Citywest.\nThe Guinness brewery is an exception, as it stayed at St James's Gate where Arthur Guinness took his lease in 1759.\nChange in retail and commercial use:\nSuburban shopping centres such as The Square in Tallaght in 1990, Blanchardstown Centre in 1996, Liffey Valley in 1998 and Dundrum Town Centre in 2005 were built beside major roads or the M50.\nThese centres have car access, free parking and cheaper land, so shoppers moved to the edge and city-centre units lost trade and became vacant.\nThe city centre responded with pedestrianisation and regeneration of central streets.\nOut-of-town office parks took some commercial activity away from the centre.\nRegeneration of the Docklands brought new commercial use, with Google's European headquarters at Grand Canal Dock in what is known as Silicon Docks.",
