    "model": "Region – India:\nIndia is a subcontinental region of about 3.3 million square kilometres.\nIt is bounded by the Himalayas to the north, the Arabian Sea to the west and the Bay of Bengal to the east.\nIts population is very unevenly distributed because of a mix of physical and human factors.\nDense area – the Ganges plain:\nThe Ganges plain is densely populated, with Uttar Pradesh and Bihar as major population centres.\nHimalayan rivers deposit fertile alluvium on the plain, which gives high crop yields.\nThe land is flat, so farming, roads and railways are easy to build.\nPerennial rivers fed by Himalayan snow melt supply water for irrigation all year.\nMonsoon rain from June to September feeds the rivers and waters the crops.\nFarming here supports many people per square kilometre, and rice and wheat are grown intensively.\nThe plain has been settled for thousands of years, so populations have built up over a long time.\nDense area – cities and coasts:\nMumbai, Delhi and Kolkata are densely populated cities.\nMumbai is on the west coast, Kolkata is on the Hooghly near the Bay of Bengal, and Delhi is on the Yamuna in the north.\nJobs, industry and services in these cities attract rural migrants.\nPoor, overcrowded farming areas push people out, so rural-to-urban migration adds to city densities.\nThe Malabar coast of Kerala is densely populated because heavy monsoon rain and laterite soils support crops such as rice and coconuts.\nSparse area – the Thar Desert:\nThe Thar Desert in Rajasthan is sparsely populated.\nRainfall is very low there, because the Arabian Sea monsoon passes parallel to the Aravalli Range and has no barrier to force it upwards.\nThe desert soils are sandy, low in humus and sometimes saline, so farming is difficult.\nOnly places reached by canals, such as along the Indira Gandhi Canal, can support farming and settlement.\nSparse area – the Himalayas and the Deccan:\nThe Himalayas are sparsely populated because they are cold, and temperatures fall with altitude.\nSteep slopes and thin, immature mountain soils limit farming in the Himalayas.\nThe Deccan Plateau lies in the rain shadow of the Western Ghats, so it is much drier than the coast.\nLower rainfall on the Deccan makes farming less productive, so its densities are lower than on the Ganges plain.\nSketch map locations:\nOn a sketch map the Ganges flows south-east from the Himalayas across the northern plain to its delta on the Bay of Bengal.\nThe densest area is shaded along this river, and the Thar Desert is shown as a sparse area in the north-west.",
