    "label": "7C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named MNC – Dell:\nDell is a United States computer multinational with its headquarters at Round Rock, Texas.\nDell operated branch plants abroad, so key decisions were made at its headquarters.\nLocation 1 – Limerick:\nDell opened at Raheen, Limerick, in 1990, attracted by IDA support and a skilled English-speaking workforce.\nA base in Ireland gave Dell tariff-free access to the EU single market.\nWhy modern activities are mobile:\nComputers are standardised products, so assembly can be done by low-skilled workers and can move easily.\nLower transport costs through air freight and containerisation let goods move cheaply from any plant.\nInstant communications let the headquarters manage plants at a distance.\nLocation 2 – Łódź, Poland:\nIn January 2009 Dell announced that manufacturing would move from Limerick to Łódź in Poland, with about 1,900 jobs lost in Limerick.\nPoland joined the EU on 1 May 2004, so Łódź offered tariff-free access to the EU market with lower costs.\nThe move was relocation and rationalisation to cut costs.\nDell kept sales and service functions in Ireland, so some jobs remained.\nConsequences for the host region:\nThe EU used the European Globalisation Adjustment Fund, about €14.8 million, to help about 2,400 former Dell and supplier workers in the Limerick area.\nBranch plants are vulnerable because decisions are made at a distant headquarters.\nCountries compete for investment with tax and grants, so firms can compare locations and move capital quickly.\nHost countries can invest in education and research so that jobs are harder to move.\nDiagram:\nA labelled diagram would show Round Rock in Texas at the centre with arrows to Limerick, opened in 1990, and Łódź, used for manufacturing from 2009.",
