    "label": "11B",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Impact of falling fertility on population structure:\nFalling fertility narrows the base of the population pyramid because fewer children are born in each cohort.\nImpact of falling mortality on population structure:\nFalling mortality raises life expectancy and widens the top of the population pyramid.\nNamed example – Ireland:\nIreland is an example where both fertility and mortality have changed and altered the population structure.\nEffects of changing fertility:\nIreland's total fertility rate fell from about 4 in the early 1970s to about 1.5 in 2023, well below the replacement level of about 2.1.\nFewer births mean smaller young cohorts, so only about one-fifth of Irish people are now under 15 and the pyramid is no longer young and wide-based.\nSmaller youth cohorts reduce the young dependency ratio, which eases pressure on schools and maternity services.\nBy contrast Niger has a fertility rate of about 6 children per woman, a median age of about 15 and a very wide-based pyramid.\nHigh fertility gives a high young dependency ratio, creating demand for schools, child health care and future jobs.\nEffects of changing mortality:\nIrish life expectancy at birth is about 82–83 years and infant mortality is about 3 per 1,000 live births.\nMore people survive into old age, so the top of the pyramid widens and about 776,000 people, about 15% of the population, were aged 65 or over in 2022.\nWomen live about four years longer than men, so women outnumber men in the oldest age groups of the pyramid.\nFalling mortality raises the old-age dependency ratio, which increases pension, health care and nursing home costs.\nCombined effect on the pyramid:\nWhen fertility falls and life expectancy rises together the pyramid becomes top-heavy, as in Japan where about 29% of people are aged 65 or over.\nJapan's fertility rate is about 1.2 and its population peaked at about 128 million around 2008–2010, so it is now falling.\nA shrinking workforce must support more pensioners, so the tax base falls and the pension age comes under pressure to rise.\nIn Niger deaths have fallen faster than births, so the young population is growing by over 3% a year.\nAverage age in Ireland rose from 37.4 in 2016 to 38.8 in 2022, showing the pyramid ageing as fertility stays low and life expectancy rises.",
    "model_source": "sonnet-thirty",
    "answerStatus": "written",
    "type": [
     "long-answer"
    ],
    "diagramRelation": "none",
    "offSpec": false,
