    "label": "15",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Global trading patterns create winners and losers, and Apple's iPhone shows how producer regions in Asia and Africa and consumer regions in the USA and Europe are linked and affected.\n\nAspect 1 – Producer region, assembly in China and India:\nApple designs the iPhone in California but outsources production to contract manufacturers such as Foxconn, a Taiwanese company.\nFoxconn assembles iPhones at very large plants at Zhengzhou, known as iPhone City, and at Shenzhen in China.\nThese plants bring jobs and export earnings, and China has been the world's largest exporter of goods since 2009.\nThis follows the new international division of labour, in which assembly moved to low-wage countries while headquarters, design and research stayed in the core.\nThe core keeps design, finance and marketing, which are the most valuable stages of production.\nWorking conditions in supplier factories have been criticised.\nDependence on China exposes Apple to trade tensions, such as the US tariffs on Chinese goods from 2018.\nA growing share of iPhones is now assembled in India, around Chennai and Bengaluru, by Foxconn and the Tata group, which reduces dependence on China.\nThis shows that the division of labour keeps changing as producer regions compete for assembly work.\n\nAspect 2 – Producer regions, components and raw materials:\nThe iPhone processor chip is designed by Apple and made by TSMC in Taiwan.\nScreens are supplied by Samsung and LG in South Korea, so East Asian high-tech firms gain exports and skilled jobs.\nSouth Korea is an export-led newly industrialised country dominated by chaebol such as Samsung, and supplying Apple supports its industries.\nRaw materials for the iPhone include aluminium, lithium and cobalt for batteries, rare earth metals and gold.\nMost of the world's cobalt is mined in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, a periphery region.\nAfrica holds only a small share of world trade, mostly in raw materials, so the Congo gains little of the value added.\nDependence on a single raw-material export leaves producer regions vulnerable when prices or demand change.\nCOVID-19 disrupted supply chains and caused the chip shortage of 2021, which shows how closely producer regions depend on one another.\nThe supply chain also depends on chokepoints such as the Suez Canal, where the Ever Given blocked shipping for about six days in 2021.\n\nAspect 3 – Consumer regions, the USA and Europe:\niPhones are sold worldwide, and the Americas, Europe and China are the largest regions of sale.\nThe USA is the world's largest economy and largest importer, so US consumers buy huge numbers of imported goods such as iPhones.\nThe USA has had a trade deficit in goods every year since the mid-1970s, because it imports more than it exports.\nConsumers gain a wide choice of high-tech products through Apple Stores, the online store and mobile phone networks.\nHome industries and manufacturing jobs in consumer regions can be lost as production moves to cheaper producer regions.\nThe USA raised tariffs on Chinese goods from 2018 and on imports from most countries in 2025, which can raise prices for consumers.\nEuropean consumers also buy iPhones through Apple Stores and mobile phone networks, so their spending flows to Apple's headquarters in California.\nApple's Cork campus, its European headquarters, employs about 6,000 people, so a consumer region also gains jobs from the company.\nIn 2024 the EU Court of Justice reinstated the ruling that Ireland had given Apple illegal tax benefits, and Ireland received about €14 billion including interest.",
