    "label": "16",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Soil characteristics such as texture and structure, organic matter and pH, and water and air content control the processes that form a soil, and so decide which soil type develops.\n\nAspect 1 – Texture and structure:\nTexture is the proportion of sand, silt and clay in a soil, and it controls how fast water drains through the soil.\nSandy soils are free-draining, so water moves quickly downward and leaching of nutrients dominates their development.\nClay soils hold water and drain slowly, so they become waterlogged and cold, which leads to gleying.\nGleys develop on impermeable boulder clay in the drumlin belt of Cavan, Monaghan and Leitrim, where the hollows between drumlins stay wet.\nWaterlogging excludes air, so iron is reduced to grey or blue-grey with orange mottles where air enters along roots.\nA loam of roughly 40% sand, 40% silt and 20% clay balances drainage with water retention, so it develops into fertile brown earths.\nStructure is the grouping of particles into peds, and crumb structure in humus-rich topsoil opens pores for air and water.\nCompaction by machinery and poaching by livestock destroy peds, so drainage worsens and waterlogging begins.\nClay particles and humus form the clay-humus complex, which holds nutrients that would otherwise be washed out of sandy soils.\n\nAspect 2 – Organic matter and pH:\nBacteria, fungi and earthworms decompose organic matter into humus, which is the main store of nitrogen in the soil.\nHumus binds particles into crumbs, holds water and nutrients, and darkens the topsoil.\nMull humus is mild and crumbly and forms under deciduous woodland and grassland, giving brown earths.\nBrown earth, the main Irish lowland soil of areas such as the Golden Vale, has a dark brown loamy A horizon with crumb structure about 20 to 30 cm deep.\nMor humus is acidic, fibrous and poorly decomposed, and forms under conifer forest and heather.\nAcid mor humus releases organic acids that carry iron and aluminium down the profile in podzolisation, leaving a bleached E horizon.\nPodzols occur on the granite of the Wicklow Mountains, with a pH of about 4 to 4.5 and an iron pan in the B horizon.\nParent rock sets the starting pH, since limestone gives base-rich, less acidic soils and granite gives sandy, acidic soils.\nEarthworms prefer slightly acidic to neutral soils, so in acid soils fewer earthworms mix in the humus and decomposition is slow.\n\nAspect 3 – Water and air content:\nSoil air fills the pores not occupied by water, so water and air share the pore space and one rises as the other falls.\nWhere precipitation exceeds evapotranspiration, as in Ireland, water moves downward and leaches bases such as calcium and potassium from the soil.\nMild leaching gives brown earths, while intense leaching gives podzols.\nWhere evaporation exceeds precipitation, as on the steppes of Ukraine, calcification produces the chernozem, with a thick black A horizon about 1 m deep.\nWell-aerated soils support fast aerobic decomposition, so humification releases nutrients for plants.\nPoorly aerated anaerobic soils decompose slowly, so organic matter builds up as peat.\nPeat covers about a fifth of Ireland, with blanket bog in Mayo and Connemara and raised bog in Offaly.\nIn the hot, wet tropics, laterisation leaves deep red latosols rich in iron and aluminium oxides, with little humus because litter decays so fast.\nSoil air is richer in carbon dioxide than the atmosphere, which forms weak carbonic acid in soil water and aids chemical weathering.",
