    "label": "17",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Human activity can damage soil through overcropping and overgrazing, yet it can also conserve and restore soil, as the Dust Bowl, the west of Ireland and the Sahel show.\nAspect 1 – Overcropping, the Dust Bowl:\nOvercropping means growing crops too intensively on the same land without fallow, rotation or enough organic matter returned.\nNutrients are removed faster than they are replaced, so humus content falls and the crumb structure of the soil breaks down.\nMonoculture takes the same nutrients each year and encourages pests.\nThe dusty, exhausted soil holds less water and is easily eroded by wind and rain, so yields fall.\nIn the 1930s the natural grassland of the Great Plains of the USA, including Oklahoma and Kansas, was ploughed up for wheat.\nSevere drought then left the bare, dry soil exposed, and strong winds blew it away in huge dust storms.\nMany farming families were forced to leave the land.\nThe US Soil Conservation Service was set up in 1935 to tackle the damage.\nShelter belts of trees, contour ploughing and crop rotation were promoted to protect the remaining soil.\nAspect 2 – Overgrazing, western Irish commonage:\nOvergrazing means keeping more animals on land than its vegetation can support, which exceeds its carrying capacity.\nFrom the 1980s EU headage payments paid farmers per animal, so sheep numbers rose sharply on hill commonage in the west of Ireland.\nThe affected areas include Connemara in Co. Galway and the mountains of Mayo, Donegal and Kerry.\nHeather and grass were eaten down and their roots died, leaving blanket peat exposed to rain-splash and wind.\nTrampling compacted the soil, which reduced infiltration and increased runoff.\nSheet, rill and gully erosion removed topsoil and humus.\nSilt washed into rivers harmed salmon and trout spawning grounds.\nPayments were changed from per-head to per-area, and Commonage Framework Plans from the early 2000s set lower stocking rates.\nREPS, introduced in 1994, paid farmers to farm in environmentally friendly ways.\nAspect 3 – Positive impact, soil conservation in the Sahel:\nHuman activity can also conserve and improve soil, as farmers in the Sahel have shown after decades of desertification.\nIn Yatenga, northern Burkina Faso, farmers such as Yacouba Sawadogo revived the traditional zaï technique.\nZaï are small pits dug in hard, crusted soil and filled with manure or compost.\nThe pits catch rainwater and concentrate nutrients around the seed.\nFarmers also built stone lines along the contours to slow runoff and trap soil.\nIn Niger, farmer-managed natural regeneration means farmers protect and prune tree shoots that regrow from old stumps and roots.\nThis has regreened about 5 million hectares since the 1980s, especially in the Maradi and Zinder regions.\nThe trees shade the soil, cut wind speed and add leaf litter, and Faidherbia albida also adds nitrogen.\nThe Great Green Wall, launched by the African Union in 2007, aims to restore 100 million hectares of degraded land by 2030, although progress has been slow.",
