    "label": "20",
    "marks": 80,
    "model": "Many cultural groups lack a nation state of their own because boundaries cut across their homelands or because stronger states control them, as the Kurds, the Tibetans and the Roma show.\nAspect 1 – The Kurds, a divided nation:\nThe Kurds are commonly estimated at 30 million or more, the largest ethnic group without its own state.\nMost live in Kurdistan, a mountainous region divided between Turkey, Iran, Iraq and Syria.\nThe largest number live in south-east Turkey.\nThe Treaty of Sèvres in 1920 provided for a possible Kurdish state.\nThe Treaty of Lausanne in 1923 replaced it and drew the borders of modern Turkey with no Kurdish state.\nTurkey banned Kurdish in public life until 1991 and in broadcasting until the early 2000s.\nThe PKK, founded in 1978, began an armed campaign in 1984, and the conflict cost about 40,000 lives.\nOn 16 March 1988 Saddam Hussein's forces attacked Halabja with chemical weapons, killing about 5,000 people.\nIraq's 2005 constitution recognised the Kurdistan Region as autonomous, with its own government in Erbil.\nAspect 2 – The Tibetans:\nTibet acted as an independent state from 1913 until 1950.\nChina's army entered Tibet in 1950.\nAfter a failed uprising in 1959 the Dalai Lama fled to India.\nTibet's government-in-exile is based in Dharamsala.\nThe Tibet Autonomous Region was set up within China in 1965.\nTibetans see Han Chinese migration as a threat to their culture.\nThey also see controls on monasteries as a threat to their religion.\nMandarin-medium schooling is seen as a threat to the Tibetan language.\nThe exile government and diaspora organisations keep Tibetan identity alive.\nAspect 3 – The Roma, a people without territory:\nThe Roma number about 10 to 12 million in Europe, making them Europe's largest ethnic minority.\nThey have no territory of their own.\nTheir Romani language is related to languages of northern India, showing that their ancestors migrated from India.\nThey reached south-eastern Europe by the 14th century.\nLarge Roma populations live in Romania, Bulgaria, Hungary, Slovakia, Spain and Turkey.\nBecause they are spread across many states, they cannot claim a single homeland.\nRoma were targeted for extermination in the Holocaust.\nIn D.H. and Others v Czech Republic in 2007 the European Court of Human Rights ruled that placing Roma children in special schools was discrimination.\nThey keep their identity through their Romani language rather than through a territory.",
