    "label": "5B",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Named region – India:\nIndia is a sub-continental region in South Asia, with the Himalayas to the north and the Indian Ocean to the south.\nActivity 1 – Factors influencing agriculture:\nTropical temperatures give a long growing season, so two or three crops a year can be grown.\nThe south-west monsoon supplies seasonal rain for kharif crops such as rice, which are sown in June and July.\nFertile alluvial soil covers the Northern Plains and gives high yields of rice and wheat.\nThe black soils of the Deccan, in Maharashtra and Gujarat, are well suited to growing cotton.\nThe flat land of the Northern Plains makes cultivation and transport easier.\nCanals in Punjab and tubewells supply irrigation water, so rabi crops such as wheat can be grown in the dry season.\nThe Green Revolution from the mid-1960s, with high-yielding varieties, fertilisers and irrigation, raised food-grain output from about 51 million tonnes in 1950-51 to about 330 million tonnes in 2022-23.\nActivity 2 – Importance of manufacturing:\nManufacturing began under British rule with cotton textiles in Mumbai and Ahmedabad and jute mills near Kolkata.\nIndia is the world's second-largest crude steel producer, with steel made at Jamshedpur on the coal and iron ore of the Chota Nagpur Plateau.\nEconomic liberalisation in 1991 removed most licences, cut tariffs and opened India to foreign investment, which led to rapid growth in manufacturing.\nA huge, young, low-cost labour force and many engineering graduates from the Indian Institutes of Technology attract manufacturers.\nIndia is a leading maker of generic medicines, known as the pharmacy of the world, and the Serum Institute of India in Pune is the world's largest vaccine maker by number of doses.\nCar manufacturing has grown, with Maruti Suzuki producing at Gurugram and Manesar in Haryana and a car cluster around Chennai.\nFoxconn assembles Apple iPhones at Sriperumbudur near Chennai, which shows how foreign firms use India for electronics assembly.",
