    "label": "7C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Policy – Common Agricultural Policy:\nThe Common Agricultural Policy (CAP) is an EU policy that affects Ireland's economy.\nIts main effect is to raise and stabilise farm incomes in Ireland.\nThe CAP was launched in 1962 to raise productivity, give farmers a fair standard of living and secure food supplies.\nAfter Ireland joined in 1973, guaranteed prices above world levels sharply raised farm prices and incomes.\nA guaranteed market opened for beef and dairy, and farms modernised with machinery, fertiliser, silage and drainage in the golden age of the 1970s.\nMilk quotas introduced in 1984 capped milk output and held back Irish dairying until they were abolished on 1 April 2015.\nAfter quotas ended, Irish milk output rose from about 5.5 billion litres in 2014 to about 8.3 billion litres in 2020.\nThe MacSharry reform of 1992 replaced price cuts with direct payments, and the 2003 Fischler reform decoupled payments from production.\nDirect payments such as the Basic Payment Scheme give stable incomes, though many beef and sheep farms depend heavily on them.\nRural development schemes such as REPS and LEADER fund environmental farming and rural enterprise such as farm tourism and crafts.\nIreland was a net recipient of EU money until about 2014, receiving over €40 billion more than it paid, mostly through the CAP.\nA second effect is negative, as intensification and the larger dairy herd have damaged water quality through nitrates and raised greenhouse gas emissions.",
