    "label": "8C",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Named example – Corrib gas conflict:\nThe Corrib gas project off Co. Mayo caused conflict between the developers and the local community of Rossport.\nThe Corrib gas field lies about 80 km off north-west Co. Mayo and was discovered in 1996.\nEconomic interests supported development for energy security, a domestic gas supply and revenue for the State and the companies.\nConstruction also offered jobs in a poor rural region.\nThe original plan piped high-pressure, unprocessed gas onshore near homes at Rossport, which created a safety risk for residents.\nThe Shell to Sea campaign from 2005 argued that the gas should be processed at sea rather than onshore.\nSruwaddacon Bay, a protected estuary and Special Area of Conservation, lay on the pipeline route, so environmental groups objected.\nThe Rossport Five were jailed in June 2005 for refusing to obey a court order not to obstruct pipeline work, and were released after 94 days.\nProtests were heavily policed for years and the dispute divided the local community.\nThe onshore route was changed to keep the pipeline further from houses, and a tunnel was bored under Sruwaddacon Bay.\nGas first flowed in December 2015 and was processed at the Bellanaboy terminal, supplying over half of Ireland's gas for a time.\nShell sold its share in the project in 2018.",
