    "label": "10B",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Factor 1 – Income levels:\nIreland, a high-income country, had a total fertility rate of about 1.5 in 2023, well below replacement level.\nIn low-income countries such as Niger the birth rate is high, with about 6 children per woman, because children work on farms and care for their parents in old age.\nRising income means that pensions replace children as old-age security, so couples have fewer children and birth rates fall.\nHigher income pays for education, and educated women in paid work have later and fewer children.\nHigher income buys a better diet, housing and clean water, so death rates are low and life expectancy in Ireland is about 82 to 83 years.\nIn low-income countries poverty, poor diet and weak health services keep death rates high, and famine, disease and conflict are the main causes of death.\nFactor 2 – Medical technology:\nVaccines and antibiotics prevent or cure infectious diseases, so death rates fall quickly in the early stages of the demographic transition.\nFalling infant mortality means parents can expect their children to survive, so they no longer have extra children as insurance and the birth rate falls.\nModern contraception, legalised in Ireland by the Health (Family Planning) Act 1979, lets couples limit the size of their families and lowers the birth rate.\nTreatment of heart disease and cancer in high-income countries such as Ireland keeps death rates low and raises life expectancy.\nWhere medical technology is lacking, as in Niger, death rates are higher because vaccination, mosquito nets and malaria treatment are not widely available.\nFalling death rates combined with continued high birth rates cause rapid population growth in Niger, which is growing by over 3 per cent a year.",
