    "model": "Named city – Dublin:\nDublin is a city in which urban sprawl has occurred.\nReasons for sprawl in Dublin:\nRapid population growth increased the demand for housing across the Dublin region.\nHigh house prices in the inner city pushed families out to cheaper greenfield sites at the edge of the city.\nMany people wanted houses with gardens, which led to large suburbs such as Tallaght, Blanchardstown and Swords.\nRising car ownership and the M50 orbital motorway, built in stages from 1990, made distant sites accessible.\nPlanners zoned greenfield land for housing, and developers preferred cheaper greenfield sites to costly inner-city sites.\nTax incentives during the Celtic Tiger boom of the late 1990s and 2000s encouraged a large amount of building.\nAs a result Dublin's sprawl has extended into the commuter counties beyond the city boundary, and a Government survey in 2010 found almost 3,000 unfinished housing estates after the 2008 property crash.\nSolution – Compact growth and brownfield development:\nThe National Planning Framework promotes compact growth, which means building on brownfield land inside the existing city instead of on farmland at the edge.\nHigher densities of housing are built near public transport, so that fewer people need cars to travel to work.\nVacant and derelict inner-city sites are regenerated for housing, which brings people back into the city centre.\nThis reduces the loss of farmland, shortens commutes and makes services such as roads, water and schools cheaper to provide.",
