    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Two reasons for rural to urban migration:\nPeople leave rural areas because of the lack of jobs and the low incomes from small farms.\nPeople are drawn to cities by jobs in industry and services that pay higher wages.\nEffects on urban areas – Dublin:\nDublin gains a large labour pool, which attracts multinationals and service firms.\nDemand from migrants for homes is greater than the supply, which causes housing shortages, high rents and prices, and homelessness.\nUrban sprawl spreads into Meath, Kildare and Wicklow, and the Greater Dublin Area now holds about 2.1 million people, about 40 per cent of the State.\nMany workers live in commuter towns such as Navan, Drogheda and Naas, which causes long commutes and traffic congestion.\nThe growing population increases demand for water and other services, which puts them under pressure.\nLarge populations create economies of scale, so transport, universities and hospitals can be provided.\nDublin gains a rich cultural life because of its large and varied population.\nEffects on rural areas – The West and North-West:\nYoung adults leave, so the population ages and dependency ratios rise.\nWith fewer young adults, birth rates fall.\nSchools, post offices, Garda stations, shops and pubs close because too few customers remain.\nLeitrim had about 155,000 people in 1841 and only about 35,000 in the 2022 census, making it the least populous county.\nGAA clubs and other community groups struggle and teams amalgamate because there are too few young people.\nFarms have no successor, so land is left unused or consolidated.\nThe Gaeltacht declines because young Irish speakers move away, causing cultural loss.",
