    "label": "12B",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Developing world city – Mumbai:\nMumbai in India is a rapidly growing city in the developing world, with about 18.4 million people in its urban area in 2011.\nReasons for rapid growth:\nMumbai has a deep, sheltered natural harbour, which made it a major port that attracts trade and jobs.\nCotton mills were built in Mumbai from the 19th century, and industry still provides jobs.\nMumbai is India's financial capital, with the headquarters of the Reserve Bank of India and the Bombay Stock Exchange, founded in 1875.\nThe Bollywood film industry is based in Mumbai and offers jobs and glamour that attract migrants.\nPush factors such as small holdings, debt, landlessness and drought in poor rural states like Bihar and Uttar Pradesh drive migrants to the city.\nNatural increase adds to the growth because many migrants are young adults who start families in the city.\nProblems caused by rapid growth:\nDharavi in central Mumbai is one of Asia's largest slums, where many people live in overcrowded housing without secure tenure.\nLand and housing costs are very high on the narrow peninsula, which limits space and pushes poor people into slums.\nThe suburban trains are overcrowded and the roads are congested.\nWater supply and sanitation are inadequate, so disease spreads easily.\nThe flood of 26 July 2005, when about 944 mm of rain fell at Santacruz in one day, paralysed the city.\nSolution – Navi Mumbai:\nNavi Mumbai is a planned twin city across the harbour that was begun in the 1970s.\nIt diverts growth away from the overcrowded core of Mumbai.\nIt provides new housing and jobs for people who would otherwise settle in Mumbai's slums.\nBy easing pressure on housing, water and transport in the old city, it improves living conditions.",
